window.READINGS=[{"id":"e01","lvl":"e","topic":"travel","title":"Why Coming Home Can Feel So Strange","body":"Most travellers expect to feel confused when they arrive in a foreign country. Far fewer **anticipate** the strange sense of loss that may appear when they return home. Psychologists call this experience reverse culture shock, and it is surprisingly common among people who have spent several months **abroad**.\n\nThe problem is not that home has changed, but that the traveller has. After half a year of buses, markets and new faces, a person becomes **accustomed** to constant movement and daily surprises. Back in a familiar kitchen, ordinary life can suddenly seem slow and grey. Friends may ask about the trip, yet many lose interest after a few minutes, and the returning traveller feels that nobody truly understands what she has **undergone**. Some people even describe feeling like a **stranger** in their own city.\n\nResearchers have found that these feelings are often stronger than the original culture shock, precisely because they are unexpected. Whereas travellers prepare for difficulties abroad, they rarely consider that returning might require an adjustment of its own. The **tension** between the old self and the new one can create sadness, restlessness and even a desire to leave again immediately.\n\nFortunately, the condition is temporary, and there are ways to **overcome** it. Experts suggest keeping some habits from the road, such as cooking a favourite dish or walking without a **precise** plan. Others recommend finding people with similar experiences, so that stories can be shared with someone who is **genuinely** interested. Rather than trying to forget the journey, a returning traveller can **gradually** bring its lessons into everyday life, turning a painful **phase** into a period of real personal growth.","gloss":[{"w":"anticipate","he":"לצפות מראש"},{"w":"abroad","he":"בחו\"ל"},{"w":"accustomed","he":"רגיל ל-"},{"w":"undergo","he":"לעבור (חוויה)"},{"w":"stranger","he":"זר"},{"w":"tension","he":"מתח"},{"w":"overcome","he":"להתגבר על"},{"w":"precise","he":"מדויק"},{"w":"genuine","he":"באמת, בכנות"},{"w":"gradually","he":"בהדרגה"},{"w":"phase","he":"שלב"}]},{"id":"e02","lvl":"e","topic":"travel","title":"The Secret Life of the Shared Dorm","body":"For many young travellers, a hostel is not simply a cheap bed. It is a place to meet people, and for this reason backpackers often choose a shared dorm even when they could **afford** a private room. Understanding why reveals something important about how humans behave when they are far from home.\n\nThe most obvious attraction is price. A bed in a dorm in Hanoi or Kathmandu may cost only a few dollars, which allows travellers to stay on the road for months rather than weeks. Yet money **by no means** explains everything. Hostels are designed for **mutual** contact: kitchens, rooftops and common rooms encourage guests to cook together, share maps and plan trips. A **novice** traveller who arrives alone can find a **companion** for the next bus journey within hours.\n\nSociologists point out that travel creates unusual conditions for friendship. People on the road are **eager** for connection, they have plenty of free time, and they are away from the **hierarchical** structures of school or work. As a result, conversations that would normally take weeks to develop happen in a single evening. Many backpackers describe hostel friends as surprisingly **sincere**, even though they knew each other only briefly.\n\nOf course, shared dorms are not perfect. Snoring, late arrivals and a lack of privacy can **exhaust** even the most social guest, and some travellers become **weary** of introducing themselves again and again. Nevertheless, the hostel remains a central **institution** of backpacking culture, because it offers something that a hotel rarely can: the chance to feel part of a temporary community wherever one goes.","gloss":[{"w":"afford","he":"להרשות לעצמו (כלכלית)"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"mutual","he":"הדדי"},{"w":"novice","he":"מתחיל, טירון"},{"w":"companion","he":"בן לוויה"},{"w":"eager","he":"להוט, משתוקק"},{"w":"hierarchical","he":"היררכי"},{"w":"sincere","he":"כן, אמיתי"},{"w":"exhaust","he":"להתיש"},{"w":"weary","he":"עייף, נלאה"},{"w":"institution","he":"מוסד"}]},{"id":"e03","lvl":"e","topic":"travel","title":"Eating Safely on Bangkok's Streets","body":"Bangkok is often described as the street food capital of the world. Every evening, thousands of stalls appear on its pavements, selling noodles, grilled meat, fresh fruit and sweet desserts for remarkably low prices. For visitors, this **cuisine** is one of the main reasons to visit, yet many are **reluctant** to try it because they fear becoming sick.\n\nSuch fears are understandable but often **exaggerated**. Although stomach problems are common among travellers, they are not necessarily caused by street stalls. In fact, food that is cooked quickly over high heat in front of the customer can be safer than a dish that has waited for hours in a restaurant kitchen. The **crucial** factor is not where the food is sold, but how it is prepared and stored.\n\nExperienced travellers rely on a few simple rules. First, they choose stalls with a long line of local customers, since a high **frequency** of sales means that **ingredients** are fresh rather than old. Second, they prefer dishes that are served hot and avoid raw salads washed in water from an **unreliable** source. Third, they watch how the cook handles money and food, because hygiene **varies** greatly from one stall to another. Ice is generally safe in Bangkok, as it is usually produced in factories, but drinking tap water is still not recommended.\n\nNone of these rules can **guarantee** perfect health, but they **reduce** the risk significantly. More importantly, they allow travellers to **appreciate** a central part of Thai culture. Eating at a plastic table beside a busy road, surrounded by noise and steam, is an experience that few visitors forget.","gloss":[{"w":"cuisine","he":"מטבח (סגנון בישול)"},{"w":"reluctant","he":"מסויג, לא ששׂ"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"},{"w":"frequency","he":"תדירות"},{"w":"ingredient","he":"מרכיב"},{"w":"unreliable","he":"לא אמין"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות, לגוון"},{"w":"guarantee","he":"להבטיח"},{"w":"reduce","he":"להפחית"},{"w":"appreciate","he":"להעריך, ליהנות מ-"}]},{"id":"e04","lvl":"e","topic":"travel","title":"How the Banana Pancake Trail Was Born","body":"Anyone who has backpacked through Southeast Asia has probably eaten a banana pancake. The sweet snack is sold in guesthouses from northern Thailand to southern Laos, and it has given its name to the popular route that connects them: the \"banana pancake trail.\" The story of how this trail came to exist says a great deal about travel and commerce.\n\nIts **origin** goes back to the 1960s and 1970s, when young Western travellers began to **roam** across Asia on very small budgets. Some followed the overland \"hippie trail\" to India and Nepal, and over time others moved east into Thailand. After the wars in the **region** ended and borders opened, Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia were gradually added to the route. Guidebooks played a **significant** role, because they told readers where to sleep and eat, so that thousands of people ended up in the same few towns.\n\nLocal families quickly noticed the opportunity. To attract foreigners, many guesthouse owners began to **imitate** the comfort food that Western guests seemed to want, and the banana pancake became a cheap and **reliable** favourite. In places such as Vang Vieng or Pai, small villages were **transformed** into busy centres full of cafés, bars and travel agencies.\n\nToday the trail is both praised and criticised. **Proponents** argue that it brings **revenue** to **rural** communities and makes travel accessible to young people. **Critics**, however, claim that it creates a bubble in which visitors **encounter** other backpackers more often than local people. Perhaps the challenge for travellers is not to avoid the trail, but to step off it from time to time.","gloss":[{"w":"origin","he":"מקור"},{"w":"roam","he":"לשוטט, לנדוד"},{"w":"region","he":"אזור"},{"w":"significant","he":"משמעותי"},{"w":"imitate","he":"לחקות"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"},{"w":"transform","he":"לשנות מן היסוד"},{"w":"proponent","he":"תומך, מצדד"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסה"},{"w":"rural","he":"כפרי"},{"w":"critic","he":"מבקר"},{"w":"encounter","he":"לפגוש, להיתקל ב-"}]},{"id":"e05","lvl":"e","topic":"fitness","title":"Why Muscles Hurt the Day After","body":"Almost everyone who starts a new training programme knows the feeling. The workout itself seems fine, but the next morning climbing the stairs becomes **torment**, and the pain often grows even worse on the second day. This experience has a name: delayed onset muscle soreness, or DOMS.\n\nFor many years, people believed that soreness was caused by lactic acid **accumulating** in the muscles. This explanation sounded logical, yet research has shown it to be a **fallacy**. Lactic acid is removed from the muscles within an hour or so after exercise, long before the pain **emerges**. Instead, scientists now think that DOMS is the result of tiny injuries in muscle fibres, followed by an **inflammation** process as the body repairs the damage.\n\nInterestingly, not every type of movement produces the same effect. Soreness is most **severe** after so-called eccentric exercise, in which a muscle lengthens while working, such as walking downhill or slowly lowering a heavy weight. It is also more likely when a person tries an unfamiliar activity or suddenly increases the intensity of training.\n\nThe good news is that the body **adapts** quickly. After a single session, the same exercise usually causes much less pain when it is repeated a week later, a **phenomenon** known as the repeated bout effect. Light movement, sleep and good **nutrition** may help a little, but no **remedy** has been **proven** to remove soreness completely. Most importantly, DOMS is not a reliable sign of a good workout. A person can make **substantial** progress without feeling sore at all.","gloss":[{"w":"torment","he":"ייסורים, סבל"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"fallacy","he":"טעות, תפיסה שגויה"},{"w":"emerge","he":"להופיע, לצוץ"},{"w":"inflammation","he":"דלקת"},{"w":"severe","he":"חמור, קשה"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"nutrition","he":"תזונה"},{"w":"remedy","he":"תרופה, פתרון"},{"w":"proven","he":"מוכח"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"}]},{"id":"e06","lvl":"e","topic":"fitness","title":"The Surprising Origin of 10,000 Steps","body":"Many fitness trackers congratulate their users when they reach 10,000 steps in a day. The number seems scientific, as if doctors had calculated it **precisely**. In reality, its origin is **commercial** rather than medical.\n\nThe goal can be traced back to Japan in the 1960s, around the time of the 1964 Tokyo Olympics. A company **launched** a pedometer called manpo-kei, which translates **roughly** as \"10,000-step meter.\" The round figure was easy to remember and sounded impressive, and the Japanese character for 10,000 even looks a little like a person walking. The product was a success, and the number spread across the world long before researchers had examined it.\n\nWhen scientists finally began to **assess** the target, the results were more complex than the slogan suggested. Several large studies have found that the benefits for health and life expectancy increase **significantly** as people move from very few steps to around 7,000 or 8,000 a day. Above that level, the advantages continue but become smaller, especially for older adults. In other words, 10,000 is not a magic **threshold**, but it is **by no means** a bad goal either.\n\nExperts therefore **emphasize** that the exact number matters less than the general direction. For someone who walks only 3,000 steps a day, adding a short walk after lunch can **contribute** more to health than worrying about a figure chosen by a marketing team. The history of the 10,000-step goal is a useful reminder that a popular **notion** is not always based on **empirical** evidence.","gloss":[{"w":"precise","he":"במדויק"},{"w":"commercial","he":"מסחרי"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"roughly","he":"בערך"},{"w":"assess","he":"להעריך, לבחון"},{"w":"significantly","he":"באופן משמעותי"},{"w":"threshold","he":"סף"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי, מבוסס על ניסוי"}]},{"id":"e07","lvl":"e","topic":"fitness","title":"From Prison Camp to Pilates Studio","body":"Today Pilates is associated with bright studios, expensive leggings and elegant machines. Few of the people who **attend** classes know that the method was developed in an internment camp during the First World War.\n\nJoseph Pilates was born in Germany in 1883. As a child he was often ill, and he **devoted** much of his youth to bodybuilding, gymnastics and other disciplines in an attempt to strengthen his body. When the war broke out in 1914, he was living in England, and because he was a German citizen he was **confined** in a camp for so-called enemy aliens, eventually on the Isle of Man. There, according to the story he later told, he taught fellow prisoners his exercises and **attached** springs to hospital beds so that patients who could not stand could still train their muscles. These early inventions became the basis of the equipment now known as the Reformer.\n\nAfter the war, Pilates **emigrated** to the United States and opened a studio in New York with his wife Clara. He called his system \"Contrology,\" and it **emphasized** controlled breathing, precise movement and a strong centre. Dancers were among his most **eager** students, because the method helped them recover from injuries and **enhance** their **stamina** without building bulky muscles.\n\nFor decades, Pilates remained a fairly **marginal** practice. Only after his death in 1967 did his students spread the method widely, and by the 2000s it had become a global industry. Its journey from a wartime camp to fashionable gyms shows how an idea born from **scarcity** and difficulty can eventually **flourish**.","gloss":[{"w":"attend","he":"להשתתף, לנכוח"},{"w":"devote","he":"להקדיש"},{"w":"confine","he":"לכלוא, להגביל"},{"w":"attach","he":"לחבר, להצמיד"},{"w":"emigrate","he":"להגר"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"eager","he":"להוט, נלהב"},{"w":"enhance","he":"לשפר, להגביר"},{"w":"stamina","he":"כוח סבל, סיבולת"},{"w":"marginal","he":"שולי"},{"w":"scarcity","he":"מחסור"},{"w":"flourish","he":"לשגשג"}]},{"id":"e08","lvl":"e","topic":"fitness","title":"Will Lifting Weights Make Women Bulky?","body":"For decades, many women avoided the weight room because of a single fear: that lifting heavy objects would make them look big and masculine. This belief has been remarkably **persistent**, yet it is largely based on a misunderstanding of how muscles grow.\n\nThe main reason is biological. Building large muscles requires high levels of testosterone, a hormone that women generally produce in much smaller **quantities** than men. As a result, a woman who trains with weights two or three times a week will usually become stronger and firmer, but she will not **acquire** the size of a professional bodybuilder. Those athletes reach their **appearance** through years of extreme training, strict diets and, in some cases, drugs. Muscle growth is a slow process, not something that happens **accidentally**.\n\nAnother common myth claims that women should only use light weights with many repetitions in order to \"tone\" their bodies. In fact, \"toning\" is not a **distinct** physical process. A firm look comes from building muscle and, if desired, reducing body fat, and heavier weights are often more **efficient** for this purpose.\n\nThe benefits of strength training **extend** far beyond appearance. Research shows that it **strengthens** bones, which is **vital** for women because they face a higher risk of osteoporosis later in life. It also improves balance, posture and mood, and it can increase confidence in daily life. Fortunately, attitudes are changing, and more women now **regard** the weight room as their space too. Rather than fearing the barbell, many have discovered that strength is a source of both health and **independence**.","gloss":[{"w":"persistent","he":"עקשני, מתמשך"},{"w":"quantity","he":"כמות"},{"w":"acquire","he":"לרכוש, להשיג"},{"w":"appearance","he":"מראה חיצוני"},{"w":"accidental","he":"במקרה, בטעות"},{"w":"distinct","he":"נפרד, מובחן"},{"w":"efficient","he":"יעיל"},{"w":"extend","he":"להשתרע, להגיע מעבר"},{"w":"strengthen","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"regard","he":"לראות כ-, להתייחס"},{"w":"independence","he":"עצמאות"}]},{"id":"e09","lvl":"e","topic":"routine","title":"What a Morning Routine Can Really Do","body":"Social media is full of people who wake up at five, drink lemon water, meditate, run and write in a journal before breakfast. Such routines are often presented as the secret of success. Although a good morning can certainly help, the **claims** made about it are frequently **exaggerated**.\n\nThere are real advantages to starting the day in a **consistent** way. When the first hour follows a familiar pattern, a person does not need to make many decisions, which may **preserve** mental energy for more important tasks later. A routine can also reduce stress, because it creates a feeling of control before the demands of work or study begin. Exercise in the morning, exposure to daylight and a proper breakfast all have benefits that are well documented.\n\nHowever, the routine itself does not create success. Many famous morning routines belong to people who were already **wealthy** and had **ample** free time, so it is difficult to **infer** that waking early caused their achievements. Moreover, people differ in their natural body clocks. Some are \"larks,\" who feel alert at **dawn**, whereas others are \"night owls,\" whose energy peaks much later. Forcing a night owl to rise at five may only **deprive** them of sleep, which is far more **crucial** for health than any special habit.\n\nThe most useful approach, therefore, is to design a routine that fits a person's actual life rather than copying someone else's. A morning that includes enough sleep, a little movement and a calm start may be **modest**, but it is realistic. In the end, what matters is not how early the day begins, but whether the habit can be **sustained** for months.","gloss":[{"w":"claim","he":"טענה"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"},{"w":"wealthy","he":"עשיר"},{"w":"ample","he":"רב, מספיק"},{"w":"infer","he":"להסיק"},{"w":"dawn","he":"שחר"},{"w":"deprive","he":"לשלול, למנוע"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"},{"w":"modest","he":"צנוע"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים לאורך זמן"}]},{"id":"e10","lvl":"e","topic":"routine","title":"Why Small Habits Beat Big Plans","body":"Every January, millions of people **commit** to ambitious goals: running every morning, reading a book a week, or never eating sugar again. By February, most of these plans have been **abandoned**. According to many behavioural scientists, the problem is not a lack of willpower but the size of the goal.\n\nThe idea behind \"tiny habits\" is simple. Instead of starting with a demanding task, a person begins with a version so small that it is almost impossible to fail. Rather than promising to run for forty minutes, she might commit to putting on her running shoes. Rather than reading a chapter, she reads a single page. One popular rule, sometimes called the two-minute rule, suggests that any new habit should take no longer than two minutes at the beginning.\n\nThis may sound **ludicrous**, yet it addresses a real psychological **barrier**. Starting is often the hardest part of any activity, because the brain tends to **anticipate** effort and discomfort. When the first step is tiny, the feeling of **resistance** almost disappears. Moreover, each small success builds a sense of **identity**: a person who puts on her shoes every day gradually begins to see herself as someone who exercises. Once the behaviour becomes automatic, it can be **expanded** step by step.\n\nResearchers have also found that habits are easier to **establish** when they are attached to an existing routine, such as doing ten squats after brushing one's teeth. This **technique** links the new action to a **constant** cue. Big changes, in other words, rarely **commence** with big efforts. They usually grow from small actions repeated with patience and **consistency**.","gloss":[{"w":"commit","he":"להתחייב"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש, לזנוח"},{"w":"ludicrous","he":"מגוחך"},{"w":"barrier","he":"מחסום"},{"w":"anticipate","he":"לצפות מראש"},{"w":"resistance","he":"התנגדות"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"expand","he":"להרחיב"},{"w":"establish","he":"לבסס, לייסד"},{"w":"technique","he":"טכניקה, שיטה"},{"w":"constant","he":"קבוע"},{"w":"commence","he":"להתחיל"},{"w":"consistency","he":"עקביות"}]},{"id":"e11","lvl":"e","topic":"routine","title":"Procrastination Is About Feelings, Not Time","body":"Most people who **postpone** important tasks describe themselves as lazy or badly organised. Yet a growing body of research suggests that procrastination has little to do with laziness. Psychologists increasingly **regard** it as a problem of emotion **regulation** rather than time management.\n\nThe logic is as follows. Some tasks, such as writing an essay, studying for an exam or answering a difficult email, produce unpleasant feelings: boredom, anxiety, self-doubt or fear of failure. When people delay such a task, they experience immediate relief, because the bad mood disappears for a while. The brain learns that avoidance feels good, and the cycle is **reinforced**. The cost, of course, comes later, when the deadline approaches and stress becomes much more **severe**.\n\nThis explains why procrastinators are often not resting while they avoid work. They may clean the kitchen, organise their desk or scroll through their phones for hours, all of which require effort. The aim is not to be **idle** but to escape a specific feeling. It also explains why **guilt** tends to make the situation worse: feeling bad about procrastinating simply adds another negative emotion to the task.\n\nBased on this **insight**, researchers suggest **strategies** that focus on mood. One study found that students who forgave themselves for procrastinating before a first exam delayed less before the next one. Other **techniques** include breaking a task into smaller pieces, so that it seems less **formidable**, and focusing on the next small action rather than the entire project. Procrastination may never be **eliminated** completely, but understanding its emotional roots makes it far easier to **resist**.","gloss":[{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"regard","he":"לראות כ-, להתייחס"},{"w":"regulation","he":"ויסות"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"severe","he":"חמור"},{"w":"idle","he":"בטל, לא עסוק"},{"w":"guilt","he":"אשמה"},{"w":"insight","he":"תובנה"},{"w":"strategy","he":"אסטרטגיה"},{"w":"technique","he":"טכניקה, שיטה"},{"w":"formidable","he":"מאיים, קשה במיוחד"},{"w":"eliminate","he":"לחסל, להעלים"},{"w":"resist","he":"להתנגד, לעמוד בפני"}]},{"id":"e12","lvl":"e","topic":"routine","title":"Two Days Without a Smartphone","body":"The average smartphone user checks the device dozens of times a day, often without being fully **conscious** of doing so. In response, a growing number of people try a \"digital detox\": a weekend in which they turn off their phones and stay away from screens. What actually happens during those two days?\n\nFor many participants, the first hours are surprisingly uncomfortable. People report reaching for a phone that is not there, and some feel **anxious** that they are missing important messages. This reaction resembles a mild form of withdrawal and shows how deeply the habit is **embedded** in daily life. Although experts debate whether heavy phone use should be called an **addiction**, the strength of the urge is difficult to deny.\n\nBy the second day, however, the experience often changes. Without constant notifications, participants **notice** that their attention lasts longer and that conversations with friends feel deeper. Many **devote** time to activities they had **neglected**, such as reading, cooking or walking without headphones. Some also sleep better, since they no longer look at bright screens late at night.\n\nYet the evidence on detoxes is mixed. Several studies have found only small or short-term improvements in mood, and the benefits tend to **fade** quickly once people return to their old habits. **Critics** argue that a single weekend cannot change behaviour that **occurs** hundreds of times every week. For this reason, many researchers recommend **moderate**, lasting changes instead, such as keeping the phone out of the bedroom. A weekend detox, then, may be most valuable not as a cure, but as a way to **perceive** one's habits more clearly.","gloss":[{"w":"conscious","he":"מודע"},{"w":"anxious","he":"חרד, מודאג"},{"w":"embed","he":"להטמיע, לשבץ"},{"w":"addiction","he":"התמכרות"},{"w":"notice","he":"להבחין"},{"w":"devote","he":"להקדיש"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"fade","he":"לדעוך"},{"w":"critic","he":"מבקר"},{"w":"occur","he":"להתרחש"},{"w":"moderate","he":"מתון"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, להבין"}]},{"id":"e13","lvl":"e","topic":"social","title":"How Many Friends Can One Person Have?","body":"In an age of social media, it is possible to have thousands of online \"friends.\" But how many real relationships can a human being actually **maintain**? In the 1990s, the British anthropologist Robin Dunbar proposed an answer that has since become famous: roughly 150.\n\nDunbar **derived** this number from a study of primates. He noticed that species with a larger neocortex, the part of the brain involved in complex thinking, tended to live in larger groups. Based on the **ratio** between brain size and group size in monkeys and apes, he **estimated** that humans should be able to keep about 150 stable relationships. He then found that many human groups, from traditional villages to military units, were **approximately** this size.\n\nAccording to Dunbar, these relationships are arranged in layers. At the centre are about five very close people, followed by roughly fifteen good friends, fifty friends, and finally the wider circle of 150 people we know personally. Each **layer** requires a different amount of time and emotional investment, and because time is limited, a new close friendship usually means that another one becomes **distant**.\n\nThe theory, however, is **controversial**. Some researchers have reanalysed the data and argue that the evidence is too weak to support a single number, since group sizes **vary** enormously between individuals and cultures. Others point out that some people **thrive** with large networks while others prefer a few close **companions**. Even so, Dunbar's idea remains **influential**, perhaps because it captures a familiar experience: friendship demands time, and nobody can be close to everyone.","gloss":[{"w":"maintain","he":"לשמר, לתחזק"},{"w":"derive","he":"להפיק, לגזור"},{"w":"ratio","he":"יחס"},{"w":"estimate","he":"להעריך"},{"w":"approximate","he":"בערך, בקירוב"},{"w":"layer","he":"שכבה"},{"w":"distant","he":"רחוק, מרוחק"},{"w":"controversial","he":"שנוי במחלוקת"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות"},{"w":"thrive","he":"לשגשג"},{"w":"companion","he":"בן לוויה, חבר"},{"w":"influential","he":"בעל השפעה"}]},{"id":"e14","lvl":"e","topic":"social","title":"Keeping Friendships Alive Across Distance","body":"Friendships rarely end because of a dramatic argument. Far more often, they **fade** slowly after one person moves to another city or country. For young adults who travel, study **abroad** or change jobs frequently, the question of how to keep friends across distance has become increasingly important.\n\nResearch suggests that friendships depend heavily on regular contact. Unlike family ties, which tend to survive long periods of silence, friendships **deteriorate** when people stop investing time in them. Some studies indicate that emotional closeness can **decline** noticeably within months if friends neither meet nor talk. Distance, in other words, does not destroy a friendship directly, but it removes the everyday occasions that once kept it alive.\n\nTechnology offers **partial** solutions. Video calls, voice messages and shared photos allow friends to remain part of each other's daily lives, even across time zones. Interestingly, researchers have found that the type of contact matters. Voice and video seem to create a stronger sense of connection than text messages, perhaps because tone and facial expressions **convey** emotion more clearly. Laughing together, even through a screen, appears to be especially effective at keeping a bond strong.\n\nNevertheless, the most important factor may be **intention**. Friends who live far apart often benefit from creating small **rituals**, such as a weekly call or a shared playlist, so that contact does not **rely** on chance. They also need to accept that the friendship will **alter** its form: conversations may become less frequent but more **profound**. A friendship that **adapts** to new circumstances can **endure** for decades, while one that is simply left alone will gradually disappear.","gloss":[{"w":"fade","he":"לדעוך"},{"w":"abroad","he":"בחו\"ל"},{"w":"deteriorate","he":"להידרדר"},{"w":"decline","he":"לרדת, לפחות"},{"w":"partial","he":"חלקי"},{"w":"convey","he":"להעביר (מסר)"},{"w":"intention","he":"כוונה"},{"w":"ritual","he":"טקס, מנהג קבוע"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך"},{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"endure","he":"להחזיק מעמד, לשרוד"}]},{"id":"e15","lvl":"e","topic":"social","title":"The Unexpected Value of Talking to Strangers","body":"On a crowded train, most passengers stare at their phones and avoid eye contact. Talking to a **stranger** seems awkward, and many people assume that the other person would prefer to be left alone. Yet a **series** of psychological studies suggests that this assumption is largely mistaken.\n\nIn one well-known experiment, researchers in Chicago asked commuters either to start a conversation with the person next to them or to sit in silence. Before the experiment, most participants predicted that talking would be unpleasant. In fact, those who spoke with a stranger reported a more positive journey than those who kept to themselves. Moreover, almost none of the participants who tried to start a conversation reported being **rejected**. The researchers concluded that people **underestimate** how interested others are in connecting.\n\nOther studies have found similar effects in everyday situations. Exchanging a few friendly words with a barista, for example, can make people feel more cheerful and more connected to their community. Such brief **encounters** are sometimes called \"weak ties,\" and sociologists argue that they **contribute** to well-being in ways that close friendships cannot. They create a sense of belonging, and they may even lead to new opportunities, since acquaintances often share information that close friends do not have.\n\nWhy, then, do people avoid small talk? Part of the answer is fear of **awkwardness**, and part is a **bias** that makes us believe others are less friendly than they really are. Overcoming this **inhibition** requires a small act of courage. However, the evidence **implies** that the reward is usually worth the effort, both for the speaker and for the **recipient** of an unexpected **courteous** word.","gloss":[{"w":"stranger","he":"זר"},{"w":"series","he":"סדרה"},{"w":"reject","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"underestimate","he":"להמעיט בהערכה"},{"w":"encounter","he":"מפגש"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"awkwardness","he":"מבוכה"},{"w":"bias","he":"הטיה"},{"w":"inhibition","he":"עכבה, מעצור"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז"},{"w":"recipient","he":"מקבל, נמען"},{"w":"courteous","he":"אדיב"}]},{"id":"e16","lvl":"e","topic":"social","title":"Alone in a Crowd","body":"It may seem **paradoxical** that a person can feel lonely while surrounded by millions of others. Yet surveys in large cities around the world repeatedly show that loneliness is common among urban **residents**, particularly young adults who have recently moved there for work or study.\n\nOne reason is that density does not automatically create connection. In a small village, people meet the same neighbours at the shop, the bus stop and the local café, and these repeated **encounters** slowly turn into relationships. In a big city, by contrast, a person may see thousands of faces every day but rarely the same one twice. Long working hours, expensive housing and frequent moves make it even harder to build stable ties. Some sociologists also argue that city dwellers protect themselves from constant stimulation by becoming **indifferent** to the people around them.\n\nLoneliness is not merely an unpleasant feeling. Researchers have linked chronic loneliness to poor sleep, higher stress and a greater risk of several **ailments**, including heart disease. It can also become a trap: lonely people may grow more suspicious of others, which leads them to **withdraw** further and **reinforces** their isolation.\n\nFortunately, cities also offer **remedies**. Clubs, sports teams, language exchanges and community gardens provide what experts call \"repeated **proximity**,\" the regular contact that allows friendships to develop naturally. Some cities have even **launched** official programmes to address loneliness, and the United Kingdom appointed a **minister** for loneliness in 2018. The crowd itself does not **guarantee** connection, but with a little effort it can become a **source** of community rather than isolation.","gloss":[{"w":"paradoxical","he":"פרדוקסלי, סותר את עצמו"},{"w":"resident","he":"תושב"},{"w":"encounter","he":"מפגש"},{"w":"indifferent","he":"אדיש"},{"w":"ailment","he":"מחלה, מיחוש"},{"w":"withdraw","he":"להסתגר, לסגת"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"remedy","he":"תרופה, פתרון"},{"w":"proximity","he":"קרבה"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"minister","he":"שר"},{"w":"guarantee","he":"להבטיח"},{"w":"source","he":"מקור"}]},{"id":"e17","lvl":"e","topic":"love","title":"How Couples Meet in the Age of Apps","body":"For most of human history, people found partners through family, neighbours, school, work or religious communities. Within just two decades, this pattern has been **transformed**. In the United States, studies indicate that meeting online has become the most common way for couples to meet, **surpassing** introductions through friends.\n\nThe rise of dating apps is easy to explain. They offer a huge **variety** of potential partners, they are available at any hour, and they allow people to **approach** others without the risk of rejection in public. For individuals who belong to a **minority**, who have recently moved, or who are simply shy, this access can be especially valuable. Some researchers also suggest that apps lead to more relationships between people from different social and **ethnic** backgrounds, since users are no longer limited to their own circles.\n\nHowever, the experience is not always positive. Many users describe \"swipe fatigue,\" a feeling of **exhaustion** caused by endless profiles and short conversations that lead nowhere. Because there always seems to be another option, some people find it hard to **commit**, while others feel that they are being judged only by their **appearance**. Psychologists have also observed that too many choices can make decision-making more difficult rather than easier.\n\nDespite these problems, there is little evidence that couples who meet online are less happy than those who meet in other ways. Some studies even suggest that their relationships are equally stable. The tool, in other words, is neither good nor bad in itself. What **determines** success is how people use it: with patience, **sincere** communication and realistic expectations.","gloss":[{"w":"transform","he":"לשנות מן היסוד"},{"w":"surpass","he":"לעלות על"},{"w":"variety","he":"מגוון"},{"w":"approach","he":"לפנות אל, לגשת"},{"w":"minority","he":"מיעוט"},{"w":"ethnic","he":"אתני"},{"w":"exhaustion","he":"תשישות"},{"w":"commit","he":"להתחייב"},{"w":"appearance","he":"מראה חיצוני"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"},{"w":"sincere","he":"כן, אמיתי"}]},{"id":"e18","lvl":"e","topic":"love","title":"Can Love Survive the Distance?","body":"When one partner moves abroad for work or study, friends often predict that the relationship will not last. Long-distance relationships have a poor **reputation**, and many people assume that physical separation must **inevitably** lead to a breakup. Research, however, paints a more complex picture.\n\nSeveral studies have compared long-distance couples with couples who live in the same city, and the differences are smaller than expected. On average, partners who live apart report similar levels of satisfaction and commitment. Some research has even found that they feel more **intimate** in certain ways. One explanation is that people in long-distance relationships tend to **disclose** more about their thoughts and feelings during calls, since conversation is their main form of contact. Another is that they may **idealise** each other, focusing on positive qualities because they do not see the small irritations of daily life.\n\nThis idealisation, however, can become a problem when the couple finally moves together. Some studies suggest that the most **fragile** period is not the time apart, but the reunion, when the romantic image must face reality. Questions about who does the dishes or how to spend weekends suddenly **emerge**, and partners may discover differences they had **overlooked**.\n\nExperts therefore point to several factors that **contribute** to success. Couples who share a clear plan for ending the distance, who communicate regularly and honestly, and who trust each other appear to do best. Uncertainty, on the other hand, is a significant source of **tension**. Distance is a challenge, but it is **by no means** a sentence of failure for a **mutual**, committed relationship.","gloss":[{"w":"reputation","he":"מוניטין, שם"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בהכרח, באופן בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"intimate","he":"אינטימי, קרוב"},{"w":"disclose","he":"לחשוף, לגלות"},{"w":"idealise","he":"להאדיר, לראות באור אידיאלי"},{"w":"fragile","he":"שביר"},{"w":"emerge","he":"לצוץ, להופיע"},{"w":"overlook","he":"להתעלם, לא לשים לב"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"tension","he":"מתח"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"mutual","he":"הדדי"}]},{"id":"e19","lvl":"e","topic":"love","title":"Do We Really Speak Different Love Languages?","body":"In 1992, the American pastor and counsellor Gary Chapman published The Five Love Languages, a book that has since sold millions of copies. Its central **notion** is simple: people express and receive love in different ways, and conflicts arise when partners speak different \"languages.\"\n\nChapman described five such languages: words of affirmation, quality time, receiving gifts, acts of service and physical touch. According to the theory, each person has a **primary** language. Someone who values acts of service, for example, may feel loved when a partner cooks dinner, while someone whose language is words of affirmation needs to hear **praise** and appreciation. If partners fail to recognise these differences, both may feel **neglected** even though both are trying hard to show **affection**.\n\nThe idea is **appealing** because it is easy to understand and offers practical advice. Yet when psychologists began to test it, the evidence proved weaker than its popularity suggests. Several studies have found little **empirical** support for the claim that each person has one dominant love language. Most people appreciate all five forms of affection to some **extent**. Furthermore, couples whose love languages \"match\" do not appear to be significantly happier than couples whose languages differ.\n\nSome researchers therefore propose a different **metaphor**: rather than five separate languages, love may be more like a balanced diet, in which every **component** is necessary. Even so, the book has made a **valid** point. It encourages partners to pay attention to each other's needs and to communicate them openly, and that alone may **enrich** many relationships.","gloss":[{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"primary","he":"עיקרי, ראשי"},{"w":"praise","he":"שבח"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"affection","he":"חיבה"},{"w":"appealing","he":"מושך"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי, מבוסס על ניסוי"},{"w":"extent","he":"מידה, היקף"},{"w":"metaphor","he":"מטאפורה, דימוי"},{"w":"component","he":"רכיב"},{"w":"valid","he":"תקף"},{"w":"enrich","he":"להעשיר"}]},{"id":"e20","lvl":"e","topic":"love","title":"Why Love on the Road Feels So Intense","body":"Many travellers return from a long journey with a story about a romance: a few days or weeks with someone they met in a hostel, on a beach or on a night bus. Such relationships often feel more **intense** than anything at home, even though they are short. Psychologists suggest several reasons for this **phenomenon**.\n\nFirst, travel changes the way people behave. Far from their usual responsibilities and social roles, travellers feel freer to be open, spontaneous and adventurous. They are also constantly experiencing new places, and research suggests that **novelty** and excitement can be mistaken for attraction. In a famous experiment from the 1970s, men who met a woman on a frightening **suspension** bridge were more likely to call her afterwards than men who met her on a stable one. The **physical** arousal caused by the bridge was, **apparently**, partly **attributed** to her.\n\nSecond, a holiday romance has a clear end date. Paradoxically, this limitation can make the relationship feel more precious rather than less. Knowing that time is **brief**, partners skip the slow stages of getting to know each other and share personal stories almost immediately. The fact that the relationship exists outside ordinary life also means that it is never tested by bills, work or family **obligations**.\n\nThis is also why many holiday romances do not survive the return home. Once the **exotic** setting disappears, partners sometimes discover that they have little in common. Yet this does not make the experience meaningless. For many young travellers, a brief romance becomes a valuable memory and a lesson about their own feelings, a story that they **recall** with **affection** long after the trip has ended.","gloss":[{"w":"intense","he":"עז, אינטנסיבי"},{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"novelty","he":"חידוש, חדשנות"},{"w":"suspension","he":"תלוי (גשר תלוי)"},{"w":"physical","he":"גופני, פיזי"},{"w":"apparently","he":"כנראה, ככל הנראה"},{"w":"attribute","he":"לייחס"},{"w":"brief","he":"קצר"},{"w":"obligation","he":"מחויבות, חובה"},{"w":"exotic","he":"אקזוטי"},{"w":"recall","he":"להיזכר"},{"w":"affection","he":"חיבה"}]},{"id":"e21","lvl":"e","topic":"beauty","title":"Five Thousand Years of Painted Nails","body":"Painting the nails may look like a modern habit, but its **origin** goes back at least five thousand years. In ancient China, people made an early form of polish by mixing egg whites, beeswax, gelatin and flower petals. According to some historical accounts, colour was not simply a matter of taste. During certain **dynasty** periods, gold and silver were reserved for **royal** families, whereas ordinary people who wore those shades could face a serious **penalty**.\n\nAncient Egypt had a similar custom. Wealthy Egyptians coloured their nails with henna, a plant dye that produced shades from orange to deep red. Here too, colour was a sign of rank: the darkest tones belonged to the highest classes, so that a single glance at someone's hands could reveal their place in society. Nail colour, in other words, was **by no means** a private choice but a public statement of **identity**.\n\nThe modern product **emerged** much later, and from an unexpected industry. In the early twentieth century, chemists developed glossy paints for cars, and cosmetic companies soon **adapted** the same technology for nails. One brand **launched** a coloured polish in the 1930s and quickly **attained** enormous success, partly because it matched lipstick shades and was heavily **advertised** in women's magazines.\n\nToday, nail polish is a global **commodity** that generates billions of dollars in **revenue** every year. Although the ingredients have changed dramatically, the basic idea has remained surprisingly **constant**: people still use their nails to express who they are, which group they belong to, and how they wish to be seen.","gloss":[{"w":"origin","he":"מקור"},{"w":"dynasty","he":"שושלת"},{"w":"royal","he":"מלכותי"},{"w":"penalty","he":"עונש, קנס"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"emerge","he":"להופיע, לצוץ"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להתאים"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"attain","he":"להשיג"},{"w":"advertise","he":"לפרסם (מודעות)"},{"w":"commodity","he":"מוצר, סחורה"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסות"},{"w":"constant","he":"קבוע"}]},{"id":"e22","lvl":"e","topic":"beauty","title":"The Hidden Chemistry Behind Gel Nails","body":"To many clients, a gel manicure seems almost magical: the colour goes on as a soft liquid, and after a minute under a small lamp it becomes hard, shiny and **durable** enough to last for weeks. Yet the process is not magic but chemistry, and understanding it can help nail technicians work more safely and **efficiently**.\n\nGel polish contains small molecules called monomers and oligomers, along with a special **ingredient** known as a photoinitiator. When this **substance** is exposed to ultraviolet light, it absorbs energy and produces highly reactive particles. These particles cause the small molecules to link together into long, connected chains, a reaction called polymerization. Rather than drying through evaporation, as regular polish does, gel **undergoes** a real chemical change, which is why it hardens so quickly and resists chipping.\n\nThis difference explains several **phenomena** that technicians **encounter** every day. If a layer is too thick, light cannot **penetrate** it completely, so the bottom remains soft while the surface looks finished. Similarly, a weak or old lamp may leave the gel only partly cured, which can cause lifting and, in some cases, skin irritation. The sticky film that often remains on top is also chemical: oxygen in the air **inhibits** the reaction at the very surface.\n\nExperts therefore recommend several **precautions**: applying thin layers, using a lamp that is **compatible** with the product, and keeping uncured gel off the skin, since repeated contact may lead to allergies. Some researchers have also raised questions about UV exposure to the hands, which is why some clients wear fingerless gloves or sunscreen. Knowing the science, in short, is a **crucial** part of the craft.","gloss":[{"w":"durable","he":"עמיד"},{"w":"efficiently","he":"ביעילות"},{"w":"ingredient","he":"רכיב"},{"w":"substance","he":"חומר"},{"w":"undergo","he":"לעבור (תהליך)"},{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"encounter","he":"להיתקל ב-"},{"w":"penetrate","he":"לחדור"},{"w":"inhibit","he":"לעכב, לבלום"},{"w":"precaution","he":"אמצעי זהירות"},{"w":"compatible","he":"תואם"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"}]},{"id":"e23","lvl":"e","topic":"beauty","title":"Reading the Numbers on a Sunscreen Bottle","body":"Most people **purchase** sunscreen by looking at a single number, but that number tells only part of the story. Sunlight **comprises** several types of ultraviolet radiation, and the two that matter most for skin are UVA and UVB. Although both are invisible, they affect the body in **distinct** ways, and a good product must protect against both.\n\nUVB rays are mainly responsible for sunburn. They damage the outer **layer** of the skin, and their strength **varies** greatly with the season, the time of day and the altitude. UVA rays, by contrast, are present at a more **constant** level throughout the year and **penetrate** deeper into the skin. They contribute to wrinkles, dark spots and **premature** ageing, and both types increase the risk of skin cancer.\n\nThe SPF, or sun protection factor, measures protection against UVB only. A product with SPF 30 blocks **roughly** 97 percent of UVB rays, whereas SPF 50 blocks about 98 percent, so the difference is smaller than the numbers suggest. To protect against UVA, consumers should look for the words 'broad spectrum' or a separate UVA rating on the label.\n\nEven the best formula is useless if it is applied incorrectly. Laboratory tests use a thick, **generous** amount, while most people apply only a quarter to half of that quantity, which significantly reduces the real protection. Sunscreen also wears off through sweat, water and rubbing, so dermatologists recommend reapplying it every two hours. In other words, careful habits are just as **vital** as the number printed on the bottle, and **relying** on sunscreen alone, without shade or clothing, is rarely **sufficient**.","gloss":[{"w":"purchase","he":"לרכוש"},{"w":"comprise","he":"לכלול, להיות מורכב מ-"},{"w":"distinct","he":"נבדל, שונה"},{"w":"layer","he":"שכבה"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות"},{"w":"constant","he":"קבוע"},{"w":"penetrate","he":"לחדור"},{"w":"premature","he":"מוקדם מדי"},{"w":"roughly","he":"בערך"},{"w":"generous","he":"נדיב, בשפע"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"rely","he":"לסמוך על, להסתמך"},{"w":"sufficient","he":"מספיק"}]},{"id":"e24","lvl":"e","topic":"beauty","title":"Skin Care in Hot and Humid Places","body":"Travellers who arrive in Bangkok or Hanoi in the rainy season often notice that their skin behaves differently within days. The combination of heat, **humidity**, strong sun and pollution creates a new environment, and skin that was calm at home may suddenly become oily, irritated or **prone to** breakouts. Fortunately, a few simple changes can help it **adapt**.\n\nIn humid weather, the body sweats more so that it can cool down, and sweat mixed with oil and dust can block pores. For this reason, dermatologists generally advise lighter products rather than the thick creams that suit cold climates. A gentle cleanser in the evening helps remove the **accumulation** of sunscreen, sweat and city dirt, whereas washing too often or too aggressively may strip the skin and make it produce even more oil.\n\nSun protection is **crucial**, but it is also more difficult to **sustain** in the tropics. Water-resistant sunscreen still needs to be reapplied, especially after swimming or long walks, and a hat often protects better than any cream. Travellers should also remember that some common medications, including certain antibiotics, make skin more sensitive to light.\n\nFinally, the climate can **disrupt** the skin from the inside. Dehydration, little sleep and unfamiliar food may all show on the face, so drinking enough water is not a minor detail but an **essential** part of a routine. Many travellers **resort to** carrying their entire bathroom in a backpack, yet experience suggests that a short list of **reliable** products, used **consistently**, is far more effective than an **elaborate** collection used only occasionally.","gloss":[{"w":"humidity","he":"לחות"},{"w":"prone to","he":"נוטה ל-"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"accumulation","he":"הצטברות"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לשמר, לקיים לאורך זמן"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"essential","he":"חיוני, הכרחי"},{"w":"resort to","he":"לפנות ל-, להיעזר ב-"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"},{"w":"consistently","he":"בעקביות"},{"w":"elaborate","he":"מורכב, משוכלל"}]},{"id":"e25","lvl":"e","topic":"growth","title":"The Growth Mindset: Powerful Idea, Modest Effect","body":"In the early 2000s, the psychologist Carol Dweck made a simple **notion** famous: people who believe that intelligence can grow through effort tend to learn more than those who see it as fixed. She called the first view a 'growth mindset' and the second a 'fixed mindset'. Her book became a bestseller, and schools around the world **implemented** programmes designed to change how students think about their abilities.\n\nThe idea is attractive for obvious reasons. It suggests that failure is not a final **verdict** on a person's talent but information about what to practise next. Students with a growth mindset, according to this view, are less likely to give up when work becomes difficult, because they **interpret** mistakes as part of learning rather than as proof that they are not clever enough.\n\nIn recent years, however, researchers have examined the theory more **critically**. Several large studies found that mindset interventions do have an effect, but a much smaller one than early reports suggested, and mostly for students who were already struggling. Some critics argue that the message was **distorted** in popular culture, where it became a slogan that **implied** effort alone could solve any problem.\n\nDweck herself has **acknowledged** this problem, warning against a 'false growth mindset' in which teachers simply praise effort without teaching better strategies. The **overall** lesson, therefore, is balanced. Believing in growth can help, especially at **crucial** moments, but it is no **substitute** for good teaching, practice and support. A mindset is a starting point, not a **guarantee**.","gloss":[{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, מושג"},{"w":"implement","he":"ליישם"},{"w":"verdict","he":"פסק דין"},{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"critically","he":"באופן ביקורתי"},{"w":"distort","he":"לעוות"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להודות, להכיר ב-"},{"w":"overall","he":"בסך הכול, כללי"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע"},{"w":"substitute","he":"תחליף"},{"w":"guarantee","he":"ערובה"}]},{"id":"e26","lvl":"e","topic":"growth","title":"Why Writing Things Down Can Help","body":"Keeping a journal is an old habit, practised by everyone from ancient rulers to modern athletes. The Roman **emperor** Marcus Aurelius wrote private notes to himself that we now know as the Meditations. Today, psychologists have begun to study what such writing actually does, and the results suggest that it is more than a pleasant hobby.\n\nSome of the best-known research comes from the psychologist James Pennebaker, who asked volunteers to **participate** in a simple experiment: writing about difficult emotional experiences for about fifteen minutes on several consecutive days. Compared with people who wrote about neutral topics, many participants later reported better mood and even made fewer visits to the doctor. Although not every study has repeated these results, the general pattern has **prevailed**: putting painful feelings into words seems to help people **resolve** them.\n\nWhy should writing have this effect? One explanation is that writing forces a person to turn a confused mass of emotions into a **coherent** story. When thoughts are organized in a **sequence**, with causes and consequences, they become easier to understand and less likely to **linger** in the mind at night. Writing also creates distance: on paper, a problem can be examined almost as if it belonged to someone else.\n\nJournaling is not only for hard times. Gratitude journals, in which people list good things that happened, are associated with greater satisfaction, and simple notes about training or sleep can reveal patterns that would otherwise be **overlooked**. The method matters less than **consistency**. A few honest lines written regularly are **substantially** more useful than an **elaborate** diary that is **abandoned** after one week.","gloss":[{"w":"emperor","he":"קיסר"},{"w":"participate","he":"להשתתף"},{"w":"prevail","he":"לגבור, לשרור"},{"w":"resolve","he":"לפתור, ליישב"},{"w":"coherent","he":"קוהרנטי, ברור והגיוני"},{"w":"sequence","he":"רצף, סדר"},{"w":"linger","he":"להתעכב, להישאר"},{"w":"overlook","he":"להחמיץ, להתעלם"},{"w":"consistency","he":"עקביות"},{"w":"substantially","he":"באופן ניכר"},{"w":"elaborate","he":"מפורט, משוכלל"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש"}]},{"id":"e27","lvl":"e","topic":"growth","title":"It Is Never Too Late to Learn","body":"Many adults believe that learning a new skill, such as a language, a musical instrument or a sport, is something best done in childhood. This belief is partly true. Young children have remarkably flexible brains, and some abilities, like speaking a language without an accent, are easier to **acquire** early in life. Yet the idea that adults cannot learn well is a **fallacy** that stops many people from even trying.\n\nIn fact, adults have several advantages over children. They usually have a clearer **motive**, better concentration and more experience in organizing information. An adult who studies English for a university exam, for example, can **deliberately** focus on weak areas, use **strategies** such as spaced repetition, and connect new words to knowledge she already **possesses**. Children, by contrast, mostly learn through long, unplanned exposure.\n\nNeuroscience supports this more optimistic view. The adult brain keeps changing throughout life, a capacity known as neuroplasticity. New connections form whenever a skill is practised, and they **strengthen** with repetition, although the process may be **gradual** and slower than in childhood.\n\nThe biggest obstacles for adult learners are often emotional rather than biological. Adults are **accustomed** to feeling competent, so being a **novice** again can be uncomfortable and even embarrassing. Many quit after a few weeks, not because they lack talent, but because early progress feels slow. Researchers who study expertise **emphasize** that steady, focused practice matters more than age. Accepting mistakes, setting small goals and practising a little every day **enable** adults to **overcome** that difficult early stage.","gloss":[{"w":"acquire","he":"לרכוש (ידע, מיומנות)"},{"w":"fallacy","he":"טעות, כשל לוגי"},{"w":"motive","he":"מניע"},{"w":"deliberately","he":"במכוון"},{"w":"strategy","he":"אסטרטגיה"},{"w":"possess","he":"להחזיק, להיות בעל"},{"w":"strengthen","he":"להתחזק"},{"w":"gradual","he":"הדרגתי"},{"w":"accustomed","he":"רגיל ל-"},{"w":"novice","he":"מתחיל, טירון"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"enable","he":"לאפשר"},{"w":"overcome","he":"להתגבר"}]},{"id":"e28","lvl":"e","topic":"growth","title":"Learning to Say No Without Guilt","body":"For many people, especially those who care deeply about their friends, the word 'no' is surprisingly hard to say. They agree to extra shifts, favours and social plans even when they are exhausted, and then feel **bitter** about it later. Psychologists sometimes describe this pattern as people-pleasing: a strong **inclination** to put other people's needs first, often at a personal cost.\n\nThe roots of this behaviour are understandable. Humans evolved in small groups, where being rejected could be dangerous, so the fear of disappointing others has a long history. Many people also learned in childhood that being helpful earned **praise** and **affection**. As adults, they may feel **guilt** whenever they refuse a request, as though setting a limit were the same as being selfish.\n\nYet constantly saying yes has a price. It can **exhaust** a person's time and energy, damage their health and even harm relationships, because hidden resentment tends to grow. Paradoxically, people who never refuse may also be seen as less **reliable**, since they promise more than they can deliver.\n\nExperts suggest a few practical **techniques**. One is to pause before answering, rather than agreeing automatically; a simple 'let me check and get back to you' creates space to think. Another is to give a short, **sincere** refusal without long excuses, which often sound less convincing. It also helps to remember that a polite no is not a **hostile** act. Healthy friendships are based on **mutual** respect, and a friend who cannot **tolerate** a reasonable refusal may not be much of a friend. Learning to say no, in the end, is a way of protecting the relationships that matter most.","gloss":[{"w":"bitter","he":"מריר"},{"w":"inclination","he":"נטייה"},{"w":"praise","he":"שבח"},{"w":"affection","he":"חיבה"},{"w":"guilt","he":"אשמה"},{"w":"exhaust","he":"לרוקן, למצות"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"},{"w":"technique","he":"טכניקה, שיטה"},{"w":"sincere","he":"כן, אמיתי"},{"w":"hostile","he":"עוין"},{"w":"mutual","he":"הדדי"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"}]},{"id":"e29","lvl":"e","topic":"business","title":"Opening a Nail Studio at Home","body":"For many nail technicians, the first business step is not renting a salon but turning a room at home into a small studio. The **appeal** is obvious: there is no expensive rent, the hours are flexible, and the owner can build a client base slowly. However, a home studio is still a real business, and treating it casually can lead to problems later.\n\nThe first issue is legal. In many countries, a person who sells services must **register** as self-employed and report income to the tax authorities. Some cities also have rules about running a business from a private **residence**, and certain places require a professional **license** for nail work. Checking these **prerequisites** early is far cheaper than paying a fine afterwards.\n\nThe second issue is hygiene. Clients cannot see how tools are cleaned, so they **rely** on the technician's **integrity**. Metal tools should be properly cleaned and sterilized, files and buffers that cannot be disinfected should not be reused, and the work area must have good ventilation, since dust and chemical **vapors** can harm both the technician and the client. A clean, organized space is not only safer but also a powerful message about professionalism.\n\nFinally, the studio should be planned with comfort and **efficiency** in mind. A stable table, good lighting, a comfortable chair and storage that keeps products within reach all make long working days easier. Many beginners are **tempted** to buy every colour and gadget at once, yet it is usually wiser to start with **adequate**, good-quality basics and **expand** gradually as income grows. A home studio, in short, can be a small but serious **foundation** for a larger business.","gloss":[{"w":"appeal","he":"משיכה, קסם"},{"w":"register","he":"להירשם"},{"w":"residence","he":"מקום מגורים"},{"w":"license","he":"רישיון"},{"w":"prerequisite","he":"תנאי מוקדם"},{"w":"rely","he":"לסמוך על"},{"w":"integrity","he":"יושרה"},{"w":"vapor","he":"אדים"},{"w":"efficiency","he":"יעילות"},{"w":"tempt","he":"לפתות"},{"w":"adequate","he":"מספק, הולם"},{"w":"expand","he":"להתרחב"},{"w":"foundation","he":"בסיס, יסוד"}]},{"id":"e30","lvl":"e","topic":"business","title":"Finding First Clients on Instagram","body":"For a beauty business, Instagram works almost like a shop window. Nail art is highly visual, and a single photo can show a potential client exactly what a technician is able to do. It is no surprise, then, that many young **entrepreneurs** **launch** their businesses on the platform before they even have a business card.\n\nThe most important element is the quality of the images. Clients scroll quickly, so photos should be sharp, well lit and **consistent** in style. Natural daylight and a simple background usually work better than heavy filters, which can **distort** colours and make the work look less **genuine**. Short videos that show the process, from the bare nail to the finished design, often attract more attention than still photos because they **demonstrate** skill in real time.\n\nYet beautiful pictures alone rarely bring clients. Experts in social media marketing **emphasize** the importance of local reach. Using location tags and local hashtags, and responding quickly to messages, makes it easier for people nearby to find the account and book an appointment. Collaborations with local hairdressers, make-up artists or cafés can also **expand** an audience, since each partner introduces the other to new followers.\n\nFinally, a new business should **regard** its first clients as its best **advertising**. Asking satisfied customers for permission to post their nails, and encouraging them to tag the studio, creates a kind of digital word of mouth. Offering a modest **incentive**, such as a small discount for a recommendation, can work well too. Although followers are pleasant, the real **objective** is not popularity but a steady flow of loyal, paying clients.","gloss":[{"w":"entrepreneur","he":"יזם"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"distort","he":"לעוות"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, אותנטי"},{"w":"demonstrate","he":"להדגים, להראות"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"expand","he":"להרחיב"},{"w":"regard","he":"לראות כ-, להתייחס ל-"},{"w":"advertising","he":"פרסום"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"objective","he":"מטרה"}]},{"id":"e31","lvl":"e","topic":"business","title":"How Much Should a Beginner Charge?","body":"One of the hardest questions for any new business owner is how much to **charge**. Many beginners set very low prices, hoping that cheap services will attract clients quickly. Although this strategy may seem logical, it often creates problems that are difficult to **reverse** later.\n\nA sensible price begins with real costs. A nail technician, for instance, must **account** for materials such as polish, gel and files, as well as electricity, equipment, rent and taxes. Just as important is the value of her time. If a set of gel nails takes two hours, the price should reflect two hours of skilled **labor**, not only the cost of the products. Many beginners **neglect** this calculation and discover, after several months, that they are working long days for almost no profit.\n\nThe market matters too. Comparing prices in the area shows what clients are **accustomed** to paying, but it should be used as a guide rather than a strict rule. A technician who offers a **distinct** advantage, such as detailed hand-painted art or unusually long-lasting work, can reasonably ask for more than a basic salon nearby.\n\nPrices that are too low can also harm a business's **image**. Clients often use price as a signal of quality, so a very cheap service may suggest a lack of experience. In addition, raising prices later can feel awkward, and some loyal clients may leave. A common **compromise** is to set a fair **initial** price from the beginning and offer a **temporary** introductory discount, which can attract new clients without permanently lowering the value of the work. Prices can then be **revised** each year as skills and demand grow.","gloss":[{"w":"charge","he":"לגבות (מחיר)"},{"w":"reverse","he":"להפוך, לבטל"},{"w":"account for","he":"לקחת בחשבון"},{"w":"labor","he":"עבודה"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"accustomed","he":"רגיל ל-"},{"w":"distinct","he":"מובחן, ברור"},{"w":"image","he":"תדמית"},{"w":"compromise","he":"פשרה"},{"w":"initial","he":"ראשוני, התחלתי"},{"w":"temporary","he":"זמני"},{"w":"revise","he":"לעדכן, לתקן"}]},{"id":"e32","lvl":"e","topic":"business","title":"Why So Many Small Businesses Close Early","body":"Every year, millions of people around the world open small businesses, from cafés and clothing shops to beauty salons. Many of them **flourish**, but a significant number close within a few years. In the United States, for example, government data suggest that roughly one in five new businesses fails within the first year, and about half are gone after five years. Understanding why can help new **entrepreneurs** avoid the most common mistakes.\n\nThe most frequently **cited** reason is money. Many owners start with too little **capital** and underestimate how long it will take before the business becomes profitable. A few slow months can quickly **exhaust** their savings, especially when they must still pay rent, suppliers and loans. Poor cash flow, rather than a bad idea, is therefore what **terminates** many young companies.\n\nAnother problem is weak demand. Some entrepreneurs build a product they personally love without first checking whether enough customers are willing to pay for it. Others enter crowded markets where established **rivals** already offer similar services at lower prices. Without a clear reason for customers to choose them, these businesses struggle to attract attention.\n\nFinally, many failures are related to the owner's own limits. Running a business **requires** skills in marketing, accounting and management, not only talent in the core craft. An excellent baker is **by no means** automatically an excellent manager. Experts therefore recommend careful planning, realistic budgets, and the courage to **seek** advice from experienced mentors. Failure is not **inevitable**, and many successful owners **admit** that their first business taught them more than any course could.","gloss":[{"w":"flourish","he":"לשגשג"},{"w":"entrepreneur","he":"יזם"},{"w":"cite","he":"לצטט, להזכיר"},{"w":"capital","he":"הון"},{"w":"exhaust","he":"לרוקן, לכלות"},{"w":"terminate","he":"לסיים, לחסל"},{"w":"rival","he":"מתחרה, יריב"},{"w":"require","he":"לדרוש"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"seek","he":"לחפש, לבקש"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"admit","he":"להודות"}]},{"id":"e33","lvl":"e","topic":"mind","title":"Why Do We Dream?","body":"Every night, most people spend about two hours dreaming, although they usually remember very little in the morning. Dreams can be strange, frightening or wonderful, and for thousands of years humans have tried to **interpret** them. Ancient cultures often regarded dreams as messages from gods or **ancestors**, whereas modern science looks for explanations inside the brain itself.\n\nMost vivid dreams **occur** during a stage of sleep called REM, which stands for rapid eye movement. During REM sleep, the brain is surprisingly active, almost as active as when we are awake, while the muscles of the body are temporarily paralysed. This paralysis is a useful **precaution**: it prevents us from physically acting out what we see in our dreams.\n\nScientists have proposed several theories about the **purpose** of dreaming, and none has been **conclusively** **proven**. One influential **hypothesis** suggests that dreams help the brain process memories, strengthening important information and discarding what is unnecessary. Another suggests that dreams allow us to rehearse threatening situations in a safe environment, which may explain why so many dreams involve being chased or arriving late. A third view holds that dreams help **regulate** emotions, reducing the **intensity** of painful experiences.\n\nSome researchers are more skeptical, arguing that dreams may simply be a side effect of random brain activity during sleep, without any special meaning. Despite this **controversy**, one point is widely accepted: sleep, including REM sleep, is **vital** for memory and mental health. Whether or not every dream has a message, the brain is clearly doing important work while we rest.","gloss":[{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"ancestor","he":"אב קדמון"},{"w":"occur","he":"להתרחש"},{"w":"precaution","he":"אמצעי זהירות"},{"w":"purpose","he":"מטרה, תכלית"},{"w":"conclusively","he":"באופן חד-משמעי"},{"w":"proven","he":"מוכח"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"},{"w":"regulate","he":"לווסת"},{"w":"intensity","he":"עוצמה"},{"w":"controversy","he":"מחלוקת"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"}]},{"id":"e34","lvl":"e","topic":"mind","title":"How Caffeine Tricks the Tired Brain","body":"Coffee, tea and energy drinks are **consumed** by billions of people every day, mostly for one reason: they help us stay awake. Yet caffeine does not actually give the body any new energy. Instead, it works by blocking a signal that tells the brain it is time to rest.\n\nThat signal comes from a substance called adenosine. As we stay awake, adenosine gradually **accumulates** in the brain and **attaches** to special receptors, producing the feeling of tiredness that grows during the day. Caffeine has a molecular structure that **resembles** adenosine, so it can attach to the same receptors without activating them. In effect, it **occupies** the parking spaces, and the tiredness signal cannot get through. As a result, we feel more alert, even though the body still needs sleep.\n\nThis explains several familiar experiences. Caffeine usually takes about twenty to forty minutes to reach its full effect, and in many adults about half of it is still in the body five or six hours later. A cup of coffee in the late afternoon may therefore **disrupt** sleep at night, even when a person falls asleep without difficulty. When the caffeine wears off, the adenosine that has been waiting can finally bind, producing the well-known 'crash'.\n\nRegular drinkers also develop **tolerance**. The brain responds to daily caffeine by producing more receptors, so the same cup has a weaker effect, and stopping suddenly can cause headaches and fatigue. None of this means that coffee is harmful; in **moderate** amounts, it is safe for most healthy adults. However, it is a **substitute** for sleep only in the short term, and **relying** on it for too long can make tiredness a **constant** companion.","gloss":[{"w":"consume","he":"לצרוך"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"attach","he":"להיצמד, להתחבר"},{"w":"resemble","he":"לדמות ל-"},{"w":"occupy","he":"לתפוס, לאכלס"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"tolerance","he":"סבילות"},{"w":"moderate","he":"מתון"},{"w":"substitute","he":"תחליף"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך"},{"w":"constant","he":"קבוע"}]},{"id":"e35","lvl":"e","topic":"mind","title":"Can Colour Change How We Feel?","body":"Restaurants paint their walls red, hospitals choose soft greens, and banks prefer serious shades of blue. Behind these choices lies a popular belief that colour has a direct effect on our mood and behaviour. The idea is intuitive, but scientific research suggests that the reality is more complicated than designers and marketing experts often **claim**.\n\nSome studies have found **apparent** effects. In one well-known experiment, people who saw red before a test performed slightly worse, perhaps because red is associated with danger and mistakes. Other research has linked blue light to alertness, and a few experiments have reported that people **perceive** warm-coloured rooms as warmer than cool-coloured ones. Such results have been widely **cited** in popular books and articles.\n\nHowever, many of these findings are less **reliable** than they first appear. Several studies used small samples, and later attempts to repeat them produced weaker effects or none at all. Moreover, reactions to colour are strongly shaped by **culture**. In many Western countries white is associated with weddings, whereas in parts of East Asia it has traditionally been linked to **mourning**. A colour, in other words, does not have a single, universal meaning.\n\nMost researchers now **regard** the effects of colour as real but modest and highly dependent on context. A red dress, a red warning sign and a red wall in a restaurant send very different messages. Personal experience matters too: a shade connected to a happy memory may calm one person and **bother** another. Colour can **contribute** to how we feel, but it is **by no means** a magic switch for human behaviour.","gloss":[{"w":"claim","he":"לטעון"},{"w":"apparent","he":"נראה לעין, לכאורה"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לחוות"},{"w":"cite","he":"לצטט"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"},{"w":"culture","he":"תרבות"},{"w":"mourn","he":"להתאבל"},{"w":"regard","he":"לראות כ-, להתייחס ל-"},{"w":"bother","he":"להטריד"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"}]},{"id":"e36","lvl":"e","topic":"mind","title":"Why Some Songs Give Us Chills","body":"Almost everyone has had the experience: a song reaches a certain moment, and suddenly a shiver runs down the back, the skin tingles and the hair on the arms stands up. Scientists call this **sensation** 'frisson', a French word for shiver. Although it is common, not everyone feels it, and researchers have long been curious about why music, which is only organized sound, can **provoke** such a strong **physical** response.\n\nStudies of this **phenomenon** using brain scans have found that musical chills are connected to the brain's reward system, the same network that responds to food and other pleasures. During intense musical moments, this system releases dopamine, a chemical related to pleasure and motivation. Interestingly, some research suggests that dopamine is released not only at the peak itself but also in the seconds before it, as listeners **anticipate** what is coming.\n\nThis expectation helps explain which moments produce chills. **Composers** often create tension and then release it, or **deviate** from what the listener expects: a sudden change in volume, a new instrument, or a voice entering after a long silence. When the brain's predictions are surprised in a pleasant way, the emotional reaction can become **profound**.\n\nPersonality also plays a **role**. Some studies have found that people who score high on openness to experience, a trait linked to imagination and curiosity, are more **prone to** musical chills. Personal memories matter too, since a song associated with an important period in life may carry extra emotional weight. Chills, therefore, are not simply a property of the music itself but the result of an **encounter** between a particular song and a particular listener.","gloss":[{"w":"sensation","he":"תחושה"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר"},{"w":"physical","he":"גופני"},{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"anticipate","he":"לצפות מראש"},{"w":"composer","he":"מלחין"},{"w":"deviate","he":"לסטות"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"},{"w":"role","he":"תפקיד"},{"w":"prone to","he":"נוטה ל-"},{"w":"encounter","he":"מפגש"}]},{"id":"e37","lvl":"e","topic":"world","title":"Elephant Rides or Sanctuaries?","body":"For many years, riding an elephant through the jungle was one of the most popular activities for tourists in Thailand. Today, a growing number of travellers choose instead to visit sanctuaries, where they can watch, feed and sometimes bathe elephants without riding them. This shift reflects a broader debate about animal **welfare** and the responsibility of tourists.\n\nAsian elephants have lived alongside humans in Thailand for centuries, working in forests and taking part in **royal** ceremonies. When commercial logging was banned in 1989, thousands of working elephants and their owners lost their **occupation**, and many turned to tourism. Elephant rides became a **lucrative** business, but critics argue that it came at a high price for the animals.\n\nAnimal welfare organizations have **condemned** the training methods traditionally used to make elephants **obedient**, which often involve separating young animals from their mothers and using pain to control them. They also point out that heavy seats on an elephant's back can cause injuries over time, and that animals kept in chains may suffer from stress. For these reasons, many travel companies have stopped selling ride tours altogether.\n\nYet sanctuaries are not automatically the perfect alternative. Some places that call themselves sanctuaries still keep elephants in poor conditions or allow close contact that remains stressful for the animals. Experts advise travellers to **assess** a sanctuary carefully: **genuine** ones usually offer no rides, limit physical contact and let elephants **roam** freely for much of the day. Because **captive** elephants cannot simply be released into the **wilderness**, ethical sanctuaries remain a necessary compromise, and informed visitors can help them **thrive**.","gloss":[{"w":"welfare","he":"רווחה"},{"w":"royal","he":"מלכותי"},{"w":"occupation","he":"עיסוק, פרנסה"},{"w":"lucrative","he":"רווחי"},{"w":"condemn","he":"לגנות"},{"w":"obedient","he":"צייתן"},{"w":"assess","he":"להעריך, לבחון"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי"},{"w":"roam","he":"לשוטט"},{"w":"captive","he":"שבוי, בשבי"},{"w":"wilderness","he":"טבע פראי"},{"w":"thrive","he":"לשגשג"}]},{"id":"e38","lvl":"e","topic":"world","title":"How Tea Conquered the World","body":"After water, tea is the most widely consumed drink on the planet, yet its **origin** lies in a small region of southwestern China. According to a famous legend, the **emperor** Shen Nong discovered tea nearly five thousand years ago when leaves accidentally fell into his boiling water. Historians cannot confirm this story, but archaeological evidence shows that tea was drunk in China at least two thousand years ago.\n\nFor centuries, tea remained mainly a Chinese and later Japanese **custom**, closely connected to medicine, poetry and Buddhist monasteries. During the Tang **dynasty**, a writer named Lu Yu produced a whole book about how to grow, prepare and drink it. Tea was also an important **commodity**: compressed bricks of tea were traded along routes that reached Tibet and Central Asia.\n\nEurope discovered tea in the seventeenth century, through Dutch and Portuguese **merchants**. In Britain it became a fashionable drink among the wealthy and **gradually** spread to every class of society. The demand was so enormous that it shaped world politics. Britain bought huge quantities of Chinese tea, and to **compensate** for the silver it spent, it sold opium to China, a trade that contributed to two wars in the nineteenth century.\n\nDetermined to reduce its **reliance** on China, Britain began to **cultivate** tea in India, particularly in Assam and Darjeeling, and later in Sri Lanka. These plantations transformed the **landscape** and the economy of entire regions, often at a heavy human cost to the workers. Today, from Indian chai to Moroccan mint tea, the drink has been **adapted** to countless local tastes, but each cup still carries traces of this long and complicated history.","gloss":[{"w":"origin","he":"מקור"},{"w":"emperor","he":"קיסר"},{"w":"custom","he":"מנהג"},{"w":"dynasty","he":"שושלת"},{"w":"commodity","he":"סחורה"},{"w":"merchant","he":"סוחר"},{"w":"gradually","he":"בהדרגה"},{"w":"compensate","he":"לפצות, לאזן"},{"w":"reliance","he":"הסתמכות, תלות"},{"w":"cultivate","he":"לגדל, לעבד"},{"w":"landscape","he":"נוף"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להתאים"}]},{"id":"e39","lvl":"e","topic":"world","title":"Holi and Diwali: Colour and Light in India","body":"India has a remarkable **variety** of religious and regional festivals, but two of them are especially famous around the world: Holi, the festival of colours, and Diwali, the festival of lights. Although both are rooted mainly in Hindu tradition, they are celebrated by people of many backgrounds, and each **illustrates** a different side of Indian culture.\n\nHoli takes place in spring, usually in March, and **commemorates** several legends, including the victory of good over evil and the playful love between the god Krishna and Radha. Its most famous feature is the throwing of coloured powder and water in the streets. For one day, ordinary social rules are relaxed: strangers, neighbours and relatives of different ages and **ranks** cover each other in pink, green and yellow. The night before, many communities light bonfires to symbolize the burning of evil.\n\nDiwali, which usually falls in October or November, has a quieter and more **domestic** character. Families clean and decorate their homes, light small clay lamps called diyas, and **exchange** sweets and gifts. For many Hindus, the festival recalls the return of the god Rama after years of **exile**, and it is also associated with Lakshmi, the goddess of **wealth** and **prosperity**. Fireworks are common, although some cities have tried to **restrict** them because of serious air **pollution**.\n\nDespite their differences, the two festivals share a **profound** message about renewal. Holi marks the end of winter, while Diwali celebrates light defeating darkness. For travellers, they offer an unforgettable experience, but they also demand **courtesy**: in both, visitors are guests in deeply meaningful traditions, not simply spectators at a show.","gloss":[{"w":"variety","he":"מגוון"},{"w":"illustrate","he":"להמחיש"},{"w":"commemorate","he":"להנציח, לציין"},{"w":"rank","he":"מעמד, דרגה"},{"w":"domestic","he":"ביתי"},{"w":"exchange","he":"להחליף, לתת זה לזה"},{"w":"exile","he":"גלות"},{"w":"wealth","he":"עושר"},{"w":"prosperity","he":"שגשוג"},{"w":"restrict","he":"להגביל"},{"w":"pollution","he":"זיהום"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"},{"w":"courtesy","he":"נימוס"}]},{"id":"e40","lvl":"e","topic":"world","title":"The Tuk-Tuk: Small Vehicle, Big Symbol","body":"Few things are as closely connected to the image of Asian cities as the tuk-tuk. In Bangkok, Phnom Penh and Delhi, these small three-wheeled **vehicles** **navigate** through heavy traffic, carrying tourists, workers, schoolchildren and even goods to market. Their name, many say, **imitates** the noisy sound of their engines, and for countless visitors a first ride is an unforgettable introduction to a new country.\n\nThe tuk-tuk has a surprisingly international history. It **derives** from motorized three-wheelers produced in Japan and Italy in the mid-twentieth century, which were later imported to South and Southeast Asia. Local mechanics **adapted** and modified them, and each country developed its own style. In Thailand, tuk-tuks are often brightly decorated, whereas in India the similar auto-rickshaw is usually painted green and yellow or black and yellow.\n\nTheir success is easy to explain. Tuk-tuks are cheap to buy and maintain, small enough to pass through narrow streets, and flexible enough to serve as taxis, delivery vans or even mobile shops. For many drivers, often migrants from **rural** areas, owning one provides a steady income and a degree of independence. In this sense, the tuk-tuk is not only a form of transport but also an important source of **employment**.\n\nNevertheless, the vehicle has faced criticism. Older models **emit** large amounts of exhaust and noise, **contributing** to the **severe** air pollution of many Asian cities, and some drivers have a reputation for overcharging tourists. In response, several governments have encouraged electric versions, which are quieter and cleaner. Whether powered by petrol or batteries, the tuk-tuk is likely to remain a **prominent** symbol of street life for many years.","gloss":[{"w":"vehicle","he":"כלי רכב"},{"w":"navigate","he":"לנווט"},{"w":"imitate","he":"לחקות"},{"w":"derive","he":"לנבוע, להתפתח מ-"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להתאים"},{"w":"rural","he":"כפרי"},{"w":"employment","he":"תעסוקה"},{"w":"emit","he":"לפלוט"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"severe","he":"חמור"},{"w":"prominent","he":"בולט"}]},{"id":"m01","lvl":"m","topic":"travel","title":"Ten Days Without a Single Word","body":"Every year, thousands of travelers in India, Nepal and Thailand sign up for an unusual experience: ten days in which they **undergo** a strict program of meditation without speaking, reading or even making eye contact with others. Vipassana courses, which are funded entirely by donations, require students to meditate for roughly ten hours a day, beginning before dawn.\n\nParticipants who complete the course often describe it as one of the most **profound** experiences of their lives, although many also admit that the first days were close to unbearable. Without the usual stream of conversation and screens, the mind does not become quiet, as one might expect; rather, memories, worries and physical **sensations** rush in. Back pain, boredom and a strong urge to leave are so common that teachers treat them as an **inevitable** stage of the practice rather than as a sign of failure.\n\nWhat does research say about this? Studies **conducted** on Vipassana participants have reported lower levels of stress and an improved ability to **tolerate** discomfort, and some researchers have found reductions in anxious thinking that last for several months. Other studies suggest that meditators become better at noticing a thought without immediately reacting to it, a skill that psychologists consider **crucial** for emotional regulation.\n\nNevertheless, the evidence is by no means **conclusive**. Many of the studies are small, and because people who choose to spend ten days in silence are hardly a random sample, it is difficult to **determine** whether the course causes the benefits or simply attracts people who are already motivated to change. Moreover, a minority of participants report the opposite effect: intense meditation can occasionally increase anxiety or bring painful memories to the surface, especially in people with a history of trauma.\n\nFor this reason, some psychologists **advocate** a more cautious **notion** of the retreat. It is not a cure, they argue, but a demanding training that can be valuable for those who are prepared, provided that they **approach** it with realistic expectations and seek support when they need it.","gloss":[{"w":"undergo","he":"לעבור (תהליך, חוויה)"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק, משמעותי"},{"w":"sensation","he":"תחושה"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"conduct","he":"לערוך, לבצע (מחקר)"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"},{"w":"conclusive","he":"חד-משמעי, מכריע"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"},{"w":"advocate","he":"לתמוך, לצדד ב-"},{"w":"notion","he":"תפיסה, רעיון"},{"w":"approach","he":"לגשת (לעניין)"}]},{"id":"m02","lvl":"m","topic":"travel","title":"How Backpackers Rebuilt Small Towns","body":"In the early 1990s, few foreigners visited Vang Vieng, a small town in the Lao countryside surrounded by limestone cliffs. Within two decades, however, it had been **transformed** into one of the busiest stops on the so-called Banana Pancake Trail, the informal **route** that carries young travelers through Thailand, Laos, Vietnam and Cambodia. The story of such towns illustrates both the promise and the risks of budget tourism.\n\nAt first glance, backpackers seem to be poor customers. They sleep in cheap hostels, eat street food and **bargain** over every bus ticket. Yet economists have pointed out that their spending is often more widely **distributed** than that of affluent tourists. Whereas luxury travelers tend to stay in international hotels whose **revenue** leaves the country, backpackers spend their money in family guesthouses, local restaurants and small tour agencies. In rural areas of Laos and northern Vietnam, this created new **incentives** for young people to stay in their villages rather than move to the cities in search of **wages**.\n\nThe benefits, however, came with a price. In Vang Vieng, the rapid growth of bars and river-tubing parties attracted crowds of drinkers, and a series of fatal accidents eventually forced the government to **intervene** in 2012, closing many riverside bars. The town has since tried to **restore** its image by promoting rock climbing, kayaking and nature tourism, with **substantial**, though uneven, success.\n\nSimilar tensions have appeared in Vietnamese towns such as Sapa, where trekking tours brought income to ethnic **minority** villages but also changed local culture. Some residents began to depend almost entirely on tourism, a **reliance** that became painfully clear when borders closed during the pandemic and visitors vanished overnight.\n\nResearchers who study the region therefore argue that backpacker tourism is neither a simple blessing nor a curse. Its effects depend on whether local communities retain control over land and businesses, and whether governments plan for growth rather than simply reacting to it.","gloss":[{"w":"transform","he":"לשנות מן היסוד, להפוך"},{"w":"route","he":"מסלול, נתיב"},{"w":"bargain","he":"להתמקח"},{"w":"distribute","he":"לחלק, לפזר"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסות"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"wage","he":"שכר, משכורת"},{"w":"intervene","he":"להתערב"},{"w":"restore","he":"לשקם, להחזיר למצב קודם"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"minority","he":"מיעוט"},{"w":"reliance","he":"הסתמכות, תלות"}]},{"id":"m03","lvl":"m","topic":"travel","title":"Why the Mountains Make Strong People Sick","body":"Trekkers who set out on the Annapurna Circuit in Nepal are often surprised to discover that physical fitness offers little protection against altitude sickness. Marathon runners and experienced athletes may suffer headaches and nausea, while less athletic companions continue without difficulty. The explanation lies not in strength but in the way the body **adapts** to air that contains less oxygen.\n\nAt high altitude, the air pressure is lower, so each breath delivers fewer oxygen molecules to the lungs. The body responds by breathing faster and, over several days, producing more red blood cells. This **adaptation**, known as acclimatization, is **gradual** and cannot be rushed. When trekkers climb faster than their bodies can adjust, fluid may begin to leak from small blood vessels, producing the familiar **symptoms** of **acute** mountain sickness: headache, fatigue, loss of appetite and poor sleep.\n\nIn most cases these symptoms are unpleasant but harmless. In rare cases, however, the condition can **deteriorate** into swelling of the brain or lungs, both of which may be **fatal** within hours unless the patient descends immediately. On the Annapurna Circuit, the highest point, the Thorong La pass, lies at about 5,400 meters, so many trekkers reach dangerous heights within a week.\n\nDoctors therefore emphasize a few simple **precautions**. Above 3,000 meters, trekkers are advised not to increase their sleeping altitude by more than roughly 500 meters a day, and to rest for an extra day every few days. The traditional rule \"climb high, sleep low\" reflects the same idea. Some travelers also take acetazolamide, a medication that speeds up acclimatization, although it should be used only after consulting a doctor.\n\nPerhaps the most **crucial** lesson, however, is psychological. Many serious cases **occur** not because the warning signs were invisible but because travelers chose to **disregard** them, reluctant to fall behind their group or abandon a trip they had paid for. On the mountain, admitting weakness is not a failure but a matter of survival.","gloss":[{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"adaptation","he":"הסתגלות"},{"w":"gradual","he":"הדרגתי"},{"w":"symptom","he":"תסמין"},{"w":"acute","he":"חריף"},{"w":"deteriorate","he":"להידרדר, להחמיר"},{"w":"fatal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"precaution","he":"אמצעי זהירות"},{"w":"crucial","he":"מכריע, חיוני"},{"w":"occur","he":"להתרחש, לקרות"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם מ-"}]},{"id":"m04","lvl":"m","topic":"travel","title":"The Long Journey After the Uniform","body":"Every year, thousands of young Israelis finish their military service and, within months, board a plane to India, Thailand or South America. The \"big trip\", as it is commonly called, can last anywhere from three months to a year, and it has become so widespread that sociologists regard it as a modern rite of passage, a ritual that marks the transition from one stage of life to the next.\n\nResearchers who have studied the **phenomenon** point out that its timing is not **accidental**. After two or three years in a **hierarchical** system in which almost every hour is planned by others, many soldiers feel an intense need for freedom and **leisure**. The trip offers precisely the opposite of army life: no commanders, no uniforms and no fixed schedule. It is not merely a vacation, therefore, but a deliberate break from **constraints**, and many travelers describe it as the first time they made decisions entirely on their own.\n\nParadoxically, however, this freedom is often experienced collectively. Israeli backpackers tend to travel along the same routes, stay in the same guesthouses and gather in places such as Pushkar, Manali or Cusco, where Hebrew signs and Israeli food are surprisingly common. Some scholars argue that the trip therefore **reinforces** national **identity** rather than replacing it, since travelers **encounter** the world largely through the company of other Israelis.\n\nThe trip also carries risks. Each year a small number of travelers are injured in accidents, and some experiment with drugs in ways that lead to serious psychological crises. Israeli organizations have even developed programs to **rescue** and treat young people who suffer breakdowns **abroad**, particularly in India.\n\nNevertheless, most travelers return with a **profound** sense of change. Many report greater confidence, a clearer idea of what they wish to study, and a new **appreciation** for home. Whether these changes are **sustained** once ordinary life resumes is less certain, yet the journey continues to shape the way an entire generation enters adulthood.","gloss":[{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"accidental","he":"מקרי"},{"w":"hierarchical","he":"היררכי"},{"w":"leisure","he":"פנאי"},{"w":"constraint","he":"מגבלה, אילוץ"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"encounter","he":"לפגוש, להיתקל ב-"},{"w":"rescue","he":"לחלץ, להציל"},{"w":"abroad","he":"בחו\"ל"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"},{"w":"appreciation","he":"הערכה"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לשמר, לקיים לאורך זמן"}]},{"id":"m05","lvl":"m","topic":"fitness","title":"Why Muscles Grow Only When Challenged","body":"Anyone who has spent time in a gym has probably heard the term \"progressive overload\". Behind this somewhat technical phrase lies a simple principle: muscles grow only when they are asked to do more than they are **accustomed** to. If a person lifts the same weight for the same number of repetitions every week, the body has no reason to change, and progress will soon **cease**.\n\nThe biological explanation begins with stress. When a muscle works against heavy resistance, its fibers experience mechanical tension and small amounts of damage. In response, the body **initiates** a repair process in which new proteins are built into the existing fibers, making them thicker and stronger. This process, known as hypertrophy, is not **rapid**; it occurs mainly during the hours and days after training, provided that the athlete obtains **sufficient** protein and rest.\n\nProgressive overload can be achieved in several ways. The most obvious is to add weight to the bar, but a lifter can also increase the number of repetitions or sets, slow down each movement, or shorten the rest between sets. Researchers have found that, contrary to an old belief, light weights can **yield** similar muscle growth to heavy ones, as long as each set is performed close to the point of failure. Heavy loads, however, remain **superior** for building maximal strength.\n\nDespite its simplicity, the principle is frequently misunderstood. Some beginners **interpret** it as a demand to add weight in every session, a strategy that inevitably leads to poor technique, plateaus or injury. Experts **emphasize** that progress should be **gradual** and measured over weeks rather than days, and that periods of lighter training allow the body to recover.\n\nPerhaps the most encouraging finding concerns **consistency**. Studies comparing different programs suggest that the precise choice of exercises matters less than people assume, whereas regular training over months **determines** most of the results. In other words, the body rewards not the most dramatic workout but the most patient one.","gloss":[{"w":"accustomed","he":"רגיל ל-"},{"w":"cease","he":"להיפסק, לחדול"},{"w":"initiate","he":"להתחיל, ליזום"},{"w":"rapid","he":"מהיר"},{"w":"sufficient","he":"מספיק"},{"w":"yield","he":"להניב"},{"w":"superior","he":"עדיף, טוב יותר"},{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"gradual","he":"הדרגתי"},{"w":"consistency","he":"עקביות"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"}]},{"id":"m06","lvl":"m","topic":"fitness","title":"Short and Hard or Long and Easy?","body":"Few debates in the fitness world are as **persistent** as the one between high-intensity interval training, known as HIIT, and traditional steady cardio. Supporters of HIIT claim that a twenty-minute session of alternating sprints and short rests can **yield** the same benefits as an hour of jogging. **Proponents** of steady exercise, on the other hand, argue that slower, longer sessions are safer and easier to maintain.\n\nThe appeal of HIIT is easy to understand. In a world in which lack of time is the most common excuse for not exercising, a workout that promises results in minutes is highly attractive. Research **conducted** over the past two decades has indeed shown that interval training improves cardiovascular fitness, measured as the body's maximal oxygen uptake, at least as **efficiently** as moderate exercise, and sometimes more so. Some studies have also found benefits for blood sugar control.\n\nHowever, the popular claim that HIIT burns far more fat is **apparently** **exaggerated**. When total energy expenditure is compared, the advantage of the so-called \"afterburn\" effect turns out to be relatively small. Moreover, high-intensity sessions place a considerable **burden** on muscles and joints, and they require a level of motivation that many people find hard to **sustain**. Several studies report that beginners are more likely to quit an exercise program when it is unpleasant, which **undermines** the theoretical advantages of intensity.\n\nSteady cardio has its own strengths. Long, easy sessions build **stamina**, strengthen the heart and can be performed almost every day without exhausting the body. Many elite endurance athletes, in fact, spend roughly eighty percent of their training at low intensity and only about twenty percent at high intensity, a pattern that coaches describe as polarized training.\n\nFor most people, therefore, the question is not which method is **superior** but how to combine them. A sensible program might include one or two interval sessions a week alongside longer, gentler workouts. Ultimately, the best form of exercise is **arguably** the one a person will actually continue to do.","gloss":[{"w":"persistent","he":"מתמשך, עיקש"},{"w":"yield","he":"להניב"},{"w":"proponent","he":"תומך, מצדד"},{"w":"conduct","he":"לערוך (מחקר)"},{"w":"efficiently","he":"ביעילות"},{"w":"apparently","he":"ככל הנראה"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"burden","he":"נטל, עומס"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לשמר לאורך זמן"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער, לפגוע ב-"},{"w":"stamina","he":"כושר סבולת"},{"w":"superior","he":"עדיף"},{"w":"arguably","he":"ניתן לטעון ש-"}]},{"id":"m07","lvl":"m","topic":"fitness","title":"Training With the Cycle: Hype and Evidence","body":"In recent years, social media has filled with advice on \"cycle syncing\", the idea that women should **alter** their training according to the phases of their menstrual cycle. Influencers recommend heavy lifting in the first half of the cycle and gentle yoga in the second, and several apps now offer workout plans based on hormonal patterns. The scientific picture, however, is considerably less clear than these confident recommendations suggest.\n\nThe theory is not **arbitrary**. Levels of estrogen and progesterone **fluctuate** substantially over a typical cycle of about twenty-eight days, and both hormones affect muscles, metabolism and body temperature. Estrogen, which rises in the first half of the cycle, may support muscle repair, whereas progesterone, which dominates the second half, slightly raises body temperature and may make exercise in the heat feel harder.\n\nYet when researchers have attempted to measure actual performance, the results have been **ambiguous**. Large reviews of the available studies have found that performance may be slightly reduced during the early follicular phase, around menstruation, but the effect is small and varies greatly between individuals. Many of the studies were small, used different methods and did not **accurately** confirm the phase of the cycle, which makes their conclusions **unreliable**.\n\nPart of the problem is historical. For decades, sports science was based **predominantly** on male participants, partly because researchers regarded the hormonal changes of women as a complication. As a result, the **scope** of reliable knowledge about female athletes remains surprisingly narrow, and experts warn against turning **tentative** findings into strict rules.\n\nWhat, then, can women **infer** from current research? Most specialists suggest that the most sensible approach is individual. Rather than following a general plan, an athlete can track her own symptoms, such as fatigue, cramps or poor sleep, and adjust training when she genuinely feels worse. Some women notice **distinct** patterns, while others notice none at all. Neither experience is abnormal, and neither should be **disregarded**.","gloss":[{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"arbitrary","he":"שרירותי"},{"w":"fluctuate","he":"להשתנות, לתנוד"},{"w":"ambiguous","he":"לא חד-משמעי"},{"w":"accurately","he":"במדויק"},{"w":"unreliable","he":"לא אמין"},{"w":"predominantly","he":"בעיקר, ברובו"},{"w":"scope","he":"היקף"},{"w":"tentative","he":"זמני, לא סופי"},{"w":"infer","he":"להסיק"},{"w":"distinct","he":"ברור, מובחן"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם מ-"}]},{"id":"m08","lvl":"m","topic":"fitness","title":"The Recovery Tool Athletes Ignore","body":"Athletes today **invest** enormous sums in recovery: massage guns, ice baths, compression boots and expensive supplements. Yet a growing body of research suggests that the most powerful recovery tool is free, widely available and frequently **neglected**: sleep.\n\nDuring deep sleep, the body releases a large proportion of its daily growth hormone, which plays a **vital** role in repairing muscle tissue. Sleep also helps regulate appetite hormones, restore energy stores and consolidate the motor skills practiced during the day. When sleep is cut short, these processes are **disrupted**, and the effects can be measured both in the laboratory and on the field.\n\nOne frequently cited study from Stanford University followed college basketball players who were asked to extend their sleep to about ten hours a night for several weeks. By the end of the period, their sprint times had improved and their shooting **accuracy** had increased significantly. Although the sample was small, the findings were striking enough to attract the attention of professional teams. Other studies have found that athletes who regularly sleep fewer than eight hours are more **prone to** injury, perhaps because fatigue impairs reaction time and coordination.\n\nSleep deprivation also affects the mind. Tired athletes **perceive** the same workout as harder, lose motivation more easily and make poorer decisions. Since effort is partly a matter of **perception**, a poor night can **undermine** performance even when the muscles themselves have recovered.\n\nDespite this evidence, many athletes continue to **sacrifice** sleep, whether because of early training sessions, late-night screens or simply the belief that rest is a sign of weakness. Experts therefore recommend treating sleep as part of training rather than as its **absence**: keeping consistent bedtimes, limiting caffeine in the afternoon and, when necessary, taking short naps. The message is **by no means** glamorous, but it may be the most **reliable** advice in sports science.","gloss":[{"w":"invest","he":"להשקיע"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"accuracy","he":"דיוק"},{"w":"prone to","he":"נוטה ל-, חשוף ל-"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לחוות"},{"w":"perception","he":"תפיסה"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער, לפגוע ב-"},{"w":"sacrifice","he":"להקריב, לוותר על"},{"w":"absence","he":"היעדר"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"}]},{"id":"m09","lvl":"m","topic":"routine","title":"The Brain's Autopilot","body":"Most people **assume** that their daily behavior is the product of conscious decisions. In reality, researchers estimate that a large proportion of what we do each day, from brushing our teeth to checking our phones, is performed largely on autopilot. Understanding how this autopilot works may explain why habits are so difficult to change.\n\nMuch of the early evidence came from experiments with rats. Researchers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology trained rats to run through a maze to find chocolate, while recording activity in a region deep inside the brain called the basal ganglia. At first, the animals' brains were highly active throughout the run, as they sniffed and explored. As the route became familiar, however, activity **declined** in the middle of the run and became concentrated at its beginning and end. The behavior had been **converted** into an automatic **sequence**, or \"chunk\", which required little mental effort.\n\nThis finding underlies the so-called habit loop, popularized by the journalist Charles Duhigg. It **comprises** three components: a cue that triggers the behavior, the routine itself, and a reward that tells the brain the sequence is worth remembering. Once the loop is established, the cue alone can launch the routine, often before the person is even **conscious** of it.\n\nThe **implications** are significant. Because habits are stored in a different system from deliberate decisions, willpower is a weak tool for changing them. A person may genuinely **intend** to stop eating sweets in the evening, yet the cue of sitting on the sofa will **relentlessly** prompt the old routine. Furthermore, old habits are rarely erased completely; they remain **dormant** and can return under stress.\n\nResearchers therefore suggest focusing on the cues rather than on **resistance**. Changing the environment, for example by keeping the phone in another room, removes the trigger entirely. Alternatively, a person can keep the cue and reward but **substitute** a new routine, gradually building a better loop in place of the old one.","gloss":[{"w":"assume","he":"להניח"},{"w":"decline","he":"לרדת, לפחות"},{"w":"convert","he":"להמיר, להפוך"},{"w":"sequence","he":"רצף"},{"w":"comprise","he":"להיות מורכב מ-"},{"w":"conscious","he":"מודע"},{"w":"implication","he":"השלכה"},{"w":"intend","he":"להתכוון"},{"w":"relentlessly","he":"ללא הרף"},{"w":"dormant","he":"רדום"},{"w":"resistance","he":"התנגדות"},{"w":"substitute","he":"להחליף, לשים במקום"}]},{"id":"m10","lvl":"m","topic":"routine","title":"Owls, Larks and the Tyranny of Morning","body":"Some people jump out of bed at six in the morning full of energy, while others struggle to think clearly before noon but feel most alert late at night. For a long time, these differences were **attributed** to discipline or laziness. Scientists now recognize them as **genuine** biological variations, known as chronotypes.\n\nEvery human body follows a circadian rhythm, an internal cycle of roughly twenty-four hours that regulates sleep, body temperature and hormone release. The rhythm is controlled by a small cluster of cells in the brain that responds mainly to light. However, the exact timing of the clock **varies** from person to person. Twin research and genetic studies suggest that chronotype is partly **hereditary**, with dozens of genes contributing to whether someone is an early \"lark\" or a late \"owl\".\n\nChronotype also changes with age. Children tend to be early risers, whereas **adolescents** shift dramatically toward later hours, reaching a peak of lateness around the age of twenty. After that, the clock gradually moves earlier again. This pattern has led some researchers to **advocate** later school start times, arguing that forcing teenagers to learn at eight in the morning is like asking adults to work in the middle of their biological night.\n\nThe difficulty is that modern society is organized largely around early schedules. Owls who must wake up early experience what researchers call \"social jet lag\", a **chronic** mismatch between their internal clock and their obligations. Studies have linked this mismatch to poorer mood, lower academic performance and a greater **inclination** toward unhealthy habits, although it is difficult to separate cause from effect.\n\nCan an owl become a lark? To some **extent**, yes. Exposure to bright morning light and the **avoidance** of screens at night can shift the clock earlier by an hour or two. Nevertheless, the underlying tendency appears **inherent** and is unlikely to disappear entirely. For many people, the most **feasible** solution is not to fight their chronotype but to design a routine that **accommodates** it.","gloss":[{"w":"attribute","he":"לייחס"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות, להיות שונה"},{"w":"hereditary","he":"תורשתי"},{"w":"adolescent","he":"מתבגר"},{"w":"advocate","he":"לתמוך, לצדד ב-"},{"w":"chronic","he":"כרוני, מתמשך"},{"w":"inclination","he":"נטייה"},{"w":"extent","he":"מידה, היקף"},{"w":"avoidance","he":"הימנעות"},{"w":"inherent","he":"מובנה, טבוע"},{"w":"feasible","he":"בר-ביצוע, אפשרי"},{"w":"accommodate","he":"להתאים את עצמו ל-"}]},{"id":"m11","lvl":"m","topic":"routine","title":"Why the Hundredth Choice Is the Hardest","body":"By the end of a long day, even simple choices, such as what to eat for dinner or which show to watch, can feel strangely exhausting. Psychologists have a name for this experience: decision fatigue, the idea that making many decisions gradually **depletes** our ability to make good ones.\n\nThe concept gained wide attention through a famous study of parole judges in Israel, published in 2011. Researchers found that judges were far more likely to grant parole early in the morning and immediately after food breaks, while the rate of favorable **verdicts** dropped to nearly zero just before each break. The authors suggested that, as the judges grew mentally tired, they **resorted to** the easiest and safest option: denying the request and keeping the prisoner in **detention**.\n\nThe study became one of the most frequently **cited** examples in popular psychology, but it has also attracted serious criticism. Some researchers pointed out that the order of cases was not random; prisoners without lawyers, whose requests were less likely to succeed, tended to be heard just before breaks. Such factors may **account for** part of the pattern. Similarly, the broader theory of \"ego depletion\", which claimed that willpower is a limited resource like fuel, has failed to **replicate** in several large studies, leaving its **validity** in doubt.\n\nNevertheless, the everyday experience remains familiar, and some explanations do not depend on the original theory. Making decisions requires attention and comparison, and after hours of effort, people may simply prefer shortcuts, choosing the default option or **postponing** the decision altogether.\n\nWhatever the precise mechanism, the practical advice is similar. Successful people often reduce the number of trivial choices they face, for instance by planning meals in advance or wearing similar clothes every day. By making important decisions early and turning small ones into routines, they **preserve** their mental energy for the choices that **genuinely** matter.","gloss":[{"w":"deplete","he":"לרוקן, לדלדל"},{"w":"verdict","he":"פסק דין, הכרעה"},{"w":"resort to","he":"לפנות ל- (כפתרון אחרון)"},{"w":"detention","he":"מעצר"},{"w":"cite","he":"לצטט, להביא כדוגמה"},{"w":"account for","he":"להסביר, להוות סיבה ל-"},{"w":"replicate","he":"לשחזר (תוצאה)"},{"w":"validity","he":"תוקף"},{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"},{"w":"genuinely","he":"באמת"}]},{"id":"m12","lvl":"m","topic":"routine","title":"Coming Home to an Empty Schedule","body":"For months, every day of a long journey is different. A traveler wakes up in a new town, decides on the spot where to eat and sleep, and **encounters** strangers who may become friends by evening. When the trip ends, many returning travelers are surprised to find that ordinary life, rather than feeling comfortable, feels strangely empty. Rebuilding a daily routine turns out to be one of the most underestimated challenges of coming home.\n\nPsychologists refer to this difficulty as reverse culture shock. Although it receives less attention than the shock of arriving in a foreign country, it can be equally intense. Returning travelers have changed, yet their home **surroundings** have largely remained the same, and friends who were **eager** to hear about the trip quickly lose interest. The **contrast** between constant novelty and predictable days can produce boredom, restlessness and even mild depression.\n\nOne reason routine matters is biological. Regular times for waking, eating and exercising help stabilize the circadian rhythm, which may have been **disrupted** by months of night buses and changing time zones. Psychologically, routine also provides a sense of control and purpose, two factors that researchers **consistently** associate with wellbeing.\n\nHowever, experts warn against trying to rebuild everything at once. A person who returns and immediately **imposes** a **rigid** schedule of work, study and training is likely to feel overwhelmed and **abandon** it within weeks. A more effective strategy is to begin with a few **anchors**, such as a fixed wake-up time and three weekly workouts, and to add new elements **gradually** once these feel natural.\n\nInterestingly, the trip itself can become a resource rather than a loss. Many travelers discover habits on the road, such as walking long distances, cooking simple food or spending time without a phone, that they wish to **retain**. Incorporating these into daily life allows the journey to continue in a quieter form, long after the backpack has been put away.","gloss":[{"w":"encounter","he":"לפגוש, להיתקל ב-"},{"w":"surroundings","he":"סביבה"},{"w":"eager","he":"להוט, משתוקק"},{"w":"contrast","he":"ניגוד"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"consistently","he":"בעקביות"},{"w":"impose","he":"לכפות"},{"w":"rigid","he":"נוקשה"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש"},{"w":"anchor","he":"עוגן"},{"w":"gradually","he":"בהדרגה"},{"w":"retain","he":"לשמור, להחזיק ב-"}]},{"id":"m13","lvl":"m","topic":"social","title":"Why Friendships Fade After Big Changes","body":"Most people can name at least one close friend who gradually disappeared from their life, not after a dramatic fight but simply through **absence**. Messages became less frequent, plans were **postponed**, and eventually the friendship existed mainly as a memory. Researchers who study social networks have found that this kind of quiet drift is by far the most common way friendships end.\n\nMajor life transitions are particularly dangerous moments. Finishing school, completing military service, moving to a new city, starting university or entering a serious relationship all **disrupt** the structures that once kept friends together. Psychologists emphasize that many friendships are built less on deep **compatibility** than on proximity: people become close because they see each other regularly, often without planning to. When the shared classroom, base or neighborhood disappears, so does the effortless contact.\n\nTime is another factor. One study by the communication researcher Jeffrey Hall estimated that it takes roughly two hundred hours spent together to turn a stranger into a close friend. Maintaining that level of closeness also requires **substantial** investment, and after a transition, the hours are often **devoted** to new obligations and new people. The evolutionary psychologist Robin Dunbar has argued that people who enter a romantic relationship typically lose about two close friends, because emotional energy is limited.\n\nTransitions can also create subtle differences in **identity**. A friend who travels for six months may return with new interests and priorities, while those who stayed behind feel that the person they knew has changed. Neither side is necessarily at fault, yet the resulting **gap** can make conversations feel awkward, and both may **hesitate** to admit it.\n\nNevertheless, drifting is not **inevitable**. Researchers suggest that friendships survive transitions when at least one person makes a **deliberate** effort to maintain contact, such as a regular call or a shared activity. Perhaps most importantly, people should not **interpret** a decline in contact as rejection, since the other person is often simply overwhelmed and would **appreciate** the first message.","gloss":[{"w":"absence","he":"היעדרות, היעדר"},{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"compatibility","he":"התאמה"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"devote","he":"להקדיש"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"gap","he":"פער"},{"w":"hesitate","he":"להסס"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"deliberate","he":"מכוון, מתוכנן"},{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"appreciate","he":"להעריך"}]},{"id":"m14","lvl":"m","topic":"social","title":"Scrolling Through Other People's Best Days","body":"Long before the invention of the smartphone, the psychologist Leon Festinger proposed that people evaluate themselves largely by comparing themselves with others. His social comparison theory, published in 1954, has since been developed by researchers who **distinguish** between upward comparison, in which we look at people who seem better off, and downward comparison, in which we look at those who seem worse off. Seventy years later, social media has turned this ancient tendency into a constant activity.\n\nThe problem is not only the **frequency** of comparison but also its content. Instagram and TikTok present a carefully edited version of reality: holidays, toned bodies, perfect nails and happy couples. Because people rarely post their boring or painful moments, users compare their ordinary lives with other people's highlights, a contest they are certain to lose. Several studies have linked heavy passive use, meaning scrolling without interacting, to lower self-esteem, increased **envy** and **symptoms** of depression, particularly among young women.\n\nHowever, the research is more complex than headlines suggest. Many of the findings are correlational, which means they cannot **determine** whether social media causes unhappiness or whether unhappy people simply spend more time online. Moreover, the effects appear to be relatively small on average, while varying **considerably** between individuals. For some users, platforms provide support, information and a sense of belonging, especially for those who feel isolated or belong to a **minority**.\n\nWhat seems to matter most is the way people use these platforms. Active use, such as messaging friends or commenting, tends to be less harmful than passive consumption. Equally important is the **perception** of what one sees: users who remind themselves that posts are **selective** and often staged are less affected by upward comparison.\n\nSome psychologists therefore recommend practical steps rather than complete **abstinence**: unfollowing accounts that consistently **provoke** envy, limiting scrolling time, and noticing one's mood after using an app. Such habits do not eliminate comparison, which is **inherent** in human nature, but they may **restrain** its most damaging effects.","gloss":[{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין, להבדיל"},{"w":"frequency","he":"תדירות"},{"w":"envy","he":"קנאה"},{"w":"symptom","he":"תסמין"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"},{"w":"considerably","he":"במידה ניכרת"},{"w":"minority","he":"מיעוט"},{"w":"perception","he":"תפיסה"},{"w":"selective","he":"בררני, סלקטיבי"},{"w":"abstinence","he":"הימנעות מוחלטת"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר, להתסיס"},{"w":"inherent","he":"טבוע, מובנה"},{"w":"restrain","he":"לרסן"}]},{"id":"m15","lvl":"m","topic":"social","title":"When the Group Chat Turns Hostile","body":"Almost everyone who belongs to a group chat has witnessed it: a casual message is misread, someone responds sharply, others take sides, and within minutes a friendly conversation has become a **tense** argument. Although group chats help friends stay connected across distances, they also seem to **generate** misunderstandings that would rarely occur face to face.\n\nOne reason is the absence of nonverbal signals. In spoken conversation, listeners rely heavily on tone of voice, facial expressions and **gestures** to understand what a speaker means. In text, these signals disappear, leaving the reader to **interpret** words on their own. Research on email communication has shown that people greatly overestimate how clearly their tone comes across; writers who intended to be sarcastic or funny were often perceived as serious or rude. A short reply such as \"ok\" may be neutral to the sender but **hostile** to the recipient.\n\nReaders also tend to fill the gap with their own emotions. Someone who is already anxious about their place in the group is likely to read **ambiguous** messages negatively, a **bias** that turns small comments into apparent insults. Silence is especially easy to misread: when a message receives no reply, members may **infer** rejection, although the others may simply be busy.\n\nThe group format adds further complications. Disagreements that would normally be **resolved** privately take place in front of an audience, which makes participants more defensive and less willing to **admit** they were wrong. Furthermore, messages are permanent, so a careless remark can be reread, photographed in a screenshot and **circulated** long after the moment has passed.\n\nCommunication experts therefore offer a simple rule: text is suitable for logistics but poor at handling conflict. When tension **emerges**, moving the conversation to a phone call or a personal meeting usually reduces hostility quickly. Assuming good intentions, asking rather than accusing, and responding after a short pause rather than immediately can also prevent a minor misunderstanding from becoming a lasting **rift**.","gloss":[{"w":"tense","he":"מתוח"},{"w":"generate","he":"לייצר, ליצור"},{"w":"gesture","he":"מחווה, תנועת יד"},{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"hostile","he":"עוין"},{"w":"ambiguous","he":"דו-משמעי"},{"w":"bias","he":"הטיה"},{"w":"infer","he":"להסיק"},{"w":"resolve","he":"לפתור, ליישב"},{"w":"admit","he":"להודות"},{"w":"circulate","he":"להפיץ, להסתובב"},{"w":"emerge","he":"לצוץ, להופיע"},{"w":"rift","he":"קרע, שבר (ביחסים)"}]},{"id":"m16","lvl":"m","topic":"social","title":"They Liked You More Than You Think","body":"After a conversation with someone new, many people replay the exchange in their heads, wondering whether they talked too much, said something foolish or bored the other person. According to research published in 2018, these worries are usually **unfounded**. Psychologists have identified a consistent **misperception** they call the \"liking gap\": after meeting someone, people systematically underestimate how much the other person liked them and enjoyed their company.\n\nIn the original studies, conducted by researchers at several American universities, pairs of strangers were asked to have short conversations, after which each participant **assessed** how much they liked their partner and how much they believed their partner liked them. The results were strikingly **consistent**. Participants liked their partners more than they thought their partners liked them, and the gap appeared whether the conversation lasted five minutes or forty-five. It also **lingered**: in a study of first-year university students living together, the gap persisted for much of the academic year.\n\nWhy does this happen? One explanation is that people are highly **critical** of their own performance in conversation. While talking, they are busy monitoring themselves, worrying about **awkward** pauses or mistakes, and afterward they recall these moments **vividly**. Their partner, by contrast, barely noticed these details and focused on the general **impression**, which was usually positive. In other words, we judge ourselves by our flaws and others by the overall experience.\n\nThe liking gap has practical consequences. Because people **presume** that others are less interested than they really are, they may **hesitate** to follow up after a pleasant meeting, invite a new acquaintance for coffee or deepen a promising friendship. For someone who has just moved to a new city or returned from a long trip, this hesitation can **deprive** them of connections that were already within reach.\n\nThe encouraging lesson is that most people are more liked than they realize. Remembering this may make it easier to send the second message, which the other person will very likely welcome.","gloss":[{"w":"unfounded","he":"חסר בסיס"},{"w":"misperception","he":"תפיסה שגויה"},{"w":"assess","he":"להעריך, לאמוד"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"linger","he":"להתמהמה, להימשך"},{"w":"critical","he":"ביקורתי"},{"w":"awkward","he":"מביך"},{"w":"vividly","he":"בבהירות, באופן חי"},{"w":"impression","he":"רושם"},{"w":"presume","he":"להניח"},{"w":"hesitate","he":"להסס"},{"w":"deprive","he":"לשלול, למנוע מ-"}]},{"id":"m17","lvl":"m","topic":"love","title":"How Childhood Shapes the Way We Love","body":"Why do some people feel calm and confident in relationships, while others constantly fear being **abandoned** or feel suffocated when a partner gets too close? Attachment theory offers one of the most influential explanations. Originally developed by the British psychiatrist John Bowlby to describe the bond between infants and their caregivers, the theory was later applied to adult romantic relationships by researchers in the 1980s.\n\nAccording to the theory, early experiences with caregivers create internal expectations about whether others will be available and responsive. Adults with a **secure** attachment style generally trust their partners, feel comfortable with intimacy and can **tolerate** periods of distance without panic. Those with an **anxious** style, by contrast, often worry that their partner does not truly love them; they may seek constant reassurance and interpret a delayed reply as a sign of rejection. People with an **avoidant** style tend to value independence so highly that closeness feels threatening, and they may withdraw precisely when a relationship becomes serious.\n\nThe anxious-avoidant combination is **notoriously** difficult. The more one partner pursues, the more the other retreats, which in turn increases the first partner's anxiety, creating a painful cycle that both may **perceive** as proof of their fears.\n\nDespite its popularity, attachment theory is frequently **oversimplified** online, where people use it to label themselves or their partners. Researchers emphasize that attachment is better described as a set of **dimensions** than as fixed categories, and that most people show a mixture of tendencies. Moreover, attachment styles are not **predetermined** by childhood alone; genetics, temperament and later experiences all **contribute**.\n\nPerhaps the most hopeful finding is that attachment can change. Studies suggest that a stable, supportive relationship can gradually make an anxious or avoidant person more secure, a process sometimes called \"earned security\". Therapy can **facilitate** the same shift. In other words, the way we love is shaped by the past but not **confined** to it.","gloss":[{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש"},{"w":"secure","he":"בטוח"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"},{"w":"anxious","he":"חרד"},{"w":"avoidant","he":"נמנע"},{"w":"notoriously","he":"באופן ידוע (לרעה)"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לראות כ-"},{"w":"oversimplify","he":"לפשט יתר על המידה"},{"w":"dimension","he":"ממד"},{"w":"predetermined","he":"נקבע מראש"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"facilitate","he":"להקל, לסייע"},{"w":"confine","he":"להגביל, לתחום"}]},{"id":"m18","lvl":"m","topic":"love","title":"The Four Signs a Relationship Is in Trouble","body":"For several decades, the American psychologist John Gottman studied couples in a laboratory nicknamed the \"Love Lab\", recording their conversations, facial expressions and even heart rates while they discussed areas of disagreement. His central finding surprised many people: what predicts the future of a relationship is not whether a couple argues, but how they argue.\n\nGottman identified four patterns of communication that he considered particularly destructive and **dubbed** them the \"four horsemen\", a reference to the biblical horsemen of the apocalypse. The first is criticism, which attacks a partner's character rather than a specific behavior. The second is **contempt**, expressed through sarcasm, mockery or eye-rolling, which Gottman regarded as the single strongest **predictor** of divorce. The third is defensiveness, in which a partner responds to complaints with excuses or **counterattacks** instead of taking responsibility. The fourth is stonewalling, when one partner emotionally withdraws and stops responding altogether, often because they feel overwhelmed.\n\nGottman also **proposed** an **antidote** to each horseman. Criticism can be replaced by a gentle complaint that focuses on one's own feelings, contempt by **deliberately** expressing **appreciation**, defensiveness by **acknowledging** even a small part of the problem, and stonewalling by taking a short break to calm down before returning to the conversation.\n\nSome of Gottman's claims have been **controversial**. In particular, his reports of predicting divorce with over ninety percent accuracy have been criticized, since the predictions were based on statistical models **derived** from the same couples they were meant to predict, rather than tested on new ones. Nevertheless, the broader insight is widely accepted among therapists.\n\nThe value of the four horsemen lies in their simplicity. Conflict in a long relationship is **inevitable**, and even happy couples have problems that are never fully **resolved**. What distinguishes them is that they manage to argue without destroying the **mutual** respect on which the relationship rests.","gloss":[{"w":"dub","he":"לכנות"},{"w":"contempt","he":"בוז"},{"w":"predictor","he":"מנבא"},{"w":"counterattack","he":"מתקפת נגד"},{"w":"propose","he":"להציע"},{"w":"antidote","he":"תרופת נגד"},{"w":"deliberately","he":"במכוון"},{"w":"appreciation","he":"הוקרה, הערכה"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להכיר ב-, להודות ב-"},{"w":"controversial","he":"שנוי במחלוקת"},{"w":"derive","he":"לגזור, להפיק"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"resolve","he":"לפתור"},{"w":"mutual","he":"הדדי"}]},{"id":"m19","lvl":"m","topic":"love","title":"Too Many Matches, Too Little Love","body":"Dating apps promise something earlier generations could hardly imagine: access to thousands of potential partners at the touch of a screen. Yet many users report feeling **exhausted**, cynical and less satisfied than ever. Psychologists suggest that the very **abundance** of choice may be part of the problem.\n\nThe concept of choice overload became famous through a study in which shoppers at a supermarket were offered either six or twenty-four varieties of jam. Although the larger **display** attracted more attention, customers who saw fewer options were considerably more likely to make a purchase. Later attempts to **replicate** the effect produced mixed results, but the general idea, that too many options can make decisions harder and less satisfying, has been applied widely, including to romance.\n\nOn dating apps, the effect may take a particular form. When an **apparently** endless supply of profiles is available, users may become less committed to any single person, constantly wondering whether someone better is one swipe away. Some researchers have found that people who browse many profiles in a row become increasingly likely to reject them, a pattern **attributed** to fatigue and rising standards. Others note that evaluating people through photos and short descriptions encourages a **superficial**, shopping-like mindset that **contradicts** the slow process through which attraction often develops.\n\nThe algorithms themselves add further complexity. Companies rarely reveal exactly how they decide which profiles to show, but their **revenue** depends on keeping users engaged, and some critics argue that this creates a **conflict of interest**: a user who finds a lasting partner is a user who deletes the app. The companies **reject** this claim, emphasizing that successful couples are their best advertising.\n\nNone of this means that apps cannot work; millions of couples have met through them. Experts suggest, however, that users can **counteract** choice overload by limiting the number of profiles they view, moving more quickly from chatting to meeting, and giving each match a **genuine** chance before **disregarding** it in favor of the next.","gloss":[{"w":"exhausted","he":"מותש"},{"w":"abundance","he":"שפע"},{"w":"display","he":"תצוגה"},{"w":"replicate","he":"לשחזר (תוצאה)"},{"w":"apparently","he":"לכאורה, כנראה"},{"w":"attribute","he":"לייחס"},{"w":"superficial","he":"שטחי"},{"w":"contradict","he":"לסתור"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסות"},{"w":"conflict of interest","he":"ניגוד עניינים"},{"w":"reject","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"counteract","he":"לנטרל, לפעול נגד"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, כן"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם מ-, לפסול"}]},{"id":"m20","lvl":"m","topic":"love","title":"Why Heartbreak Really Hurts","body":"People in many languages describe the end of a relationship in physical terms: a broken heart, a painful loss, a feeling of being hit. For a long time, such expressions were regarded as mere metaphors. Over the past two decades, however, neuroscientists have found evidence that the comparison may be more **literal** than it appears.\n\nIn a well-known series of experiments, participants played a simple computer game in which a ball was passed between them and two other players. Without warning, the other players stopped throwing the ball to the participant, who was left **excluded**. Brain scans **conducted** during the game showed increased activity in a region called the anterior cingulate cortex, an area **associated** with the distressing aspect of physical pain. Later, a study of people who had recently **undergone** an unwanted breakup found that viewing a photograph of the former partner activated regions involved in processing pain.\n\nFrom an evolutionary point of view, this overlap makes sense. For early humans, being **abandoned** by the group could be **fatal**, since survival depended on cooperation and protection. A system that made social rejection feel painful would have motivated people to repair relationships and stay connected. In other words, the brain may have **borrowed** existing pain circuits to signal social danger.\n\nRemarkably, one study found that taking acetaminophen, a common painkiller, for three weeks reduced reported feelings of social hurt compared with a placebo. The finding was **tentative**, and experts caution against treating heartbreak with medication, but it highlights how closely the two experiences may be linked. Other researchers have argued that claims of neural overlap were **exaggerated**, since the same brain regions respond to many kinds of distress.\n\nUnderstanding heartbreak in this way can be reassuring. The intense **torment** that follows a breakup is not a sign of weakness or **excessive** sensitivity but a normal response of a brain designed for connection. Like physical injuries, emotional wounds generally heal **gradually**, especially when the person receives support from friends and allows time to do its work.","gloss":[{"w":"literal","he":"מילולי, פשוטו כמשמעו"},{"w":"exclude","he":"להוציא, להדיר"},{"w":"conduct","he":"לערוך, לבצע"},{"w":"associate","he":"לקשר, לשייך"},{"w":"undergo","he":"לעבור (חוויה)"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש"},{"w":"fatal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"borrow","he":"לשאול, לקחת בהשאלה"},{"w":"tentative","he":"זמני, לא סופי"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"torment","he":"ייסורים"},{"w":"excessive","he":"מופרז"},{"w":"gradually","he":"בהדרגה"}]},{"id":"m21","lvl":"m","topic":"beauty","title":"The Hidden Chemistry Behind a Perfect Set","body":"Acrylic nails appear simple: a liquid and a powder are mixed into a paste, shaped over the natural nail, and left to harden into a **durable** surface that can last for weeks. Behind this familiar routine, however, lies a chemical reaction that few clients ever consider. The liquid, known as a monomer, reacts with the powder, a polymer, and their molecules link into long chains, a process that **transforms** a soft bead into a rigid shell within minutes.\n\nThe chemistry itself is not the main problem; the **vapor** it releases is. Most professional liquids today are based on ethyl methacrylate, or EMA, but a cheaper relative, methyl methacrylate (MMA), still appears in some salons, although several American states have **restricted** its use in nail products. MMA bonds so strongly that removing it can tear the nail plate, and it has been linked to allergic reactions and nail damage that may take months to heal.\n\nFor the client, an appointment every few weeks poses relatively little risk. For the technician, who may **inhale** fumes and fine dust for eight hours a day, the picture is quite different. Studies of salon workers have reported higher rates of headaches, eye irritation and breathing difficulties, and researchers have raised concerns about long-term **exposure**, especially in small rooms where the sharp **odor** never fully disappears.\n\nThe solution is less glamorous than the product. Experts recommend local exhaust ventilation, a system attached to the work table that captures vapors and dust at the source rather than simply moving them around the room. Nitrile gloves, properly fitted masks for filing, and keeping containers closed between clients are equally important **precautions**. Opening a window, although better than nothing, is rarely **sufficient**.\n\nFor anyone building a nail business, such measures are not a luxury but a basic investment in a long career. A technician's lungs are, after all, as **vital** to her work as her hands, and a salon that smells fresh is often one that clients **perceive** as more professional and trustworthy.","gloss":[{"w":"durable","he":"עמיד, מחזיק מעמד"},{"w":"transform","he":"להפוך, לשנות צורה"},{"w":"vapor","he":"אדים"},{"w":"restrict","he":"להגביל"},{"w":"inhale","he":"לשאוף (לריאות)"},{"w":"exposure","he":"חשיפה"},{"w":"odor","he":"ריח (לרוב לא נעים)"},{"w":"precaution","he":"אמצעי זהירות"},{"w":"sufficient","he":"מספיק"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, להבין, לראות כ-"}]},{"id":"m22","lvl":"m","topic":"beauty","title":"How Korean Skincare Conquered the World","body":"Two decades ago, few shoppers outside East Asia could name a single Korean cosmetics brand. Today, sheet masks, snail-mucin serums and cushion compacts fill shelves from London to São Paulo, and South Korea has become one of the world's largest exporters of beauty products. This rise was not an accident but the result of culture, technology and a remarkably fast **commercial** system working together.\n\nThe cultural engine was the so-called Korean Wave. As K-dramas and K-pop spread across Asia and later the West, millions of fans became curious about the clear, glowing skin of their favorite stars. The South Korean government, recognizing the economic potential of this popularity, supported its creative industries, and beauty brands **exploited** the connection by featuring idols in their advertising campaigns. Skincare was thus sold not merely as a product but as part of an attractive **image** and lifestyle.\n\nEqually important was speed. Korean companies rely heavily on specialized contract **manufacturers** that develop formulas for many brands at once, so that a new idea can move from the laboratory to the store in a matter of months rather than years. This structure lets small start-ups **compete** with global giants and encourages constant **innovation**: whereas Western brands might **launch** a few products a year, Korean firms release new textures and ingredients almost continuously.\n\nThe famous \"ten-step routine\" illustrates both the appeal and the controversy of the industry. Although it was widely presented abroad as an ancient Korean tradition, many critics argue that it was largely a marketing concept, designed to encourage consumers to **purchase** more products. Dermatologists also warn that layering many active ingredients can irritate the skin.\n\nNevertheless, K-beauty has had a **profound** influence on how the world thinks about cosmetics. It shifted attention from covering flaws with makeup to caring for the skin itself, and its emphasis on sun protection and gentle cleansing has been **acclaimed** by many experts. Whether the trend will **sustain** its momentum remains uncertain, but its success shows how culture can create an industry.","gloss":[{"w":"commercial","he":"מסחרי"},{"w":"exploit","he":"לנצל"},{"w":"image","he":"תדמית"},{"w":"manufacturer","he":"יצרן"},{"w":"compete","he":"להתחרות"},{"w":"innovation","he":"חדשנות"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"purchase","he":"לרכוש, לקנות"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק, משמעותי"},{"w":"acclaim","he":"לשבח, להלל"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לשמר, לקיים לאורך זמן"}]},{"id":"m23","lvl":"m","topic":"beauty","title":"Why Hair Grows, Falls and Turns Grey","body":"The average human scalp holds roughly one hundred thousand hairs, and losing between fifty and a hundred of them every day is entirely normal. This constant shedding is in fact a sign of a healthy and highly organized biological cycle.\n\nEach hair grows from a follicle, a tiny organ beneath the skin that passes through three main phases. During the growth phase, which can last from two to seven years, cells at the base of the follicle divide rapidly and push the hair upward. A short transition phase follows, in which growth **ceases** and the follicle shrinks. Finally, in the resting phase, the old hair remains in place for about three months before it is pushed out by a new one. Because each follicle follows its own schedule, we never lose all our hair **simultaneously**.\n\nThis cycle explains a puzzling **phenomenon** that many people experience. After a high fever, childbirth or a period of severe stress, a large number of follicles may enter the resting phase at once. The resulting hair loss, however, appears only two or three months later, so that people rarely connect it to its real cause. Fortunately, this type of shedding is usually temporary, and the hair **gradually** recovers once the body is no longer under **strain**.\n\nGrey hair has a different origin. The color of hair comes from pigment cells that are supplied by a **reservoir** of stem cells within the follicle. With age, this reservoir becomes **exhausted**, and new hairs grow without pigment. Genetics largely **determines** when this happens, which is why some people find their first grey strands at twenty-five. A widely reported 2020 study in mice, conducted at Harvard, found that intense stress activated nerves that **depleted** these stem cells, offering **tentative** support for the popular belief that stress can turn hair grey.\n\nAlthough findings in mice do not **automatically** apply to humans, the science suggests that hair is far more than decoration: it is a sensitive record of what the body has recently **endured**.","gloss":[{"w":"cease","he":"להיפסק, לחדול"},{"w":"simultaneously","he":"בו-זמנית"},{"w":"phenomenon","he":"תופעה"},{"w":"gradually","he":"בהדרגה"},{"w":"strain","he":"לחץ, מאמץ"},{"w":"reservoir","he":"מאגר"},{"w":"exhaust","he":"לרוקן, למצות"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"},{"w":"deplete","he":"לדלדל, לרוקן"},{"w":"tentative","he":"זהיר, לא סופי"},{"w":"automatically","he":"באופן אוטומטי"},{"w":"endure","he":"לסבול, לשאת"}]},{"id":"m24","lvl":"m","topic":"beauty","title":"Less Is More: The Case for Skinimalism","body":"For much of the past decade, the beauty industry encouraged consumers to believe that more products meant better skin. Bathroom shelves filled with acid toners, grainy scrubs, peeling masks and retinoid serums, many of which promised to remove dead cells and reveal a brighter face underneath. Recently, however, a counter-trend known as \"skinimalism\" has emerged, and dermatologists have largely welcomed it.\n\nTo understand why, it helps to know how the skin protects itself. Its outermost **layer**, the skin barrier, is often compared to a brick wall: flattened dead cells act as the bricks, while fats such as ceramides function as the mortar that holds them together. This structure keeps water inside the body and **prevents** irritants and bacteria from getting in. Exfoliation removes some of the outer bricks, which can make the surface look smoother, but when it is done too often or too aggressively, it also strips away the mortar.\n\nThe consequences of over-exfoliation are surprisingly common. The skin may become red, tight and shiny, sting when ordinary creams are applied, or break out more than before. Ironically, many people **interpret** these symptoms as a sign that they need even stronger products, and so they **intensify** the very routine that caused the damage. A **damaged** barrier loses moisture more rapidly, which leaves the skin dry and **fragile** and makes it more **vulnerable** to sun damage and infection.\n\nSkinimalism proposes a simpler approach: a gentle cleanser, a moisturizer suited to one's skin type, and daily sunscreen, with active ingredients introduced slowly and used only when **necessary**. Rather than chasing perfection, the trend encourages people to accept visible pores and minor flaws as a natural part of having skin.\n\nCritics point out, somewhat **cynically**, that minimalism has itself become a marketing **strategy**, with brands selling \"essential\" kits that are anything but cheap. Nevertheless, the underlying message is supported by science. Healthy skin is not achieved by constant **intervention** but by allowing the barrier to do the job that it has been performing for millions of years.","gloss":[{"w":"layer","he":"שכבה"},{"w":"prevent","he":"למנוע"},{"w":"interpret","he":"לפרש"},{"w":"intensify","he":"להגביר, להעצים"},{"w":"damage","he":"לפגוע, להזיק"},{"w":"fragile","he":"שביר, עדין"},{"w":"vulnerable","he":"פגיע"},{"w":"necessary","he":"הכרחי, נחוץ"},{"w":"cynically","he":"בציניות"},{"w":"strategy","he":"אסטרטגיה"},{"w":"intervention","he":"התערבות"}]},{"id":"m25","lvl":"m","topic":"growth","title":"Feeling Like a Fraud","body":"In 1978, the psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes described a puzzling pattern among the successful women they had studied. Despite impressive degrees, promotions and professional **recognition**, many of these women were convinced that they were not truly intelligent and that their achievements were the result of luck or of having somehow fooled others. Sooner or later, they feared, someone would discover that they were a **fraud**. Clance and Imes called this experience the \"impostor phenomenon.\"\n\nAlthough the original research focused on high-achieving women, later studies found that men experience similar feelings, and estimates of how common the phenomenon is **vary** enormously, partly because there is no single agreed way to measure it. It is not a mental disorder but a pattern of thinking, and it seems especially **prevalent** among people who are the first in their family to attend university, members of **minority** groups, and anyone entering an environment where they feel they do not quite belong.\n\nResearchers have identified a typical cycle. Faced with a new task, the person feels **anxious** and either over-prepares obsessively or delays until the last moment. When the task succeeds, she does not **attribute** the success to her ability but to the enormous effort or to chance. The relief is therefore brief, and the next challenge **provokes** the same fear all over again.\n\nSome scholars argue that the concept has been misused. If a woman doubts herself in a workplace that repeatedly questions her competence, the problem may lie less in her mind than in her **surroundings**. Labelling such doubt as a personal \"syndrome,\" they suggest, can shift responsibility away from organizations that tolerate **bias**.\n\nNevertheless, recognizing the pattern can be useful. Talking openly about these feelings often reveals that colleagues share them, which **undermines** the belief that one is uniquely unqualified. Keeping a record of concrete achievements, and accepting that competent people also make mistakes, can gradually **reinforce** a more accurate self-image. Ironically, those who worry most about being impostors are frequently the people most **committed** to doing their work well.","gloss":[{"w":"recognition","he":"הכרה"},{"w":"fraud","he":"רמאי, נוכל"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות, לנוע בין"},{"w":"prevalent","he":"נפוץ, רווח"},{"w":"minority","he":"מיעוט"},{"w":"anxious","he":"חרד, מודאג"},{"w":"attribute","he":"לייחס"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר, לגרום"},{"w":"surroundings","he":"סביבה"},{"w":"bias","he":"דעה קדומה, הטיה"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"committed","he":"מחויב, מסור"}]},{"id":"m26","lvl":"m","topic":"growth","title":"Why Being Kind to Yourself Works","body":"Many ambitious people share an unspoken belief: that harsh self-criticism is the price of success. If they stop scolding themselves after every mistake, they fear, they will become lazy and complacent. Over the past two decades, however, a growing body of research has challenged this assumption, suggesting that self-compassion may be a more effective **motive** for improvement than self-punishment.\n\nThe concept was developed largely by the psychologist Kristin Neff, who defines self-compassion as treating oneself with the same kindness one would offer a close friend. It **comprises** three elements. The first is self-kindness rather than harsh judgment. The second is a sense of common humanity, the recognition that failure and suffering are part of every life rather than a personal **deficiency**. The third is mindfulness, the ability to **acknowledge** painful feelings without either ignoring or exaggerating them.\n\nIt is important to **distinguish** self-compassion from self-esteem. Whereas self-esteem often depends on feeling better than others, and therefore tends to **collapse** after a failure, self-compassion does not require any comparison. It is available precisely when things go wrong, which is when people need it most.\n\nExperimental evidence supports this idea. In one well-known study, students who failed a difficult test and were then encouraged to respond to themselves with understanding spent more time preparing for a second attempt than students who were simply told how intelligent they were. Rather than reducing **motivation**, self-compassion appeared to make failure less threatening, so that the students could look at their mistakes **candidly** instead of avoiding them. Other studies have **associated** self-compassion with lower levels of anxiety and depression and with greater **resilience** after difficult life events.\n\nCritics caution that the research is still relatively young and that much of it **relies** on questionnaires, in which people describe themselves. Nevertheless, the core insight is hard to **dismiss**. People who forgive themselves are not necessarily lowering their standards; they may simply be removing the fear that prevents them from trying again.","gloss":[{"w":"motive","he":"מניע"},{"w":"comprise","he":"לכלול, להיות מורכב מ-"},{"w":"deficiency","he":"חיסרון, ליקוי"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להכיר ב-, להודות ב-"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין, להבדיל"},{"w":"collapse","he":"לקרוס"},{"w":"motivation","he":"מוטיבציה"},{"w":"candidly","he":"בכנות, בגילוי לב"},{"w":"associate","he":"לקשר"},{"w":"resilience","he":"חוסן"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך"},{"w":"dismiss","he":"לבטל, לדחות"}]},{"id":"m27","lvl":"m","topic":"growth","title":"The Twenties Are Their Own Life Stage","body":"A century ago, most young people in Western countries had settled into adult life by their early twenties. They had finished their education, found steady work, married and often become parents. Today, by contrast, many people in their twenties change jobs, cities and relationships repeatedly, and the traditional markers of adulthood arrive much later. In 2000, the American psychologist Jeffrey Jensen Arnett argued that this period should no longer be regarded as a delayed adolescence but as a **distinct** stage of life, which he called \"emerging adulthood.\"\n\nArnett identified several features of this stage. It is an age of identity exploration, in which young people try out different possibilities in love and work before making lasting commitments. It is also an age of **instability**, marked by frequent moves and changing plans, and of self-focus, because there are relatively few obligations to others. Many people in this period feel \"in-between,\" neither adolescents nor fully adults, yet they also report a strong sense of optimism about their **prospects**.\n\nSeveral social changes **contributed** to the emergence of this stage. The shift toward economies based on knowledge rather than **labor** has made higher education longer and more necessary, while reliable contraception and changing social norms have allowed people to **postpone** marriage and children. Neuroscience adds another dimension: the brain, particularly the regions involved in planning and controlling impulses, continues to **mature** until roughly the mid-twenties.\n\nThe theory has its critics. Some sociologists **object** that it describes mainly middle-class young people in wealthy countries, who can **afford** to experiment, whereas many of their peers must work full-time from an early age. Others warn that celebrating exploration may make real financial insecurity look like a lifestyle choice.\n\nEven so, the concept offers a useful reassurance. Feeling uncertain at twenty-three is not a sign of failure but a **normal** feature of a stage in which uncertainty is almost **inevitable**. Experiences such as long journeys, unconventional jobs and changing friendships are not detours from adult life but often the very process through which an adult **identity** is formed.","gloss":[{"w":"distinct","he":"נבדל, ייחודי"},{"w":"instability","he":"חוסר יציבות"},{"w":"prospect","he":"סיכוי, אפשרות לעתיד"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"labor","he":"עבודה (פיזית)"},{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות (במועד)"},{"w":"mature","he":"להתבגר"},{"w":"object","he":"להתנגד"},{"w":"afford","he":"להרשות לעצמו"},{"w":"normal","he":"רגיל, נורמלי"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"}]},{"id":"m28","lvl":"m","topic":"growth","title":"The Ordinary Magic of Bouncing Back","body":"For much of the twentieth century, psychologists assumed that a serious loss or a traumatic event would almost inevitably leave lasting psychological damage. People who seemed to recover quickly from the death of a loved one were sometimes viewed with suspicion, as though they were hiding their grief or **denying** their feelings. Research over the past few decades has **altered** this picture.\n\nThe psychologist George Bonanno, who has studied grief for many years, found that the most common response to loss is not long-term breakdown but **resilience**. Many bereaved people experience intense sadness at first, yet within months they are able to work, enjoy relationships and laugh again. Such people are not indifferent to their loss; rather, their distress, although genuine, does not **prevail** over their ability to function. Similarly, the developmental psychologist Ann Masten described resilience as \"ordinary magic,\" arguing that it **derives** not from rare personal qualities but from common human resources.\n\nWhat are these resources? Studies repeatedly point to supportive relationships as the most important factor. A single person who offers care and stability can make an enormous difference, particularly for children growing up in **adverse** conditions. Other factors include a sense of control over one's life, the ability to **regulate** emotions, and a flexible way of thinking that allows people to **adapt** their strategies when circumstances change.\n\nImportantly, resilience is not the same as **toughness** or the refusal to ask for help. People who recover well often lean on others, express their emotions openly and accept that some days will be harder than others. It is also not a fixed trait that one either has or lacks. Although genetic factors may play a role, resilience can be **strengthened** by building connections, maintaining healthy routines and learning from earlier difficulties.\n\nThis research carries a hopeful message. Setbacks, whether a failed business, a painful breakup or a **disrupted** plan, are an **inevitable** part of life. Yet most people possess a greater **capacity** to recover than they expect, especially when they are not facing their difficulties alone.","gloss":[{"w":"deny","he":"להכחיש"},{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"resilience","he":"חוסן"},{"w":"prevail","he":"לגבור, לשלוט"},{"w":"derive","he":"לנבוע, להפיק"},{"w":"adverse","he":"שלילי, קשה"},{"w":"regulate","he":"לווסת"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"toughness","he":"קשיחות"},{"w":"strengthen","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"disrupt","he":"לשבש"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"capacity","he":"יכולת"}]},{"id":"m29","lvl":"m","topic":"business","title":"Selling Yourself Without Selling Out","body":"For a small business owner, a social media profile has become something close to a shop window. A nail technician, a personal trainer or a baker may never pay for traditional advertising, yet her Instagram page will often be the first place potential clients **encounter** her work. As a result, many entrepreneurs now speak of building a \"personal brand,\" a consistent public **image** that connects their services to their personality and values.\n\nThe logic behind this strategy is straightforward. In crowded markets, where dozens of providers offer similar services, clients often choose not the cheapest or even the most skilled option but the person they feel they know and trust. Regular posts that show the process behind the work, share honest stories and respond warmly to comments can create a sense of familiarity that **formal** advertising rarely achieves. In this way, a small **entrepreneur** can compete with larger businesses that have far greater budgets.\n\nHowever, personal branding brings its own **dilemmas**. The first concerns **authenticity**. Followers quickly notice when posts feel **artificial** or overly polished, yet constant openness about one's private life can be exhausting and risky. Many experts therefore recommend sharing the personal side selectively, revealing enough to seem **genuine** while keeping clear boundaries.\n\nThe second dilemma concerns **consistency**. Algorithms tend to reward accounts that post frequently, which can turn marketing into an endless burden that competes with the actual work. Business owners who post every day may find that they spend more time filming their clients' nails than serving new ones. A realistic plan, such as a few high-quality posts a week, is generally more **sustainable** than a burst of activity followed by silence.\n\nFinally, a brand built on a single platform is **fragile**. Changes to an algorithm, or even a blocked account, can **eliminate** a business's visibility overnight. For this reason, successful owners also collect clients' contact details, encourage reviews and rely on recommendations. Social media, in short, can **attract** attention, but loyalty is still earned through the quality of the service itself.","gloss":[{"w":"encounter","he":"להיתקל, לפגוש"},{"w":"image","he":"תדמית"},{"w":"formal","he":"רשמי"},{"w":"entrepreneur","he":"יזם"},{"w":"dilemma","he":"דילמה"},{"w":"authenticity","he":"אותנטיות, אמינות"},{"w":"artificial","he":"מלאכותי"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, כן"},{"w":"consistency","he":"עקביות"},{"w":"sustainable","he":"בר-קיימא, שאפשר להתמיד בו"},{"w":"fragile","he":"שביר"},{"w":"eliminate","he":"לחסל, להעלים"},{"w":"attract","he":"למשוך"}]},{"id":"m30","lvl":"m","topic":"business","title":"Why Your Regulars Matter Most","body":"When small businesses think about growth, they usually think about attracting new customers. Advertising campaigns, introductory discounts and special offers for first-time visitors all reflect this assumption. Yet a large body of business research suggests that the most valuable customers are not the ones a company is trying to **acquire** but the ones it already has.\n\nThe economic argument was popularized in the 1990s by Frederick Reichheld of the consulting firm Bain & Company, whose analysis suggested that even a small increase in customer retention could raise profits **substantially**. The reasons are not difficult to understand. Winning a new customer requires advertising, time and often discounts, whereas a returning customer costs almost nothing to **retain**. Loyal clients also tend to spend more over time, as they come to trust the business and try additional services. Perhaps most importantly, satisfied regulars recommend the business to friends, providing a form of advertising that is both free and highly **persuasive**.\n\nFor service businesses such as salons, the benefits go beyond money. A technician who sees the same client every three weeks learns her preferences, her schedule and the condition of her nails, which makes each appointment more **efficient** and pleasant. A **predictable** base of regular bookings also brings **stability**, reducing the anxiety of empty days and allowing the owner to plan ahead.\n\nHow, then, is loyalty built? Loyalty programs, such as a free treatment after every tenth visit, can help, but researchers caution that rewards alone rarely create genuine **attachment**. What matters more is the overall experience: **consistent** quality, punctuality, a warm welcome, and the feeling of being remembered. Small gestures, such as recalling a client's recent trip or sending a message on her birthday, often leave a stronger **impression** than discounts.\n\nNone of this means that new customers are unimportant, since every regular was once a newcomer. The lesson is rather one of balance. A business that constantly chases strangers while **neglecting** the people already sitting in its chairs may find that it is filling a bucket full of holes.","gloss":[{"w":"acquire","he":"לרכוש, להשיג"},{"w":"substantially","he":"באופן ניכר"},{"w":"retain","he":"לשמר, להחזיק"},{"w":"persuasive","he":"משכנע"},{"w":"efficient","he":"יעיל"},{"w":"predictable","he":"צפוי"},{"w":"stability","he":"יציבות"},{"w":"attachment","he":"היקשרות"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"impression","he":"רושם"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"}]},{"id":"m31","lvl":"m","topic":"business","title":"The Freelancer's Trap","body":"Freelancing promises freedom. Without a boss or fixed hours, independent workers can choose their clients, set their own prices and arrange their schedules around their lives rather than the other way around. Yet many freelancers, from designers to hairdressers, soon discover a hidden **constraint**: because they are paid for the hours they work, their income can never **exceed** the number of hours they are physically able to work.\n\nThis is the essence of what is often called the freelancer's dilemma. An employee who takes a week of vacation continues to receive a salary, whereas a freelancer who stops working stops earning. Illness, a family emergency or simple exhaustion therefore carries a direct financial **penalty**. Moreover, not all working hours are paid. Answering messages, keeping accounts, buying supplies and marketing the business consume a substantial part of the week, yet clients pay only for the service itself.\n\nThe most obvious solution is to raise prices, but many freelancers are **reluctant** to do so, fearing that they will lose clients to cheaper competitors. As a result, they often try to increase their income by working longer hours instead, a strategy that may succeed for a while but frequently leads to **burnout**. Experts argue that a modest price increase, combined with **superior** service, usually loses fewer clients than people expect.\n\nOther strategies **attempt** to break the link between time and money altogether. A freelancer might create products that can be sold repeatedly, such as online courses, digital guides or her own line of products. Others **expand** by training assistants, so that the business earns money even when the owner is not personally working. Each approach, however, requires an initial **investment** of time and the courage to take risks, which is precisely what an overworked freelancer **lacks**.\n\nUltimately, the freelancer's dilemma is not only financial but also **psychological**. Escaping it requires her to think of herself not merely as a worker selling hours but as the owner of a business whose value can **grow** beyond her own two hands.","gloss":[{"w":"constraint","he":"מגבלה, אילוץ"},{"w":"exceed","he":"לעלות על, לחרוג"},{"w":"penalty","he":"עונש, קנס"},{"w":"reluctant","he":"מסויג, לא ששה"},{"w":"burnout","he":"שחיקה"},{"w":"superior","he":"עדיף, מעולה"},{"w":"attempt","he":"לנסות"},{"w":"expand","he":"להתרחב"},{"w":"investment","he":"השקעה"},{"w":"lack","he":"לחסור, להיעדר"},{"w":"psychological","he":"פסיכולוגי"},{"w":"grow","he":"לגדול"}]},{"id":"m32","lvl":"m","topic":"business","title":"Why Women Founders Get Less Money","body":"Women start businesses at remarkable rates, yet when it comes to raising money from investors, they face an **obstacle** that has proved surprisingly persistent. In the United States, companies founded only by women have for years received roughly two percent of the venture capital invested annually, a figure that has barely changed despite growing public attention to the problem.\n\nSeveral explanations have been proposed. One concerns networks: venture capital remains a field dominated by men, and investors, like everyone else, tend to trust people who resemble them and who are introduced through familiar **connections**. Another concerns the industries in which women often start businesses, such as beauty, fashion or childcare, which some investors **underestimate** as small or unprofitable markets.\n\nResearch also points to more subtle forms of **bias**. One study of a startup competition found that investors tended to ask male entrepreneurs questions about potential gains, such as how they planned to grow, while asking female entrepreneurs questions about potential losses, such as how they would **avoid** failure. Founders who answered questions focused on risk went on to raise considerably less money, which suggests that the conversation itself may shape the outcome rather than merely **reflect** the quality of the business.\n\nParadoxically, the evidence on performance does not **justify** this gap. A widely cited analysis by the Boston Consulting Group reported that startups founded or co-founded by women generated more revenue for every dollar invested than those founded by men. Although such studies have **limitations**, they **imply** that investors may be overlooking profitable opportunities.\n\nVarious efforts now attempt to close the gap, from funds that **specifically** invest in women to training programs that prepare founders for investor meetings. Some experts argue, however, that the most effective change would be to **diversify** the investors themselves, since a more varied group of decision-makers is more likely to recognize promising ideas from unexpected sources. Until that happens, many women will continue to build their businesses slowly, relying on personal savings and their own **revenue** rather than outside capital.","gloss":[{"w":"obstacle","he":"מכשול"},{"w":"connection","he":"קשר, היכרות"},{"w":"underestimate","he":"להמעיט בערך"},{"w":"bias","he":"הטיה, דעה קדומה"},{"w":"avoid","he":"להימנע"},{"w":"reflect","he":"לשקף"},{"w":"justify","he":"להצדיק"},{"w":"limitation","he":"מגבלה"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז"},{"w":"specifically","he":"במיוחד, במפורש"},{"w":"diversify","he":"לגוון"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסות"}]},{"id":"m33","lvl":"m","topic":"mind","title":"Why Photos Can Erase Our Memories","body":"Anyone who has travelled recently knows the scene: crowds gathered before a famous temple or sunset, each person holding up a phone to capture the moment. The assumption behind this habit seems obvious. By taking pictures, we hope to preserve experiences that would otherwise fade. Yet psychological research suggests that the camera may sometimes have the opposite effect, weakening the very memories it is meant to **protect**.\n\nIn a well-known 2014 experiment, the psychologist Linda Henkel led students on a tour of an art museum and asked them to photograph some objects while simply observing others. The next day, the students remembered fewer of the photographed objects and fewer details about them. Henkel called this the \"photo-taking-impairment effect.\" Interestingly, when students zoomed in to photograph a specific detail of an object, their memory for it did not **decline**, suggesting that the problem is not the camera itself but the way it is used.\n\nResearchers have proposed several explanations. One is cognitive offloading: when we know that a device will **preserve** information, the brain may invest less effort in storing it, much as people forget phone numbers saved in their contacts. Another explanation concerns attention. The act of framing a shot, checking the screen and adjusting the angle can **distract** us from what is happening, so that we observe the scene through the device rather than **experiencing** it directly.\n\nThe picture is not entirely **negative**, however. Some later studies found that people who took photos remembered the visual aspects of an experience well, even if they recalled less of what they heard. Moreover, looking through photographs afterward can **trigger** memories and help people **recall** events in greater detail.\n\nThe **implication** is not that travelers should leave their phones at home but that they might use them more **deliberately**. Taking a few thoughtful pictures, and then putting the camera away to look, listen and notice, may produce both better photographs and richer memories. After all, the purpose of a journey is to **absorb** it, not merely to document it.","gloss":[{"w":"protect","he":"להגן"},{"w":"decline","he":"לרדת, לפחות"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"},{"w":"distract","he":"להסיח את הדעת"},{"w":"experience","he":"לחוות"},{"w":"negative","he":"שלילי"},{"w":"trigger","he":"לעורר, להפעיל"},{"w":"recall","he":"להיזכר"},{"w":"implication","he":"השלכה, משמעות"},{"w":"deliberately","he":"במכוון, בשיקול דעת"},{"w":"absorb","he":"לספוג, לקלוט"}]},{"id":"m34","lvl":"m","topic":"mind","title":"The Pill That Works Without Medicine","body":"For centuries, doctors have known that patients sometimes improve after receiving treatments that contain no active ingredient. A sugar pill, an injection of salt water or even a fake operation can reduce pain and other symptoms in many people. This phenomenon, known as the placebo effect, was long regarded as a nuisance that **interfered** with medical research. Today, however, it is increasingly studied as a window into the connection between mind and body.\n\nPlacebos do not cure diseases such as infections or tumors, but they can **alleviate** symptoms that the brain helps to produce, such as pain, nausea and fatigue. Researchers believe that two main mechanisms are involved. The first is expectation: when a person believes that a treatment will help, the brain may release natural painkillers and other chemicals that genuinely reduce discomfort. The second is conditioning, in which the body learns to **associate** the ritual of treatment, such as swallowing a pill, with relief.\n\nThe most surprising discovery concerns so-called open-label placebos. In a 2010 study led by Ted Kaptchuk at Harvard, patients with irritable bowel syndrome were given pills that were openly described as containing no medicine. Despite knowing this, many reported a **substantial** improvement in their symptoms compared with patients who received no pills at all. Similar results have since been reported for back pain, although the studies have been small and the findings remain **tentative**.\n\nWhy would a placebo work when patients know it is fake? Some researchers **suggest** that the ritual itself, along with a caring doctor and a clear explanation, activates expectations that operate below conscious **awareness**. Others argue that the attention and **empathy** patients receive during the study play a major role.\n\nThese findings raise ethical questions, since traditional placebo use required **deceiving** patients. Open-label placebos might **resolve** this problem, allowing doctors to harness the body's own capacity to heal without lying. They also remind us that the **context** of treatment, including trust and kindness, is not a mere **supplement** to medicine but part of its effect.","gloss":[{"w":"interfere","he":"להפריע"},{"w":"alleviate","he":"להקל"},{"w":"associate","he":"לקשר"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"tentative","he":"זמני, לא סופי"},{"w":"suggest","he":"להציע, לרמוז"},{"w":"awareness","he":"מודעות"},{"w":"empathy","he":"אמפתיה"},{"w":"deceive","he":"לרמות, להונות"},{"w":"resolve","he":"לפתור"},{"w":"context","he":"הקשר"},{"w":"supplement","he":"תוסף, השלמה"}]},{"id":"m35","lvl":"m","topic":"mind","title":"What Meditation Can and Cannot Do","body":"Over the past two decades, mindfulness meditation has moved from Buddhist monasteries and Himalayan retreats into offices, schools and smartphone apps. Its supporters claim that a few minutes of daily practice can reduce stress, improve concentration, strengthen the immune system and even reshape the brain. Scientists, however, have taken a more **cautious** view, and their findings offer a more nuanced picture.\n\nThe evidence is strongest for mental health. A large review published in 2014 in the journal JAMA Internal Medicine **evaluated** dozens of trials and concluded that meditation programs produced moderate improvements in anxiety, depression and pain. These effects were roughly **comparable** to those of other active treatments, such as exercise or therapy, but not clearly superior to them. Mindfulness-based therapy has also been found to help prevent relapse in people who have **suffered** repeated episodes of depression.\n\nFor other claims, the evidence is weaker. Studies reporting changes in brain structure have often been small, and some of their results have proven difficult to **replicate**. Similarly, although many people report that meditation helps them concentrate, research on attention and memory has produced **inconsistent** results. Critics also point out that much early research lacked proper control groups, making it hard to **distinguish** the effects of meditation from those of relaxation, expectation or simply spending time on oneself.\n\nMoreover, meditation is not **universally** pleasant. A minority of practitioners, particularly during intensive retreats, report unexpected difficulties such as anxiety, disturbing memories or a sense of **detachment** from themselves. Such experiences are relatively rare, but researchers argue that teachers should **acknowledge** them rather than presenting meditation as risk-free.\n\nNone of this means that meditation is useless. For many people, it offers a valuable tool for noticing thoughts without being **overwhelmed** by them. The lesson of the research is rather that mindfulness should be regarded neither as a miracle cure nor as a passing fashion, but as one useful practice among many, whose benefits depend on the person, the method and the **commitment** involved.","gloss":[{"w":"cautious","he":"זהיר"},{"w":"evaluate","he":"להעריך"},{"w":"comparable","he":"דומה, בר-השוואה"},{"w":"suffer","he":"לסבול"},{"w":"replicate","he":"לשחזר"},{"w":"inconsistent","he":"לא עקבי"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין"},{"w":"universally","he":"באופן גורף, לכולם"},{"w":"detachment","he":"ניתוק"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להכיר ב-"},{"w":"overwhelm","he":"להציף, להכריע"},{"w":"commitment","he":"מחויבות"}]},{"id":"m36","lvl":"m","topic":"mind","title":"Your Gut May Be Talking to Your Brain","body":"The human digestive system is home to trillions of bacteria, fungi and other microorganisms, collectively known as the gut microbiome. For a long time, scientists **regarded** these microbes mainly as helpers in digestion. In recent years, however, researchers have begun to explore a far more surprising **hypothesis**: that these tiny inhabitants may also influence how we think and feel.\n\nThe connection between gut and brain is not entirely new. Most people have felt \"butterflies\" in their stomach before an exam, and the gut is linked to the brain through the vagus nerve, a long pathway that carries signals in both directions. Gut bacteria also produce chemicals that can **affect** the nervous system, including substances involved in mood. It is often noted that most of the body's serotonin is produced in the gut, although this serotonin does not **directly** reach the brain.\n\nMuch of the early evidence came from animals. Mice raised without any gut bacteria behave differently from ordinary mice, and in some experiments, transferring bacteria from depressed humans into rodents produced behavior resembling **depression**. In people, a large Belgian study found that certain types of bacteria were less common in individuals with depression, even after **accounting** for antidepressant use.\n\nDespite these intriguing results, scientists urge **caution**. Most human studies show correlation rather than cause: depression may alter the microbiome, perhaps through changes in diet and sleep, rather than the other way around. Trials of probiotics, sometimes called \"psychobiotics\" when used for mental health, have so far produced modest and **inconsistent** benefits, and the companies selling them often **exaggerate** the science.\n\nWhat, then, can people reasonably conclude? Diets rich in fiber, vegetables and fermented foods appear to **support** a diverse microbiome, and some studies have linked such diets to better mood, although other lifestyle factors may **contribute**. A balanced diet is unlikely to **substitute** for therapy or medication, but the research suggests that mental health may depend not only on the brain but on the entire body, including its smallest and most **numerous** residents.","gloss":[{"w":"regard","he":"להתייחס, לראות כ-"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"},{"w":"affect","he":"להשפיע"},{"w":"directly","he":"ישירות"},{"w":"depression","he":"דיכאון"},{"w":"account for","he":"להביא בחשבון"},{"w":"caution","he":"זהירות"},{"w":"inconsistent","he":"לא עקבי"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"support","he":"לתמוך"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"substitute","he":"להחליף, לבוא במקום"},{"w":"numerous","he":"רבים"}]},{"id":"m37","lvl":"m","topic":"world","title":"The True Price of a Cheap T-Shirt","body":"Few purchases feel as harmless as a cheap T-shirt. Yet the business model known as fast fashion, in which retailers copy runway trends and deliver new collections to stores within weeks, has turned clothing into one of the most environmentally costly products that people buy. As prices have fallen, consumption has risen dramatically: according to one widely cited estimate, global clothing production roughly doubled in the first fifteen years of this century, while the average garment was worn far fewer times before being thrown away.\n\nThe environmental damage begins long before a garment reaches the store. Growing conventional cotton requires enormous quantities of water and often heavy use of **pesticides**, and one frequently quoted figure suggests that producing a single cotton T-shirt can **consume** more than two thousand liters of water. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester avoid this problem but are made from oil, and every wash releases tiny plastic fibers that eventually **contaminate** rivers and oceans. Dyeing and finishing textiles, meanwhile, can **pollute** waterways near factories in countries where environmental rules are weakly **enforced**.\n\nThe problem continues after clothes are discarded. Only a small **fraction** of textiles is recycled into new clothing, partly because most garments **combine** several materials that are difficult to separate. Huge **quantities** of unwanted clothing are shipped to countries such as Ghana and Chile, where much of it ends up in landfills, open dumps or even in the desert.\n\nResponsibility for this situation is **disputed**. Brands increasingly promote \"sustainable\" collections, but critics argue that such initiatives are often marketing rather than genuine change, since the business model still **relies** on selling ever greater volumes. Consumers, for their part, may express concern about the environment while continuing to buy cheaply and often.\n\nPossible solutions range from stricter **regulation** of the industry to a cultural shift in how clothes are valued. Buying fewer, better-made items, repairing them, and choosing second-hand shops are small steps, yet they **challenge** the central assumption of fast fashion: that clothing is a **disposable** product rather than something worth keeping.","gloss":[{"w":"pesticide","he":"חומר הדברה"},{"w":"consume","he":"לצרוך"},{"w":"contaminate","he":"לזהם (בחומר מזיק)"},{"w":"pollute","he":"לזהם"},{"w":"enforce","he":"לאכוף"},{"w":"fraction","he":"חלק קטן, שבר"},{"w":"combine","he":"לשלב"},{"w":"quantity","he":"כמות"},{"w":"dispute","he":"להתווכח על, לחלוק על"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך"},{"w":"regulation","he":"רגולציה, פיקוח"},{"w":"challenge","he":"לערער על, לאתגר"},{"w":"disposable","he":"חד-פעמי"}]},{"id":"m38","lvl":"m","topic":"world","title":"What Ikigai Really Means","body":"Recently, the Japanese word ikigai has become a popular term in self-help books, business workshops and social media posts. It is usually presented through a neat diagram of four overlapping circles: what you love, what you are good at, what the world needs, and what you can be paid for. Where all four **overlap** lies your ikigai, the perfect purpose that makes life worth living. There is only one problem: this is not what most Japanese people **mean** by the word.\n\nThe four-circle diagram was not invented in Japan. It originally described the idea of \"purpose\" in Western career literature, and the Japanese word was attached to it in a 2014 blog post, after which the combination spread rapidly online. In this form, ikigai became linked with career success, an interpretation that **reflects** Western concerns more than Japanese ones.\n\nIn Japanese, the word is far more modest. It combines *iki*, meaning life, and *kai*, roughly meaning worth or value, and it can refer to almost anything that gives a person a sense of meaning: a morning walk, caring for grandchildren, a garden, a hobby or a **routine** shared with friends. The psychiatrist Mieko Kamiya, who studied the concept, emphasized that ikigai does not have to be connected to work or money at all, and that people can have several sources of it **simultaneously**.\n\nThe **misinterpretation** matters because it changes the message. The Western version suggests that meaning is a rare goal to be **attained** through careful planning, whereas the Japanese concept implies that it is often found in small, everyday pleasures. The first can create **pressure** to find a single perfect calling; the second offers a more **accessible** kind of contentment.\n\nIkigai is also frequently linked to the remarkable **longevity** of the people of Okinawa. Although a sense of purpose may contribute to health, such claims are difficult to prove, since diet, community and **heredity** also play a role. Perhaps the true lesson of ikigai is simply that a meaningful life need not be **extraordinary**.","gloss":[{"w":"overlap","he":"לחפוף"},{"w":"mean","he":"להתכוון, להיות פירושו"},{"w":"reflect","he":"לשקף"},{"w":"routine","he":"שגרה"},{"w":"simultaneously","he":"בו-זמנית"},{"w":"misinterpretation","he":"פרשנות שגויה"},{"w":"attain","he":"להשיג"},{"w":"pressure","he":"לחץ"},{"w":"accessible","he":"נגיש"},{"w":"longevity","he":"אריכות ימים"},{"w":"heredity","he":"תורשה"},{"w":"extraordinary","he":"יוצא דופן"}]},{"id":"m39","lvl":"m","topic":"world","title":"Can a Country Measure Happiness?","body":"Most governments judge their success by economic growth, measured as gross domestic product, or GDP. The small Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan, squeezed between India and China, has famously chosen a different **criterion**. In the 1970s, its young king, Jigme Singye Wangchuck, is reported to have declared that Gross National Happiness was more important than Gross National Product. What began as a memorable phrase gradually became an official development **philosophy**.\n\nThe idea is not that the government can make citizens happy but that it should create conditions in which well-being can **flourish**. Bhutan's constitution, adopted in 2008, directs the state to promote Gross National Happiness, and the country developed an index that **assesses** well-being across nine areas, including health, education, psychological well-being, community life, cultural **diversity** and ecological resilience. New policies are examined for their likely effects on these areas rather than on income alone.\n\nThe results are visible in several ways. Bhutan has **preserved** much of its forest cover, and it is often described as carbon negative, absorbing more carbon dioxide than it emits. Tourism has been deliberately **restricted** through a daily fee charged to most foreign visitors, so that the country avoids the crowds that have transformed parts of neighboring Nepal.\n\nYet the model has been sharply criticized. In the late 1980s and 1990s, policies emphasizing a single national identity contributed to the departure of tens of thousands of Nepali-speaking Lhotshampa, many of whom spent years in refugee camps in Nepal. More recently, high youth **unemployment** has driven large numbers of educated young Bhutanese to **emigrate**, particularly to Australia, raising questions about whether happiness can be **sustained** without economic opportunity.\n\nWhether Bhutan has succeeded is therefore **debatable**. Nevertheless, its experiment has influenced international discussion, and the United Nations has since encouraged countries to consider well-being in their policies. Bhutan's greatest contribution may be less a perfect model than a persistent question: if economic growth does not make people happier, why should it remain the **primary** measure of progress?","gloss":[{"w":"criterion","he":"קריטריון, אמת מידה"},{"w":"philosophy","he":"פילוסופיה, השקפה"},{"w":"flourish","he":"לשגשג"},{"w":"assess","he":"להעריך"},{"w":"diversity","he":"מגוון"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"},{"w":"restrict","he":"להגביל"},{"w":"unemployment","he":"אבטלה"},{"w":"emigrate","he":"להגר"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים לאורך זמן"},{"w":"debatable","he":"שנוי במחלוקת"},{"w":"primary","he":"עיקרי, ראשי"}]},{"id":"m40","lvl":"m","topic":"world","title":"The Coffee Houses That Changed History","body":"Today, a café is a place to work or meet a friend. In earlier centuries, however, coffee houses were far more than places to drink. In cities from Istanbul to London to Paris, they became centers of conversation, business and political debate, and those in power often regarded them with deep **suspicion**.\n\nCoffee drinking spread from Yemen through the Islamic world in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, and coffee houses soon appeared in Mecca, Cairo and Istanbul. They attracted men from different social classes, who gathered to listen to storytellers, play games and discuss the news. Authorities feared that such gatherings encouraged gossip and criticism of the government, and on several occasions coffee houses were **prohibited**. In the seventeenth century, the Ottoman sultan Murad IV ordered them closed, although the **ban** proved impossible to **enforce** for long.\n\nWhen coffee reached England in the mid-seventeenth century, coffee houses spread rapidly, and they were nicknamed \"penny universities,\" since for the price of a cup anyone could join conversations with merchants, scientists and writers. Newspapers were read aloud, and **commercial** deals were made at the tables. Lloyd's of London, a famous insurance market, **originated** in a coffee house where shipowners and merchants met to share information. King Charles II, worried about political plotting, attempted to **suppress** coffee houses in 1675, but public **opposition** was so strong that the order was withdrawn within days.\n\nIn Paris, cafés played a similar role. On a July day in 1789, the journalist Camille Desmoulins is said to have called a crowd to arms outside the Café de Foy, shortly before the storming of the Bastille, one of the opening events of the French Revolution.\n\nSome historians, most famously the German philosopher Jürgen Habermas, have argued that coffee houses helped create a \"public sphere,\" a space where private individuals could **debate** public affairs as equals. Although this **notion** has been criticized for **idealizing** spaces that largely **excluded** women, it **illustrates** why rulers once feared a drink that kept people awake and talking.","gloss":[{"w":"suspicion","he":"חשד"},{"w":"prohibit","he":"לאסור"},{"w":"ban","he":"איסור"},{"w":"enforce","he":"לאכוף"},{"w":"commercial","he":"מסחרי"},{"w":"originate","he":"להתחיל, לנבוע מ-"},{"w":"suppress","he":"לדכא"},{"w":"opposition","he":"התנגדות"},{"w":"debate","he":"להתווכח, לדון"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"idealize","he":"להאדיר, לייפות"},{"w":"exclude","he":"להוציא מהכלל, להדיר"},{"w":"illustrate","he":"להמחיש"}]},{"id":"h01","lvl":"h","topic":"travel","title":"Loved to Death: The Paradox of Overtourism","body":"For decades, governments across Southeast Asia regarded tourism as an unambiguous blessing, a **lucrative** industry capable of lifting remote villages out of **poverty**. Yet in places such as Bali and the Thai island of Koh Phi Phi Leh the very success of that strategy has begun to **undermine** the attractions on which it depends. The phenomenon, now widely labelled overtourism, describes a situation in which the number of visitors **exceeds** what a destination can absorb without lasting damage to its environment or the daily lives of its residents.\n\nPerhaps the most **conspicuous** example is Maya Bay, the small cove made famous by the 2000 film The Beach. At its peak, the bay received several thousand visitors a day, and speedboats crowded in so densely that their engines and anchors **devastated** the coral reef beneath them. In 2018 Thai authorities took the **unprecedented** step of closing the bay entirely. Only after more than three years of rest, during which coral was replanted and reef sharks returned, was it reopened, and then under strict rules: boats must now dock on the far side of the island, swimming is **prohibited**, and visits are limited to short, scheduled slots.\n\nBali illustrates a subtler version of the problem. Ostensibly, the island has never been more prosperous, yet the rapid construction of villas and beach clubs has placed enormous pressure on its water supply, much of which is diverted from the rice terraces that **sustain** local agriculture. Traffic, waste and rising land prices have further **alienated** some Balinese residents, who increasingly complain that their island is being transformed into a stage set for foreigners. In 2024 the provincial government introduced a levy on foreign tourists, partly to fund cultural and environmental protection.\n\nThe paradox at the heart of overtourism is that each individual traveller seeks an authentic, unspoiled experience, while travellers collectively bring about the **erosion** of precisely that quality. Critics argue that measures such as entry fees and quotas merely **restrict** travel to the **affluent**, converting natural wonders into luxury goods. Proponents respond that, unless access is regulated, there will soon be nothing left worth visiting. Neither position is entirely convincing on its own; what seems clear is that the **notion** of tourism as limitless growth has become difficult to defend.\n\nFor travellers, the lesson is arguably less about guilt than awareness: visiting outside peak season, staying longer in fewer places and spending money in locally owned businesses can **mitigate**, though not eliminate, the strain that their presence imposes.","gloss":[{"w":"lucrative","he":"רווחי, משתלם"},{"w":"poverty","he":"עוני"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער, לחתור תחת"},{"w":"exceed","he":"לעלות על, לחרוג"},{"w":"conspicuous","he":"בולט, ניכר"},{"w":"devastate","he":"להרוס, להחריב"},{"w":"unprecedented","he":"חסר תקדים"},{"w":"prohibit","he":"לאסור"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים, לכלכל"},{"w":"alienate","he":"להרחיק, לגרום לניכור"},{"w":"erosion","he":"שחיקה, סחף"},{"w":"restrict","he":"להגביל"},{"w":"affluent","he":"עשיר, אמיד"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"mitigate","he":"למתן, להקל"}]},{"id":"h02","lvl":"h","topic":"travel","title":"Pics or It Didn't Happen: Travel as Identity","body":"Not long ago, a journey was recorded in a handful of photographs that were developed weeks later. Today the record is created **simultaneously** with the experience itself: a sunrise over the temples of Bagan is often framed, filtered and shared before it has fully ended. This shift has prompted psychologists to ask whether travel is still primarily about seeing the world, or whether it has become a means of constructing and displaying an **identity**.\n\nSome researchers argue that travel has always served a social function. Like clothing or music, destinations act as signals, communicating that a person is adventurous, open-minded or wealthy enough to spend months abroad. Social media has not invented this motive but has greatly amplified it, since a single post can reach hundreds of acquaintances at **virtually** no cost. In this sense, a carefully composed image of a solitary hike in Nepal is less a private memory than a public statement about who the traveller **aspires** to be.\n\nThe consequences for the experience itself are disputed. One line of research suggests that people who photograph events with the intention of sharing them enjoy those events less, apparently because they **perceive** the moment through the eyes of an imagined audience and become **anxious** about how it will be judged. Other studies, however, have found that taking photographs can increase engagement, directing attention towards details that would otherwise be **overlooked**. The crucial **distinction** seems to lie not in the camera but in the motive: photographing for oneself tends to deepen involvement, whereas photographing for others tends to **distract** from it.\n\nMemory, too, is affected. Laboratory studies of what has been called the photo-taking impairment effect indicate that people sometimes remember less about objects they photographed than about those they simply observed, as though the device had been entrusted with the task of remembering. Paradoxically, then, the traveller who documents everything may return with an extensive archive and a rather **superficial** recollection.\n\nNone of this implies that sharing travel photographs is **inherently** harmful. For many people posting is a way to remain connected with friends at home and to **reconcile** a sense of distance with a sense of belonging. The difficulty arises when the pursuit of **recognition** begins to **determine** the itinerary itself, when destinations are chosen for how they will look online rather than for what they offer. At that point, the journey risks becoming a performance, and the traveller a spectator of her own life.","gloss":[{"w":"simultaneously","he":"בו-זמנית"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"virtually","he":"כמעט לגמרי"},{"w":"aspire","he":"לשאוף"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לראות"},{"w":"anxious","he":"חרד, מודאג"},{"w":"overlook","he":"להחמיץ, להתעלם"},{"w":"distinction","he":"הבחנה, הבדל"},{"w":"distract","he":"להסיח את הדעת"},{"w":"superficial","he":"שטחי"},{"w":"inherently","he":"מטבעו, באופן מהותי"},{"w":"reconcile","he":"ליישב, לפשר"},{"w":"recognition","he":"הכרה"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"}]},{"id":"h03","lvl":"h","topic":"travel","title":"The Gap Year as a Modern Rite of Passage","body":"Anthropologists have long been fascinated by rites of passage, the ceremonies through which societies guide individuals from one social status to another. In 1909 the ethnographer Arnold van Gennep described such rites as unfolding in three stages: separation from the familiar community, a transitional period on the **margins**, and finally reintegration with a new status. Read through this lens, the gap year, in which young people **postpone** university or work in order to travel, looks less like an extended holiday than like a secular version of an ancient ritual.\n\nThe separation phase is clear enough: the traveller leaves home and **abandons** the routines and roles that have defined her. The middle phase, which the anthropologist Victor Turner called liminality, is arguably the most revealing. Liminal individuals are 'betwixt and between', no longer what they were and not yet what they will become. Backpackers on the long trail through Asia display many classic features of this state: they wear similar clothing, **disregard** ordinary markers of status, and form intense but temporary friendships with **strangers** who share the same condition.\n\nTurner also observed that liminal groups tend to develop what he called communitas, a sense of equality and **mutual** solidarity that would be unusual in everyday life. Hostels, overnight buses and trekking routes generate exactly this atmosphere. Status earned at home counts for little; what matters instead is **stamina**, resourcefulness and good stories. Not surprisingly, many travellers later describe these months as the period in which they felt most authentically themselves.\n\nCritics, however, caution against romanticising the practice. The gap year is far from universal; it presupposes savings, a passport that opens many borders and a family able to absorb the risk, so that it remains **predominantly** available to the youth of **wealthy** countries. Moreover, some observers argue that brief encounters with poorer societies can **reinforce** rather than challenge **prejudice**, turning local communities into a mere backdrop for the personal growth of visitors.\n\nThe final stage, reintegration, is often the most difficult and the least discussed. Traditional rites ended with a public ceremony that confirmed the initiate's new status, whereas returning travellers frequently find that nothing at home has changed except themselves. The resulting sense of dislocation suggests that the modern ritual is incomplete: it provides separation and transformation but offers no **conventional** way of coming back. Perhaps that is why so many returnees **linger** on the edges of their old lives, already planning the next departure.","gloss":[{"w":"margin","he":"שוליים"},{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש, לזנוח"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם"},{"w":"stranger","he":"זר"},{"w":"mutual","he":"הדדי"},{"w":"stamina","he":"כוח סבל, סיבולת"},{"w":"predominantly","he":"בעיקר, ברובו"},{"w":"wealthy","he":"עשיר"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"prejudice","he":"דעה קדומה"},{"w":"conventional","he":"מקובל, שגרתי"},{"w":"linger","he":"להתמהמה, להשתהות"}]},{"id":"h04","lvl":"h","topic":"travel","title":"Laptops in Paradise: The Economics of Nomad Hubs","body":"When the pandemic closed offices worldwide, millions of employees discovered that they could work from a kitchen table. A smaller but highly mobile group drew a more radical conclusion: if work could be done from home, it could be done from anywhere. Within a few years, dozens of countries, from Estonia to Thailand, had introduced or announced special visas designed to attract these so-called digital nomads, offering longer stays in return for proof of a foreign income.\n\nThe rationale for governments is straightforward. Nomads typically earn salaries set in high-income economies while spending them in places where living costs are a fraction as high, so that each arrival represents a steady injection of foreign **currency**. Unlike conventional tourists, they stay for months, rent apartments, eat in local restaurants, generating **revenue** for a wide range of small businesses. For an **entrepreneur** in Chiang Mai, a city that became one of the earliest nomad hubs, a café with reliable Wi-Fi can be a highly **lucrative** venture.\n\nYet the benefits are distributed unevenly. Canggu, once a quiet stretch of rice fields on Bali's southwest coast, has been transformed within little more than a decade into a dense strip of villas, smoothie bars and coworking spaces. Rents in such hubs tend to rise **rapidly**, since landlords can charge foreigners prices that local families cannot possibly match. The result, critics argue, is a form of **displacement** in which residents are gradually pushed out of neighbourhoods whose new economy they serve but in which they can no longer afford to live.\n\nThere are also questions of fairness and taxation. Many nomads **occupy** a legal grey zone, earning money in one country while residing in another and paying little tax in either. Whereas visa schemes **ostensibly** **regulate** this situation, they rarely require nomads to contribute to the public services on which they **rely**. Some economists therefore describe the arrangement as a subtle form of **exploitation**, in which wealth **accumulates** with mobile foreigners while the costs remain local.\n\nNot all assessments are so bleak. Nomads can bring skills, international networks and investment, and some host communities have begun to explore ways of channelling that spending towards local workers and businesses rather than towards foreign-owned property. Whether the model proves **sustainable** will depend less on the nomads themselves than on whether host countries can harness their spending, and protect the **domestic** housing market, without **surrendering** the very affordability and charm that drew the newcomers in the first place.","gloss":[{"w":"currency","he":"מטבע"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסה"},{"w":"entrepreneur","he":"יזם"},{"w":"lucrative","he":"רווחי"},{"w":"rapidly","he":"במהירות"},{"w":"displacement","he":"דחיקה, עקירה"},{"w":"occupy","he":"לתפוס, לאכלס"},{"w":"ostensibly","he":"לכאורה"},{"w":"regulate","he":"להסדיר, לפקח"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך, לסמוך"},{"w":"exploitation","he":"ניצול"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"sustainable","he":"בר-קיימא"},{"w":"domestic","he":"מקומי, פנימי"},{"w":"surrender","he":"לוותר, להיכנע"}]},{"id":"h05","lvl":"h","topic":"fitness","title":"The Exercise Paradox: Lessons from the Hadza","body":"It seems self-evident that the more a person moves, the more calories she burns. Fitness trackers, gym machines and diet plans are all built on this assumption, which treats the body as a simple machine whose fuel consumption rises in direct proportion to its workload. Yet research conducted among the Hadza, a community of hunter-gatherers in northern Tanzania, has cast considerable doubt on this intuitive model.\n\nThe Hadza walk long distances every day, the men tracking game and the women digging for tubers and gathering berries, and they are **substantially** more physically active than the average Western office worker. Researchers therefore expected their daily energy expenditure to be correspondingly high. When the evolutionary anthropologist Herman Pontzer and his colleagues measured it using a precise technique involving specially labelled water, however, they found that, once differences in body size were taken into **account**, the Hadza burned roughly the same number of calories per day as adults in the United States and Europe.\n\nTo explain this result, Pontzer proposed what he called the constrained model of energy expenditure. According to this **hypothesis**, the body operates within a relatively narrow energy budget. When physical activity increases, the body does not simply add the extra cost on top; instead, it appears to **compensate** by reducing the energy **devoted** to other, less visible processes, such as inflammation, stress responses and certain reproductive functions. Total expenditure therefore levels off at moderate to high levels of activity rather than continuing to rise.\n\nThe implications are **profound**, particularly for the widespread belief that exercise is an **efficient** tool for weight loss. If the body **adapts** to higher activity by economising elsewhere, then running an extra few kilometres a day may produce disappointingly little change in body weight over the long term. This may help explain why many people who **commence** an ambitious training programme lose less weight than they **anticipate**, and why diet appears to have the greater influence on body size.\n\nIt would be a serious misreading, however, to **infer** that exercise is pointless. On the contrary, the very mechanism that limits weight loss may explain why physical activity is so beneficial: by diverting energy away from chronic inflammation and excessive stress responses, exercise may protect against **ailments** such as heart disease and diabetes. The lesson of the Hadza, therefore, is not that people should move less, but that they should **regard** exercise primarily as a means of staying healthy rather than as a way of burning off yesterday's dessert.","gloss":[{"w":"substantially","he":"במידה ניכרת"},{"w":"account","he":"חשבון (take into account – להביא בחשבון)"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"},{"w":"compensate","he":"לפצות, לאזן"},{"w":"devote","he":"להקדיש"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק, מרחיק לכת"},{"w":"efficient","he":"יעיל"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"commence","he":"להתחיל"},{"w":"anticipate","he":"לצפות מראש"},{"w":"infer","he":"להסיק"},{"w":"ailment","he":"מחלה, מיחוש"},{"w":"regard","he":"להתייחס, לראות כ-"}]},{"id":"h06","lvl":"h","topic":"fitness","title":"When More Training Makes Athletes Worse","body":"In the culture of competitive sport, effort is rarely questioned. Coaches praise athletes who arrive first and leave last. Yet sports scientists have long recognised a condition in which persistent hard work produces the opposite of its intended effect. Overtraining syndrome, as it is known, describes a prolonged decline in performance that occurs when training stress **exceeds** the body's capacity to recover, and that does not resolve even after several weeks of rest.\n\nTo understand the syndrome, it helps to recall how training normally works. Exercise itself does not make the body stronger; it causes small amounts of damage and fatigue. Improvement occurs during the subsequent recovery period, when muscles, tendons and energy systems are rebuilt to **withstand** a slightly greater load. If a new bout of hard training begins before this process is complete, fatigue **accumulates**. A short period of such overload, followed by rest, can actually enhance performance. The danger arises when overload is **sustained** for too long, without sufficient sleep, nutrition or rest days.\n\nThe symptoms are notoriously difficult to **distinguish** from those of ordinary tiredness or illness, which is partly why the condition is so often overlooked. Affected athletes may experience persistent fatigue, disturbed sleep, frequent infections, as well as a **conspicuous** loss of motivation. Hormonal and immune changes have been reported, but no single blood test can reliably **detect** the syndrome, so diagnosis typically depends on excluding other causes. Researchers have also linked chronic underfuelling, in which athletes **consume** too little energy for their training load, to many of the same symptoms.\n\nPsychological factors **exacerbate** the problem. Athletes who are highly committed and **meticulous** tend to interpret a drop in performance as a sign that they need to work harder, and so respond by increasing rather than reducing their training. This creates a vicious circle in which every attempt to **overcome** the decline only deepens it. Recovery from the full syndrome can take months, and in severe cases careers have been **prematurely** ended.\n\nThe lesson extends well beyond elite sport. Recreational runners, gym enthusiasts and anyone pursuing an ambitious fitness goal can fall into the same trap, particularly when stress and poor sleep are added. Sports scientists increasingly argue that rest should be **regarded** not as a **reluctant** break from training but as an integral part of it. Paradoxically, the athletes most likely to **excel** over a long career are often not those who train the hardest, but those who know when to stop.","gloss":[{"w":"exceed","he":"לעלות על, לחרוג"},{"w":"withstand","he":"לעמוד בפני"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים לאורך זמן"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין"},{"w":"conspicuous","he":"בולט"},{"w":"detect","he":"לגלות, לזהות"},{"w":"consume","he":"לצרוך"},{"w":"exacerbate","he":"להחמיר"},{"w":"meticulous","he":"קפדני, דקדקני"},{"w":"overcome","he":"להתגבר"},{"w":"prematurely","he":"בטרם עת"},{"w":"regard","he":"להתייחס, לראות כ-"},{"w":"reluctant","he":"מסויג, שלא מרצון"},{"w":"excel","he":"להצטיין"}]},{"id":"h07","lvl":"h","topic":"fitness","title":"Selling the Perfect Body","body":"Scroll through any social media platform and it will not take long to encounter a fitness influencer: a sculpted figure demonstrating exercises in a sunlit gym, praising a protein powder, or offering a twelve-week programme that promises a dramatic transformation. Over the past decade, such creators have become highly **influential** figures in the fitness industry, and their content, often grouped under the label 'fitspiration', attracts audiences that dwarf those of traditional sports magazines.\n\nThe economics behind this phenomenon are considerable. Successful influencers earn **revenue** not only from advertising and sponsorship deals but also from selling their own products, including training apps, clothing lines and supplements. Since their income depends on engagement, they face a constant **incentive** to produce content that is visually striking and emotionally compelling. The most effective images, unsurprisingly, are those that display exceptionally lean and muscular bodies, often captured in flattering light, at carefully chosen angles and, in some cases, after digital editing.\n\nPsychologists have **scrutinised** the effects of this imagery, and the findings are largely discouraging. A number of experimental studies have found that brief exposure to fitspiration images can increase body dissatisfaction, particularly among young women, compared with exposure to neutral images. The mechanism appears to be social comparison: viewers **perceive** the idealised bodies as a realistic standard and evaluate themselves accordingly. Because the content is framed as health rather than beauty, it may be even more persuasive, since it **implies** that the ideal can be achieved by anyone willing to work hard enough.\n\nThis framing **conceals** an important fact. The physique displayed by many influencers reflects not only discipline but also genetics, professional photography and, sometimes, strategies that are **by no means** healthy, such as extreme dieting before a photo shoot. Some creators have begun to **acknowledge** this openly, posting side-by-side photographs that show how much posture and lighting can **alter** the appearance of the same body within seconds. Such posts have been widely praised, though sceptics note that they have themselves become a successful **commercial** format.\n\nNone of this means that fitness content is inherently harmful. Many people **derive** **genuine** motivation, practical knowledge and a sense of community from following trainers online. Researchers suggest that the impact depends heavily on what is emphasised: content focused on strength, function and enjoyment tends to be less damaging than content focused on appearance. For viewers, the most useful **precaution** may be a critical one, namely remembering that every image is a carefully **constructed** product designed, above all, to sell.","gloss":[{"w":"influential","he":"משפיע, בעל השפעה"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסה"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"scrutinize","he":"לבחון בקפידה"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לראות"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז"},{"w":"conceal","he":"להסתיר"},{"w":"by no means","he":"בשום אופן לא"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להודות, להכיר ב-"},{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"commercial","he":"מסחרי"},{"w":"derive","he":"להפיק, לשאוב"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, כן"},{"w":"precaution","he":"אמצעי זהירות"},{"w":"construct","he":"לבנות, להרכיב"}]},{"id":"h08","lvl":"h","topic":"fitness","title":"Why Gym Resolutions Collapse by February","body":"Every January, gyms across much of the world experience a predictable surge in membership. Crowds of newcomers fill the treadmills, armed with **ambitious** plans. By the middle of February, however, many of these new members have already stopped attending, and the gym regains its familiar rhythm. The pattern is so consistent that some fitness businesses reportedly depend on it, selling far more memberships than their facilities could **accommodate** if every customer actually came.\n\nMotivation researchers offer several explanations for this collapse. The first concerns the nature of the goals themselves. New Year's resolutions tend to be formulated in a burst of enthusiasm, at a moment when people imagine their future selves as more disciplined than their present ones. The resulting objectives are often vague, such as 'getting fit', or extreme, such as training six days a week after years of inactivity. Neither kind is easily **sustained** once the initial **zeal** has faded and the demands of ordinary life return.\n\nA second explanation **concerns** the type of motivation involved. Self-determination theory, a framework developed by the psychologists Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, distinguishes between extrinsic motivation, which **derives** from external pressures or rewards, and intrinsic motivation, which arises from **genuine** interest and enjoyment. Studies consistently suggest that people who exercise mainly because they feel they ought to, for instance out of guilt about their appearance, are less likely to persist than those who find the activity itself rewarding. A resolution born of January **remorse** is, in this sense, built on a **fragile** foundation.\n\nHabit research adds a third perspective. Behaviours become automatic only through repetition in a **consistent** context, and the process takes considerably longer than popular advice suggests; one frequently cited study found that forming a simple new habit took, on average, about two months, with enormous variation between individuals. Since most resolvers **abandon** their plans well before this point, exercise never acquires the effortless quality of a routine and continues to demand **deliberate** effort every single time.\n\nThe practical implications are **modest** but useful. Researchers recommend setting specific, realistic goals, attaching exercise to existing routines, and choosing activities that are enjoyable rather than merely virtuous. Equally important is the response to failure: people who regard a missed session as evidence of personal weakness are far more likely to give up entirely than those who simply resume the next day. Perhaps the most reliable predictor of success, then, is not the intensity of January's resolve but the ability to **tolerate** imperfection in March.","gloss":[{"w":"ambitious","he":"שאפתני"},{"w":"accommodate","he":"להכיל, לאכלס"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים, לשמר"},{"w":"zeal","he":"להט, התלהבות"},{"w":"concern","he":"לעסוק ב-, להתייחס ל-"},{"w":"derive","he":"לנבוע, להיגזר"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, כן"},{"w":"remorse","he":"חרטה"},{"w":"fragile","he":"שביר"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש, לזנוח"},{"w":"deliberate","he":"מכוון, מודע"},{"w":"modest","he":"צנוע"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"}]},{"id":"h09","lvl":"h","topic":"routine","title":"Is Willpower a Muscle? The Ego Depletion Debate","body":"In the late 1990s, a series of ingenious experiments appeared to reveal something fundamental about human self-control. In one well-known study led by the social psychologist Roy Baumeister, hungry participants were seated in front of freshly baked cookies; some were permitted to eat them, while others were instructed to eat radishes instead. Afterwards, all participants attempted to solve a puzzle that was impossible. Those who had resisted the cookies gave up considerably sooner than those who had not.\n\nBaumeister and his colleagues interpreted this result as evidence that self-control **relies** on a limited resource that can be exhausted, a state they called ego depletion. According to this model, willpower behaves like a muscle: it becomes fatigued after use but can be strengthened through regular practice. Over the following decade, hundreds of studies appeared to confirm the effect, and the concept spread rapidly into popular books and everyday conversation, offering a **plausible** explanation for why people who resist temptation all day often **succumb** to it in the evening.\n\nNot until the 2010s, however, did serious doubts begin to **emerge**. As psychology confronted what became known as the replication crisis, researchers realised that many celebrated findings had been produced under conditions that made false positives more likely: small samples, flexible analyses and a publication system that rewarded striking results while **neglecting** studies that found nothing. In 2016, a project in which more than twenty laboratories followed an identical protocol found an ego depletion effect so small that it was statistically **indistinguishable** from zero.\n\nThe **controversy** is far from **resolved**. Defenders of the theory argue that the replication used a weak manipulation that failed to deplete participants **adequately**, and subsequent large-scale attempts have produced **ambiguous** results. Meanwhile, alternative explanations have gained ground. One influential line of research suggests that what declines after effortful tasks is not a finite resource but motivation. Another finding indicates that the effect may depend partly on belief, since people who regard willpower as unlimited appear less **prone to** depletion.\n\nWhatever the eventual **verdict**, the debate offers a valuable lesson about how scientific knowledge is constructed. An idea that seemed **empirical** and well established was, in fact, built on a **tenuous** foundation, and its popularity outpaced its evidence. For anyone trying to change her own habits, the practical implication is arguably encouraging: rather than treating fatigue as proof that willpower has run out, it may be more useful to design routines that reduce the need for constant self-**restraint** in the first place.","gloss":[{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך, להישען"},{"w":"plausible","he":"סביר, מתקבל על הדעת"},{"w":"succumb","he":"להיכנע"},{"w":"emerge","he":"לצוץ, להופיע"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"indistinguishable","he":"שאי אפשר להבחין בינו לבין"},{"w":"controversy","he":"מחלוקת"},{"w":"resolve","he":"ליישב, לפתור"},{"w":"adequately","he":"באופן מספק"},{"w":"ambiguous","he":"דו-משמעי, לא חד"},{"w":"prone to","he":"נוטה ל-"},{"w":"verdict","he":"פסק דין, הכרעה"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי, מבוסס ניסוי"},{"w":"tenuous","he":"רעוע, קלוש"},{"w":"restraint","he":"ריסון, איפוק"}]},{"id":"h10","lvl":"h","topic":"routine","title":"The Cult of Hustle and Its Discontents","body":"Few ideas have spread through contemporary working life as rapidly as the gospel of hustle. On social media, entrepreneurs boast of waking at four in the morning, answering emails from the gym, treating weekends as a chance to outdo rivals. Productivity apps promise to optimise every minute, and an entire genre of books presents **relentless** work not merely as a route to success but as a moral virtue. In this worldview, rest is **tolerated** only insofar as it improves performance.\n\nCritics argue that this culture **distorts** both the nature of productivity and the nature of a good life. Research on working hours suggests that output per hour declines sharply once people work well beyond a standard week, so that the additional hours **yield** surprisingly little. One frequently cited analysis of munitions workers during the First World War found that those who worked about seventy hours a week produced barely more than those who worked fifty-five. Fatigue, meanwhile, increases errors and accidents, and chronic overwork has been linked to cardiovascular disease and depression.\n\nThere is also a social dimension that hustle rhetoric tends to **conceal**. The celebrated stories of founders who sacrificed sleep to build empires rarely mention the inherited **wealth**, domestic support or sheer luck that made such sacrifices possible. For workers in **precarious** employment, by contrast, long hours are not a lifestyle choice but a necessity. By framing exhaustion as a sign of ambition, hustle culture arguably transforms a structural problem into a question of individual character, implying that anyone who is struggling simply has not tried hard enough.\n\nNot all critics reject the value of hard work. Their objection is rather to the **notion** that every moment must be productive and that self-worth should be measured in output. Some philosophers point out that many of the most meaningful human activities, such as friendship, play and contemplation, are valuable precisely because they serve no further purpose. When these too are **evaluated** in terms of efficiency, the result is a strangely impoverished existence in which even **leisure** becomes a task to be completed.\n\nIn recent years, a backlash has gained momentum. Movements celebrating 'quiet quitting', four-day working weeks and **deliberate** rest have attracted considerable attention, and several trials of shorter working weeks have reported stable productivity alongside improvements in wellbeing. Whether these experiments will **alter** mainstream working culture remains uncertain. What they do suggest is that the **conventional** equation of longer hours with greater achievement deserves far more scrutiny than it usually receives.","gloss":[{"w":"relentless","he":"בלתי פוסק, בלתי מרפה"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להתיר"},{"w":"distort","he":"לעוות"},{"w":"yield","he":"להניב"},{"w":"conceal","he":"להסתיר"},{"w":"wealth","he":"עושר"},{"w":"precarious","he":"רעוע, לא יציב"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"evaluate","he":"להעריך"},{"w":"leisure","he":"פנאי"},{"w":"deliberate","he":"מכוון, מתוכנן"},{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"conventional","he":"מקובל, שגרתי"}]},{"id":"h11","lvl":"h","topic":"routine","title":"The Hidden Value of Being Bored","body":"Boredom has a poor reputation. It is associated with idle teenagers, unproductive afternoons, and modern life seems engineered to eliminate it entirely. Streaming services, social media feeds and endless notifications ensure that no moment of waiting need ever be empty. Yet a growing body of psychological research suggests that this victory over boredom may come at a cost, since the uncomfortable state of having nothing to do appears to serve surprisingly useful functions.\n\nSome psychologists argue that boredom is best understood not as an **absence** of feeling but as a signal. Like hunger or pain, it alerts a person that the current situation is failing to satisfy her needs, in this case the need for meaningful engagement, and motivates her to **seek** something more rewarding. From this perspective, boredom is not a defect but a **vital** mechanism that prevents people from remaining stuck in unrewarding activities for too long.\n\nThe most intriguing findings concern creativity. In one frequently cited experiment, participants who first completed a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers from a telephone directory, subsequently **generated** more ideas in a creative test than participants who had not been bored beforehand. Researchers have proposed that boredom encourages the mind to **wander**, and that this undirected thinking allows distant ideas to combine in novel ways. Brain imaging studies of mind-wandering have identified a network of regions that is particularly active when attention turns inward.\n\nIt would be simplistic, however, to celebrate boredom without qualification. Chronic boredom, as opposed to occasional episodes, has been linked to depression, risky behaviour and **addiction**, and people who are frequently bored are not necessarily more creative. The benefits seem to depend on what a person does with the state. If boredom is instantly relieved by scrolling, the mind never has the opportunity to wander productively; the discomfort is merely replaced by passive stimulation that **inhibits** rather than **enables** original thought.\n\nThis may explain why so many people report that their best ideas **occur** in the shower, on long walks or during repetitive physical activity, situations in which the mind is lightly **occupied** but free to **drift**. For those who value creativity, the implication is not to seek boredom for its own sake but to **tolerate** it more often: to leave small gaps in the day undistracted and to resist the **temptation** to fill every pause. In an age of informational **abundance**, the scarcest resource may turn out to be emptiness.","gloss":[{"w":"absence","he":"היעדר"},{"w":"seek","he":"לחפש, לשאוף"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"generate","he":"לייצר, להפיק"},{"w":"wander","he":"לשוטט, לנדוד"},{"w":"addiction","he":"התמכרות"},{"w":"inhibit","he":"לעכב, לבלום"},{"w":"enable","he":"לאפשר"},{"w":"occur","he":"להתרחש, לעלות (רעיון)"},{"w":"occupy","he":"להעסיק"},{"w":"drift","he":"לנדוד, להיסחף"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"},{"w":"temptation","he":"פיתוי"},{"w":"abundance","he":"שפע"}]},{"id":"h12","lvl":"h","topic":"routine","title":"Why Holidays Fly By Yet Linger in Memory","body":"Most travellers are familiar with a curious **distortion** of time. During a holiday packed with new experiences, the days seem to rush past, and the end arrives far sooner than expected. Yet on returning home, the same trip feels as though it lasted much longer than an ordinary fortnight at work. The writer Claudia Hammond has called this the holiday paradox, and it offers a revealing **insight** into how the mind measures time.\n\nThe explanation lies in the fact that people judge time in two **distinct** ways. Prospective judgements are made while an experience is still under way, and they depend largely on how much attention is paid to the passage of time itself. When a person is **absorbed** in novel **surroundings**, attention is directed outward rather than towards the clock, and time therefore appears to pass quickly. Retrospective judgements, by contrast, are made afterwards, and they **rely** not on attention but on memory: the more separate memories a period has left behind, the longer it seems in hindsight.\n\nA holiday is unusually rich in such memories. A single week in a foreign city may **comprise** dozens of new places and **encounters** with strangers, each of which is encoded as a separate event. An ordinary working week, in contrast, consists largely of repeated routines that the brain compresses into a single, undifferentiated **impression**. When the two periods are compared in memory, the holiday **occupies** far more mental space and is accordingly judged to have lasted longer.\n\nThe same mechanism may help explain the widely shared feeling that time **accelerates** with age. Childhood and adolescence are full of firsts, whereas adult life tends to become increasingly routine. As fewer new memories are formed each year, years seem to **contract** in retrospect, and people **perceive** that time is slipping away ever faster. This explanation remains a **hypothesis** rather than an established fact, but it is consistent with research showing that novelty strongly influences how durations are remembered.\n\nThe implications extend beyond travel. If the subjective length of life depends partly on the **variety** of experiences it contains, then **deliberately** introducing novelty into ordinary routines, such as a different route home or a new skill, may arguably stretch time in memory. Ironically, the uniform routines that make a week feel endless while it is being lived are the same ones that cause whole years to vanish when people look back.","gloss":[{"w":"distortion","he":"עיוות"},{"w":"insight","he":"תובנה"},{"w":"distinct","he":"נפרד, שונה"},{"w":"absorb","he":"לשקוע, להיבלע (absorbed – שקוע)"},{"w":"surroundings","he":"סביבה"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך"},{"w":"comprise","he":"לכלול, להיות מורכב מ-"},{"w":"encounter","he":"מפגש"},{"w":"impression","he":"רושם"},{"w":"occupy","he":"לתפוס (מקום)"},{"w":"accelerate","he":"להאיץ"},{"w":"contract","he":"להתכווץ"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לחוש"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"},{"w":"variety","he":"מגוון"},{"w":"deliberately","he":"בכוונה, במתכוון"}]},{"id":"h13","lvl":"h","topic":"social","title":"Frenemies and the Hidden Cost of Ambivalence","body":"Psychologists have long known that close relationships are good for health. People with strong social ties tend to live longer and report greater wellbeing. Less attention, however, has been paid to a type of relationship that is **ubiquitous** yet rarely discussed: the friend, relative or colleague who is sometimes supportive and sometimes hurtful. Researchers describe such relationships as **ambivalent**, and growing evidence suggests that they may, in some respects, be more harmful than relationships that are simply negative.\n\nMuch of this research has been conducted by the psychologists Bert Uchino and Julianne Holt-Lunstad, who asked participants to rate members of their social networks separately for how helpful and how upsetting they were. A **substantial** proportion of important relationships turned out to contain both positive and negative elements. In several studies, interactions with ambivalent partners were associated with higher blood pressure than interactions with purely supportive ones, and some research has linked ambivalent ties to markers of accelerated cellular ageing.\n\nWhy should a mixed relationship be worse than an openly **hostile** one? One explanation concerns predictability. A person who is consistently unpleasant can simply be avoided, and her criticism can be **disregarded** at little emotional cost. An ambivalent friend, by contrast, cannot be dismissed so easily, because her support is genuinely valued. Each encounter therefore carries uncertainty: will she be warm or critical today? This uncertainty may keep people in a state of **vigilance**, monitoring the interaction for signs of a slight and suppressing their own reactions in order to preserve the bond.\n\nAnother factor is that ambivalent relationships tend to be **persistent**. Purely negative ties are often abandoned, whereas mixed ones are maintained precisely because they offer something of value. As a result, people may **endure** the strain of such relationships for years, and the effects on their stress responses could **accumulate** over time. Some researchers suggest that ambivalence also **undermines** the protective benefits of support, since help offered by someone who might later criticise is received with suspicion.\n\nNone of this implies that every imperfect friendship should be **terminated**. Virtually all close relationships contain some friction, and occasional conflict can **strengthen** intimacy when it is resolved openly. The research suggests rather that it is worth recognising which relationships are a **consistent** source of **tension**, and perhaps adjusting the **extent** of involvement accordingly. Spending time with an ambivalent friend in brief, structured settings, for instance, may preserve what is valuable about the relationship while limiting its toll on both mind and body.","gloss":[{"w":"ubiquitous","he":"נפוץ, נמצא בכל מקום"},{"w":"ambivalent","he":"אמביוולנטי, דו-ערכי"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"hostile","he":"עוין"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם"},{"w":"vigilance","he":"דריכות, ערנות"},{"w":"persistent","he":"מתמשך, עיקש"},{"w":"endure","he":"לסבול, לשאת"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער, לחתור תחת"},{"w":"terminate","he":"לסיים, להפסיק"},{"w":"strengthen","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"tension","he":"מתח"},{"w":"extent","he":"מידה, היקף"}]},{"id":"h14","lvl":"h","topic":"social","title":"The Pain of Being Left Out","body":"Consider a simple online game in which a participant and two other players toss a virtual ball to one another. For the first few throws, the ball comes to the participant regularly. Then, without explanation, the other two begin throwing it only to each other, and for the remaining minutes the participant can do nothing but watch. This scenario is the basis of Cyberball, a research tool developed by the social psychologist Kipling Williams, used in hundreds of studies of one of the most painful of human experiences: being ignored and excluded.\n\nWhat makes the findings so striking is how little it takes to produce distress. The other players are in fact controlled by a computer program, and the game has no consequences whatsoever. Nevertheless, excluded participants **consistently** report lower mood, a weaker sense of belonging and diminished self-esteem. Remarkably, these effects **persist** even when participants are told that the other players are computers, and even when the excluding group is one they **despise**. Such results suggest that sensitivity to exclusion is not a **deliberate** judgement but an automatic response that operates largely beneath **conscious** control.\n\nNeuroscientific research has added a further dimension. In an influential brain-imaging study, participants who were excluded during Cyberball showed increased activity in regions that are also involved in the distressing component of physical pain. Although the precise **interpretation** of these findings remains **controversial**, they have contributed to the hypothesis that the brain borrowed ancient pain systems to signal social threats.\n\nFrom an evolutionary perspective, such sensitivity makes considerable sense. For early humans, who **relied** on small groups for food, protection and child-rearing, being expelled could be **fatal**. A mechanism that detected the earliest signs of exclusion, and **provoked** an urgent effort to restore one's position, would therefore have offered a clear survival advantage. The occasional cost of overreacting to an innocent slight was trivial compared with the cost of failing to notice **genuine** rejection.\n\nWilliams' research also reveals how people respond after being ostracised. Some attempt to regain acceptance by becoming more agreeable and **compliant**, while others, particularly those who feel their sense of control has been threatened, react with **hostility**. Prolonged exclusion, as in workplace bullying or the 'silent treatment', can lead to **alienation**, depression and a sense of worthlessness. These findings carry a clear message for schools, workplaces and online communities: ignoring someone is never a neutral act, and its effects are often more **profound** than those who inflict it realise.","gloss":[{"w":"consistently","he":"באופן עקבי"},{"w":"persist","he":"להימשך, להתמיד"},{"w":"despise","he":"לבוז, לתעב"},{"w":"deliberate","he":"מכוון, מודע"},{"w":"conscious","he":"מודע"},{"w":"interpretation","he":"פרשנות"},{"w":"controversial","he":"שנוי במחלוקת"},{"w":"rely","he":"להסתמך, להיות תלוי ב-"},{"w":"fatal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר, לגרום"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי"},{"w":"compliant","he":"צייתן, מתרצה"},{"w":"hostility","he":"עוינות"},{"w":"alienation","he":"ניכור"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"}]},{"id":"h15","lvl":"h","topic":"social","title":"In Defence of Gossip","body":"Gossip has been **condemned** by moralists for centuries. Religious traditions warn against it and the word itself conjures images of malicious whispering behind closed doors. Yet researchers who study human communication have reached a more **nuanced** conclusion. Gossip, broadly defined as conversation about people who are not present, turns out to be **ubiquitous** across cultures, and its functions appear to be far more constructive than its reputation suggests.\n\nThe scale of the practice is considerable. Studies that record everyday conversations have estimated that a large share of talk, by some estimates around two-thirds, concerns social topics, including the behaviour of absent third parties. Contrary to popular stereotype, men appear to gossip roughly as much as women, and most gossip is neither negative nor malicious; a **substantial** proportion is neutral, simply exchanging information about what others are doing.\n\nThe evolutionary psychologist Robin Dunbar has proposed that language itself may have evolved partly to make gossip possible. Among other primates, social bonds are maintained through grooming, which is time-consuming and can only be carried out with one partner at a time. As human groups grew larger, Dunbar argues, our **ancestors** needed a more efficient way of **sustaining** relationships and keeping track of who could be trusted. Conversation about others allowed information about reputations to **circulate** rapidly through a community, enabling individuals to identify reliable allies and avoid those who had **betrayed** others.\n\nExperimental research supports the idea that gossip serves a protective function. In economic games, participants who observe someone behaving selfishly frequently choose to warn others and they report feeling less frustrated after doing so. Moreover, the mere **prospect** of being gossiped about appears to **deter** selfish behaviour: when people know their actions may be reported to future partners, they tend to behave more generously. In this sense, gossip acts as an informal system of social **enforcement**, **restraining** exploitation in situations where formal rules are absent.\n\nNone of this **implies** that all gossip is harmless. It can be used to **intimidate**, to exclude or to spread **distorted** information, and its targets may suffer serious damage to their reputation. The difference, many researchers argue, lies in **intention** and accuracy: gossip that warns others about genuinely harmful behaviour serves the group, whereas gossip motivated by envy or the desire to humiliate merely serves the speaker. Rather than a vice to be eliminated, then, gossip may be better understood as a powerful social tool, one that, like most tools, can be used well or badly.","gloss":[{"w":"condemn","he":"לגנות"},{"w":"nuanced","he":"מורכב, בעל דקויות"},{"w":"ubiquitous","he":"נפוץ בכל מקום"},{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"ancestor","he":"אב קדמון"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים, לשמר"},{"w":"circulate","he":"להסתובב, להתפשט"},{"w":"betray","he":"לבגוד"},{"w":"prospect","he":"סיכוי, אפשרות צפויה"},{"w":"deter","he":"להרתיע"},{"w":"enforcement","he":"אכיפה"},{"w":"restrain","he":"לרסן"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז, להשתמע"},{"w":"intimidate","he":"להפחיד, לאיים"},{"w":"distort","he":"לעוות"},{"w":"intention","he":"כוונה"}]},{"id":"h16","lvl":"h","topic":"social","title":"The Unwritten Rules of Returning Favours","body":"Few social norms are as widespread, or as rarely stated aloud, as the expectation that a favour should be returned. Anthropologists have documented systems of gift exchange in virtually every known society, and the sociologist Alvin Gouldner famously argued that a norm of reciprocity is one of the universal foundations of social life. People keep a largely unconscious **account** of what they have given and received, and they feel uneasy when the balance shifts too far in either direction.\n\nIn his classic essay The Gift, the French anthropologist Marcel Mauss observed that, in many traditional societies, a gift was never truly free. Accepting it created an **obligation** to reciprocate, and failing to do so could bring **disgrace**. Mauss argued that such exchanges were not primarily economic transactions but ways of creating and **reinforcing** social bonds. The value of a gift lay less in the object itself than in the relationship it **sustained**.\n\nThe power of this norm has been demonstrated repeatedly. In one well-known experiment, a researcher's assistant bought a soft drink for some participants as an apparently spontaneous **gesture**; later, those participants were considerably more willing to buy raffle tickets from him than participants who had received nothing, regardless of whether they liked him. Marketers have long **exploited** the same principle, offering free samples precisely because a small gift creates a subtle pressure to **purchase**.\n\nYet the rules governing reciprocity are far more **elaborate** than a simple exchange. Returning a favour too quickly, for instance, can appear calculating, as though the **recipient** wished to cancel the debt and end the relationship. An exact repayment, such as handing a friend the precise cost of a meal, may feel cold among intimates even though it would be perfectly appropriate among **strangers**. Psychologists distinguish between exchange relationships, in which benefits are expected to be repaid promptly, and communal relationships, in which people respond to one another's needs without keeping strict score. Introducing the logic of one into the other often **provokes** discomfort.\n\nReciprocity also has a darker side. When favours **accumulate** without the possibility of repayment, the recipient may feel indebted and inferior, which is why generous offers are sometimes declined. In **hierarchical** settings, gifts can become instruments of influence or even corruption. Understanding these unspoken rules does not make social life less complicated, but it does explain why a small, **sincere** gesture often matters more than a lavish one, and why friendships rarely survive when one person feels that the scales will never be balanced.","gloss":[{"w":"account","he":"חשבון, רישום"},{"w":"obligation","he":"חובה, מחויבות"},{"w":"disgrace","he":"חרפה, בושה"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"sustain","he":"לקיים, לשמר"},{"w":"gesture","he":"מחווה"},{"w":"exploit","he":"לנצל"},{"w":"purchase","he":"לקנות, לרכוש"},{"w":"elaborate","he":"מורכב, מפורט"},{"w":"recipient","he":"מקבל"},{"w":"stranger","he":"זר"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר, לגרום"},{"w":"accumulate","he":"להצטבר"},{"w":"hierarchical","he":"היררכי"},{"w":"sincere","he":"כן, אמיתי"}]},{"id":"h17","lvl":"h","topic":"love","title":"Too Many Options: Choice and Modern Romance","body":"For most of human history, the pool of potential partners was limited to the people one could meet in person. Dating apps have **transformed** this situation almost beyond recognition. A single evening of swiping can present more potential partners than a person in a traditional village might have encountered in a lifetime. Ostensibly, this **abundance** should make it easier to find a **compatible** match. A number of psychologists, however, have suggested that it may also make commitment more difficult.\n\nTheir argument draws on research into what the psychologist Barry Schwartz called the paradox of choice. In a famous study conducted in a supermarket, shoppers who were offered a display of twenty-four varieties of jam were more likely to stop and look, but far less likely to buy, than those who were offered only six. Schwartz argued that an excess of options can lead to paralysis, higher expectations and greater regret, because every choice carries the **implicit** cost of all the alternatives that were **rejected**.\n\nApplied to romance, the theory suggests that dating apps may encourage a mindset in which partners are treated as interchangeable products to be compared and **evaluated**. If a better option always seems to be one swipe away, it becomes tempting to **disregard** a promising partner over trivial **flaws**, or to **postpone** commitment indefinitely in the hope that someone more perfect will **emerge**. Some studies have found that people presented with larger sets of potential partners tend to be more selective and, under certain conditions, less satisfied with their eventual choice.\n\nThe evidence, however, is far from **conclusive**. Later attempts to replicate the original choice-overload findings produced inconsistent results, and analyses combining many studies suggest that the effect appears only under particular conditions. Moreover, critics point out that wider choice has brought genuine benefits, particularly to members of sexual and other **minorities**, for whom finding compatible partners in a small local community was once extremely difficult.\n\nPerhaps the most useful insight from this research concerns not the number of options but the attitude with which they are approached. Schwartz distinguished between maximisers, who **relentlessly** seek the best possible option, and satisficers, who are **content** with one that is good enough. In love, as elsewhere, the capacity to commit wholeheartedly to a good choice, rather than endlessly searching for a perfect one, may matter more than the size of the menu.","gloss":[{"w":"transform","he":"לשנות מן היסוד"},{"w":"abundance","he":"שפע"},{"w":"compatible","he":"מתאים, תואם"},{"w":"implicit","he":"מובלע, משתמע"},{"w":"reject","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"evaluate","he":"להעריך"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם"},{"w":"flaw","he":"פגם"},{"w":"postpone","he":"לדחות (בזמן)"},{"w":"emerge","he":"לצוץ, להופיע"},{"w":"conclusive","he":"חד-משמעי, מכריע"},{"w":"minority","he":"מיעוט"},{"w":"relentlessly","he":"ללא הרף"},{"w":"content","he":"מרוצה, מסתפק"}]},{"id":"h18","lvl":"h","topic":"love","title":"Emerging Adulthood and the Postponed Wedding","body":"In 1960, the typical American woman married at about twenty, and her husband was only a couple of years older. Today, the median age at first marriage in the United States has risen to around twenty-eight for women and thirty for men, with similar trends across Europe and East Asia. In Israel, too, the age at which people marry has risen **substantially** over recent decades, although it remains lower than in many Western countries.\n\nThe psychologist Jeffrey Arnett has argued that this shift reflects the **emergence** of a new life stage, which he called emerging adulthood, spanning roughly the ages of eighteen to twenty-nine. According to Arnett, this period is **distinct** from both adolescence and full adulthood. It is characterised by identity exploration, instability, a strong focus on the self, a feeling of being in between, and a sense of open possibility. Rather than moving directly from school into marriage, many young people now spend a decade experimenting with jobs, places and relationships before **settling** down.\n\nSeveral forces have **contributed** to this transformation. The expansion of higher education has **prolonged** the years of study for both men and women, and economies that reward advanced qualifications have made early marriage financially risky. Women's increased participation in the labour market has reduced the economic necessity of early partnership. Cultural attitudes have changed as well: marriage is increasingly **regarded** not as the **foundation** of adult life but as its capstone, a step to be taken only after one has achieved personal and financial **stability**.\n\nArnett's concept has not escaped criticism. Some sociologists argue that emerging adulthood describes the experience of a relatively **affluent** minority rather than a universal stage, since young people without family support or higher education often face adult responsibilities much earlier. Others contend that the instability Arnett frames as exploration is better understood as a consequence of **precarious** employment and **exorbitant** housing costs, which compel young people to delay independence whether they wish to or not.\n\nWhatever its causes, delayed marriage appears to have some benefits. Several studies suggest that couples who marry in their late twenties or later face a lower risk of divorce than those who marry as teenagers, perhaps because partners are more **mature** and have a clearer sense of their own **identity**. The longer path to commitment may therefore be less a sign of reluctance than of a changed conviction: that a partner is best chosen by a self that has first had time to discover who it is.","gloss":[{"w":"substantially","he":"במידה ניכרת"},{"w":"emergence","he":"הופעה, היווצרות"},{"w":"distinct","he":"נפרד, שונה"},{"w":"settle","he":"להשתקע (settle down – להתמסד)"},{"w":"contribute","he":"לתרום"},{"w":"prolong","he":"להאריך"},{"w":"regard","he":"להתייחס, לראות כ-"},{"w":"foundation","he":"יסוד, בסיס"},{"w":"stability","he":"יציבות"},{"w":"affluent","he":"אמיד, עשיר"},{"w":"precarious","he":"רעוע, לא יציב"},{"w":"exorbitant","he":"מופקע, מוגזם"},{"w":"mature","he":"בוגר"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"}]},{"id":"h19","lvl":"h","topic":"love","title":"Jealousy: Instinct or Cultural Script?","body":"Jealousy is among the most intense and most destructive of human emotions. It has inspired great literature and it remains a leading factor in **domestic** violence. Yet scholars **dispute** a fundamental question about its nature: is jealousy an **innate** product of human evolution or is it largely shaped by the cultural norms that tell people what to feel and when?\n\nEvolutionary psychologists, most **prominently** David Buss, have argued that jealousy evolved as a mechanism for protecting valuable relationships from **rivals**. Buss further proposed that the sexes faced different reproductive threats. Because fertilisation occurs inside the woman's body, a man could never be entirely certain that a child was his own, so he would be especially sensitive to sexual infidelity. A woman, by contrast, could be certain of her maternity but risked losing a partner's resources and protection, and would therefore be more distressed by emotional infidelity. Early studies, in which participants chose which betrayal would upset them more, appeared to confirm this prediction.\n\nCritics have **scrutinised** these findings closely. The size of the sex difference varies considerably across countries, and it shrinks when participants rate each type of infidelity separately. Some researchers argue that the pattern reflects not hard-wired programming but reasoning about **ambiguous** situations: many people assume that a partner who has sex with someone will also fall in love, or vice versa, so that the choice offered in the questionnaire is somewhat artificial.\n\nAnthropological evidence further complicates the evolutionary account. The circumstances that **provoke** jealousy, and the reactions considered acceptable, vary widely between societies. In some cultures, sexual relationships outside marriage are **tolerated** under specific conditions, while in others even a brief conversation with a stranger can be **perceived** as a serious threat. Historians have also **traced** how Western attitudes have shifted, from regarding jealousy as a **legitimate** defence of honour to viewing it as a sign of insecurity and possessiveness.\n\nMost researchers now **reject** a strict opposition between the two positions. It seems plausible that humans **possess** an evolved capacity for jealousy, since the emotion appears in some form across virtually all known societies, but that culture shapes its triggers, its intensity and its expression. If jealousy is neither purely instinctive nor purely learned, then it is neither **inevitable** in its harmful forms nor easily eliminated; it can, however, be understood, discussed openly and managed, so that it alerts partners to genuine threats without **devastating** the very relationship it was meant to protect.","gloss":[{"w":"domestic","he":"ביתי, במשפחה"},{"w":"dispute","he":"להתווכח על, לחלוק על"},{"w":"innate","he":"מולד"},{"w":"prominently","he":"באופן בולט"},{"w":"rival","he":"יריב"},{"w":"scrutinize","he":"לבחון בקפידה"},{"w":"ambiguous","he":"דו-משמעי, לא ברור"},{"w":"provoke","he":"לעורר, להצית"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להתיר"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, לראות כ-"},{"w":"trace","he":"להתחקות, לעקוב"},{"w":"legitimate","he":"לגיטימי, מוצדק"},{"w":"reject","he":"לדחות"},{"w":"possess","he":"להחזיק, להיות בעל"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"devastate","he":"להרוס"}]},{"id":"h20","lvl":"h","topic":"love","title":"Why New Love Makes Us Feel Bigger","body":"People who have recently fallen in love often describe a curious sensation: not merely that they are happy, but that they have somehow grown. According to self-expansion theory, developed by the psychologists Arthur and Elaine Aron, this feeling is not a romantic illusion but a reflection of a fundamental human motive: the desire to enhance one's capacity to act in the world by **acquiring** new resources, perspectives and identities.\n\nThe Arons proposed that close relationships are one of the most powerful means of satisfying this motive. When two people form an intimate bond, each begins, in a sense, to include the other in the self. The partner's knowledge, skills, friendships and experiences become, at least partly, accessible to the other. A person who has never been interested in rock climbing or Japanese cinema may, through a new partner, come to **embrace** both.\n\nResearch has provided **empirical** support for this idea. In one study that followed students over a semester, those who fell in love showed a greater increase in the **diversity** of the words they used to describe themselves than those who did not, suggesting that their self-concept had literally expanded. They also reported higher self-esteem. Other studies have found that people's mental representations of themselves and their partners **overlap** to such an extent that they are slower to **distinguish** their own traits from those of the person they love, as though the boundary between the two had **inherently** blurred.\n\nThe theory also offers an explanation for a familiar and somewhat **bitter** phenomenon: the **gradual** decline of excitement in long-term relationships. Self-expansion is most rapid at the beginning, when everything about the partner is new. Once the couple has largely **absorbed** each other's worlds, the opportunities for growth diminish, and the relationship may come to feel stale. Not surprisingly, some people then **seek** expansion elsewhere, which may help explain the **temptation** that a new romance can represent.\n\nEncouragingly, the research also suggests a **remedy**. In experiments, couples who were asked to take part in novel and moderately challenging activities together subsequently reported greater satisfaction with their relationship than couples who performed mundane tasks. Shared novelty, it seems, can **restore** some of the expansion that characterised the early stages of love. The implication is both practical and **profound**: a lasting relationship need not depend on finding someone new, but on continuing to discover new things with the person one already has.","gloss":[{"w":"acquire","he":"לרכוש"},{"w":"embrace","he":"לאמץ, לחבק"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי"},{"w":"diversity","he":"גיוון, מגוון"},{"w":"overlap","he":"לחפוף"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין"},{"w":"inherently","he":"מטבעו, ביסודו"},{"w":"bitter","he":"מר"},{"w":"gradual","he":"הדרגתי"},{"w":"absorb","he":"לספוג, לקלוט"},{"w":"seek","he":"לחפש, לשאוף"},{"w":"temptation","he":"פיתוי"},{"w":"remedy","he":"תרופה, פתרון"},{"w":"restore","he":"להשיב, לשקם"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק"}]},{"id":"h21","lvl":"h","topic":"beauty","title":"Ten Small Canvases: Nail Art and Identity","body":"Although nail polish is often dismissed as a trivial accessory, historians of fashion increasingly **regard** the decorated fingernail as a surprisingly rich cultural document. Long before glossy images of elaborate manicures began to circulate on social media, women in Black American communities had transformed their nails into a site of creativity, **identity** and quiet defiance, cultivating styles that mainstream culture would later **imitate** while rarely acknowledging their **origin**.\n\nIn the 1980s and 1990s, long acrylic nails decorated with airbrushed patterns, rhinestones and gold charms became **prominent** in hip-hop culture. When the sprinter Florence Griffith Joyner competed at the 1988 Olympics with strikingly long, painted nails, some commentators treated them as an extravagance, whereas many Black women recognised a deliberate statement of self-presentation. Precisely because such styles were associated with working-class women of colour, they were frequently labelled excessive or 'tacky', a judgement that sociologists interpret as a form of class and racial **prejudice** **disguised** as a question of taste. Not until celebrities from other backgrounds adopted the same designs, decades later, were they rebranded as fashionable and daring.\n\nJapan offers a strikingly different trajectory. There, nail art developed into a highly **meticulous** craft during the 1990s and 2000s, with technicians constructing tiny three-dimensional flowers, pearls and cartoon characters on surfaces barely a centimetre wide. Japanese salons introduced professional **certification** and formal training, so that the work came to be perceived less as a beauty treatment than as a miniature art form, comparable in its precision to traditional crafts. Japanese gel techniques were subsequently **exported** across Asia and, eventually, to salons in Europe and the United States.\n\nBetween these two traditions lies a third story that is rarely told. In the United States, the nail industry was transformed by Vietnamese immigrants who arrived after 1975 and, lacking other opportunities, built a vast network of affordable salons. Their labour made manicures accessible to millions of ordinary customers, yet the work has often involved long hours, modest **wages** and constant exposure to chemical fumes.\n\nTaken together, these histories suggest that nail art is by no means a superficial trend. It is, arguably, a lens through which questions of race, class, migration and creativity become **tangible**. Whether a design is praised as innovation or **condemned** as vulgarity often depends less on the design itself than on whose hands are wearing it, a reminder that aesthetic judgements are rarely as **impartial** as they appear.","gloss":[{"w":"regard","he":"להתייחס אל, לראות כ־"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"imitate","he":"לחקות"},{"w":"origin","he":"מקור"},{"w":"prominent","he":"בולט"},{"w":"prejudice","he":"דעה קדומה"},{"w":"disguise","he":"להסוות, להציג במסווה"},{"w":"meticulous","he":"קפדני, מדוקדק"},{"w":"certification","he":"הסמכה"},{"w":"export","he":"לייצא"},{"w":"wage","he":"שכר"},{"w":"tangible","he":"מוחשי"},{"w":"condemn","he":"לגנות"},{"w":"impartial","he":"ניטרלי, לא משוחד"}]},{"id":"h22","lvl":"h","topic":"beauty","title":"Paid to Look Good: The Hidden Work of Beauty","body":"When sociologists first began to study service work, they focused largely on what employees did with their hands and minds. In 1983, the American sociologist Arlie Hochschild introduced the concept of 'emotional labour' to describe how flight attendants were required to manage their feelings, producing warmth and calm on **behalf** of the **airline**. Two decades later, British researchers extended this **insight** in a direction that is arguably even more personal: they argued that many employers now hire, train and evaluate workers not only for their skills but also for their bodies, voices and **appearance**.\n\nThis phenomenon, which they called 'aesthetic labour', is most **conspicuous** in fashionable retail, boutique hotels and upmarket bars, where staff are expected to embody the brand they sell. Job advertisements may ask for applicants who are 'well-spoken' or 'stylish', terms that sound harmless yet **imply** particular class, age and ethnic profiles. Employees may be instructed on make-up, hairstyles and posture, and in some cases have been dismissed for failing to look the part. One American clothing chain faced a lawsuit after being accused of favouring white, conventionally attractive applicants for its shop floors, a case that demonstrated how easily aesthetic preferences allow employers to **discriminate**.\n\nThe concept also illuminates a less visible **burden**. Outside the workplace, a great deal of unpaid effort is **devoted** to maintaining an acceptable image: shaving, styling, dieting, shopping and the constant monitoring of one's reflection. Feminist scholars point out that this work falls disproportionately on women, consumes significant amounts of time and money, and is rarely recognised as work at all, precisely because it is presented as a matter of personal choice or pleasure.\n\nNot all researchers accept so **grim** an interpretation. Some argue that beauty workers themselves, such as manicurists, hairdressers and make-up artists, derive genuine pride and creative satisfaction from their craft, and that grooming can be a source of pleasure, confidence and social connection rather than mere obligation. The **tension**, therefore, is not between beauty and freedom but between choice and **constraint**: it is often impossible to **distinguish** what individuals want for themselves from what an image-saturated economy quietly demands of them.\n\nWhat makes aesthetic labour so difficult to regulate is that its rules are seldom **explicit**. Hardly any employer will openly declare that it prefers young, slim staff, yet hiring decisions may reflect exactly that. Recognising beauty as labour does not condemn anyone who enjoys it; rather, it invites us to **scrutinize** who sets the standards, who pays the costs and who profits from the results.","gloss":[{"w":"on behalf of","he":"בשם, עבור"},{"w":"airline","he":"חברת תעופה"},{"w":"insight","he":"תובנה"},{"w":"appearance","he":"מראה חיצוני"},{"w":"conspicuous","he":"בולט לעין"},{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז, להשתמע"},{"w":"discriminate","he":"להפלות"},{"w":"burden","he":"נטל"},{"w":"devote","he":"להקדיש"},{"w":"grim","he":"קודר, עגום"},{"w":"tension","he":"מתח"},{"w":"constraint","he":"אילוץ, מגבלה"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין, להפריד"},{"w":"explicit","he":"מפורש"},{"w":"scrutinize","he":"לבחון בקפידה"}]},{"id":"h23","lvl":"h","topic":"beauty","title":"Beautiful Bottles, Thin Rules","body":"Most consumers presume that a lipstick or nail polish sold in a respectable shop must have been tested and approved by some official authority. In many countries, however, this **presumption** is only partly accurate, and in the United States it was, until recently, largely mistaken. For more than eighty years, American cosmetics were governed by a law passed in 1938, under which manufacturers did not need government approval before **launching** a product, nor were they obliged to report injuries that customers attributed to it.\n\nThe contrast with Europe is striking. The European Union has prohibited or restricted well over a thousand substances in cosmetics, whereas American regulators for decades banned only around a dozen. Critics argued that this gap left consumers dependent on the goodwill of an industry with obvious commercial **incentives** to cut costs, while defenders countered that many European bans were **precautionary** rather than based on **conclusive** evidence of harm. Both positions contain some truth: the absence of proof of danger is not proof of safety, yet not every chemical with an alarming name **poses a threat** at the doses actually used.\n\nThe issue is especially **acute** in nail salons, where technicians spend long hours breathing **vapors** from polishes, removers and acrylic liquids. Three ingredients, toluene, formaldehyde and dibutyl phthalate, became known as the 'toxic trio' after studies linked them to headaches, breathing problems and possible reproductive harm. Many brands have since removed them and advertise their polishes as 'three-free' or even 'ten-free', yet such labels are largely self-declared and not always independently verified, so that marketing may **exaggerate** the change.\n\nIn 2022, the United States finally passed a reform that requires companies to register their **facilities**, list their products, report serious adverse reactions and demonstrate that what they sell is safe, and it granted regulators the power to order recalls. Supporters described it as the most significant change in generations; sceptics noted that the details depend on rules still being written and on whether the responsible agency is given **adequate** funding to **enforce** them.\n\nThe broader lesson concerns the **ambiguous** status of beauty products themselves. Because cosmetics are classified as neither medicines nor ordinary goods, they have long occupied a regulatory grey zone, and only when **sustained** public pressure accumulated did lawmakers act. For workers exposed every day, rather than consumers who use a product occasionally, closing that gap is by no means a cosmetic matter.","gloss":[{"w":"presumption","he":"הנחה מוקדמת"},{"w":"launch","he":"להשיק"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"precautionary","he":"של זהירות, מונע"},{"w":"conclusive","he":"חד־משמעי, מכריע"},{"w":"pose a threat","he":"להוות איום"},{"w":"acute","he":"חריף"},{"w":"vapor","he":"אדים"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"facility","he":"מתקן"},{"w":"adequate","he":"מספק, הולם"},{"w":"enforce","he":"לאכוף"},{"w":"ambiguous","he":"עמום, דו־משמעי"},{"w":"sustained","he":"מתמשך"}]},{"id":"h24","lvl":"h","topic":"beauty","title":"Does Beauty Really Pay?","body":"Economists are not usually associated with discussions of good looks, yet over the past three decades a **substantial** body of **empirical** research has examined what has come to be called the 'beauty premium'. The American economist Daniel Hamermesh, who has studied the topic since the 1990s, found that workers rated as attractive tend to earn several percent more than average-looking colleagues, while those rated as plain earn somewhat less. Across an entire working life, the cumulative difference can amount to a considerable sum.\n\nExplaining the premium has **proven** more difficult than **detecting** it. One explanation is straightforward **bias**: employers, colleagues and customers may unconsciously treat attractive people as more competent, a pattern psychologists describe as the 'halo effect', in which one positive **attribute** colours the perception of all others. A second explanation is that, in occupations involving constant contact with the public, attractive staff may genuinely **generate** more sales, so that the premium reflects customer preferences rather than employer prejudice. A third, more subtle account holds that people treated well since childhood develop greater confidence, and that it is this confidence, rather than appearance itself, which employers reward.\n\nEach of these accounts carries different implications. If the premium stems from prejudice, it resembles other forms of discrimination that many societies have chosen to **prohibit**. If it stems from customer preferences, it is harder to condemn, although the same argument was once used to justify excluding women and minorities from visible roles. If it stems from confidence, the solution might lie in education rather than in law.\n\nHamermesh himself provocatively suggested that unattractive people might deserve legal protection, a proposal many readers found **ludicrous**. Critics objected that beauty is far too subjective to be measured in court, and that labelling people as legally 'ugly' would be humiliating. Defenders replied that ratings of attractiveness are surprisingly **consistent** across observers, which makes the effect more measurable than it might appear.\n\nIronically, the beauty premium may help explain the **lucrative** industry built around self-improvement. If appearance is rewarded in the labour market, spending on cosmetics, fitness and fashion can be framed not as vanity but as a rational investment. Yet the evidence suggests diminishing returns: the largest gaps lie between people judged plain and everyone else, not between the attractive and the stunning. Only when we recognise how deeply looks shape economic outcomes can we begin to decide whether this is a market we are willing to **tolerate**.","gloss":[{"w":"substantial","he":"ניכר, משמעותי"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי, מבוסס תצפית"},{"w":"prove","he":"להתברר כ־, להוכיח"},{"w":"detect","he":"לגלות, לזהות"},{"w":"bias","he":"הטיה"},{"w":"attribute","he":"תכונה"},{"w":"generate","he":"לייצר, להניב"},{"w":"prohibit","he":"לאסור"},{"w":"ludicrous","he":"מגוחך"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"lucrative","he":"רווחי"},{"w":"tolerate","he":"לסבול, להשלים עם"}]},{"id":"h25","lvl":"h","topic":"growth","title":"Why Dreaming Alone Rarely Works","body":"Few ideas have been embraced as **ardently** by the self-help industry as the belief that imagining success helps bring it about. Countless books, posters and social-media accounts urge readers to visualise their goals, on the assumption that a vivid mental image of the future will supply the motivation needed to **attain** it. Over the past three decades, however, research led by the psychologist Gabriele Oettingen has cast serious doubt on this **notion**.\n\nIn a series of studies, Oettingen found that people who fantasised most positively about a desired outcome, whether losing weight, finding a partner or recovering from surgery, often fared worse than those whose expectations were more measured. One explanation is physiological: when participants indulged in idealised fantasies, measures of their energy actually declined, as if the mind had partly experienced the reward already and saw little reason to **exert** itself. Positive fantasies, in other words, may relax us precisely when we most need to be mobilised.\n\nRather than abandoning optimism altogether, Oettingen proposed a technique called mental contrasting. Participants first imagine the outcome they wish for and then deliberately identify the main internal **obstacle** standing in their way, such as anxiety, distraction or a habit of procrastinating. Combined with concrete 'if-then' plans, a method developed by her colleague Peter Gollwitzer, this procedure became known as WOOP: Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan. A woman who wishes to train more regularly might decide, for instance, that if she feels too **weary** after work, she will put on her training clothes before leaving the office.\n\nStudies applying the method to exercise, diet, academic work and relationships have reported **modest** but **consistent** improvements, and the technique is attractive precisely because it is brief and **feasible** without professional guidance. Equally important is what mental contrasting reveals when a goal is unrealistic: by confronting obstacles honestly, people become better at recognising which wishes to pursue and which to **abandon**, sparing themselves years of wasted effort.\n\nNone of this implies that pessimism is a virtue. The critique targets not hope but the belief that hope alone is **sufficient**. Arguably, the self-help industry has thrived on a **fallacy** that confuses feeling good about the future with preparing for it. Only by holding the dream and the obstacle in mind **simultaneously**, Oettingen's work suggests, does imagination become a tool for change rather than a pleasant **substitute** for it.","gloss":[{"w":"ardently","he":"בלהט"},{"w":"attain","he":"להשיג"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"exert oneself","he":"להתאמץ"},{"w":"obstacle","he":"מכשול"},{"w":"weary","he":"עייף, תשוש"},{"w":"modest","he":"צנוע, מתון"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"},{"w":"feasible","he":"בר־ביצוע"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש, לוותר על"},{"w":"sufficient","he":"מספיק"},{"w":"fallacy","he":"כשל, טעות חשיבה"},{"w":"simultaneously","he":"בו־זמנית"},{"w":"substitute","he":"תחליף"}]},{"id":"h26","lvl":"h","topic":"growth","title":"Stronger After Suffering? The Debate over Growth","body":"The idea that suffering can make people stronger is ancient, but it acquired scientific respectability only in the 1990s, when the American psychologists Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun coined the term 'post-traumatic growth'. Interviewing people who had **endured** bereavement, serious illness or disaster, they noticed that many described not only pain but also positive changes: a deeper **appreciation** of life, closer relationships, a sense of personal strength, new priorities and, for some, a renewed spiritual outlook.\n\nThe concept was quickly embraced, partly because it offered a hopeful **counterpart** to the dominant focus on trauma as damage. Questionnaires designed to measure growth have since been administered to thousands of survivors, and a large proportion **acknowledge** at least some positive change. For clinicians, the framework provided a vocabulary for discussing recovery without reducing patients to their symptoms, and for survivors it could make an apparently senseless experience feel meaningful.\n\nYet the evidence is more **tenuous** than its popularity suggests. Most studies rely on retrospective self-report: participants are asked, after the event, how much they believe they have changed. Such reports are **prone to** distortion, since people naturally wish to find meaning in their misfortune and may **perceive** growth where little has actually occurred. When one study measured traits such as gratitude and life satisfaction both before and after participants experienced a difficult event, it found that perceived growth bore surprisingly little relation to measured change. Some researchers therefore **infer** that reports of growth may sometimes function as a coping strategy rather than an accurate description of reality.\n\nCritics also warn of a cultural danger. When growth becomes an expectation rather than a possibility, survivors who feel merely damaged may conclude that they are failing at recovery, adding **guilt** to grief. The popular slogan that what does not kill us makes us stronger can, in this sense, become a burden rather than a comfort. Moreover, growth and distress are not opposites: many people report both at once, which suggests that suffering does not simply **transform** into wisdom.\n\nNone of these objections invalidates the phenomenon entirely. Arguably, the most balanced position is that some people do change in **genuine** ways after hardship, but that such change is neither **inevitable** nor necessary for a good life. Psychologists who work with survivors increasingly **emphasize** that growth should be noticed when it emerges rather than demanded, and that its absence is by no means a personal **flaw**.","gloss":[{"w":"endure","he":"לעבור, לסבול, לשאת"},{"w":"appreciation","he":"הערכה, הוקרה"},{"w":"counterpart","he":"מקבילה, עמית"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להודות ב־, להכיר ב־"},{"w":"tenuous","he":"רופף, קלוש"},{"w":"prone to","he":"נוטה ל־"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, להבחין"},{"w":"infer","he":"להסיק"},{"w":"guilt","he":"אשמה"},{"w":"transform","he":"להפוך, לשנות צורה"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי, כן"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"emphasize","he":"להדגיש"},{"w":"flaw","he":"פגם"}]},{"id":"h27","lvl":"h","topic":"growth","title":"Passion Is Built, Not Found","body":"'Find your passion' has become one of the most widely repeated pieces of career advice of the past two decades. The phrase **implies** that each person possesses a single, pre-existing calling, hidden somewhere like buried **treasure**, and that the task of a young adult is simply to **unearth** it. Increasingly, however, psychologists and career researchers argue that this framing is not only misleading but potentially counterproductive.\n\nIn experiments published in 2018, researchers compared people who held a 'fixed' theory of interests, the belief that passions are **innate** and merely waiting to be discovered, with those who held a 'growth' theory, in which interests are developed through engagement. Participants with the fixed view were less curious about subjects outside their existing interests and, strikingly, expected a true passion to provide **constant** motivation. When an initially fascinating topic became difficult, they were more likely to lose interest, as though difficulty were proof that they had chosen the wrong path.\n\nThis finding helps explain a familiar pattern. Many people **abandon** promising activities, whether a sport, an instrument or a business, at the very moment when early **enthusiasm** fades and real skill begins to require effort. If passion is assumed to be effortless, such resistance seems like a sign to move on rather than a normal stage of development. The writer Cal Newport has argued, in a similar spirit, that enjoyment of work usually follows competence rather than preceding it: people tend to love what they are good at, and they become good through **sustained** practice.\n\nThe developmental view does not **disregard** temperament altogether. Some **inclinations** clearly appear early, and nobody can be persuaded to love everything. But it shifts attention from the question 'What is my passion?' to the more practical question 'What am I willing to invest in long enough to discover whether it could become one?' A manicurist who begins doing nails simply to earn money may, after years of refining her technique and building loyal clients, find that the craft has become central to her **identity**, a passion that was **cultivated** rather than discovered.\n\nCritics of the passion narrative also point out its social bias. Advice to follow one's dreams is easiest to follow for those with financial security, whereas many people must earn a living before they can afford exploration. Seen in this light, the **notion** of a hidden calling is less a universal truth than a luxury, and the patient, often unglamorous work of developing interest may be a more **reliable** path to meaningful work.","gloss":[{"w":"imply","he":"לרמוז, להשתמע"},{"w":"treasure","he":"אוצר"},{"w":"unearth","he":"לחשוף, לחפור ולגלות"},{"w":"innate","he":"מולד"},{"w":"constant","he":"קבוע, מתמיד"},{"w":"abandon","he":"לנטוש, לזנוח"},{"w":"enthusiasm","he":"התלהבות"},{"w":"sustained","he":"מתמשך"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם מ־"},{"w":"inclination","he":"נטייה"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"cultivate","he":"לטפח"},{"w":"notion","he":"רעיון, תפיסה"},{"w":"reliable","he":"אמין"}]},{"id":"h28","lvl":"h","topic":"growth","title":"The Business of Becoming a Better You","body":"The self-help industry, which encompasses books, podcasts, seminars, coaching and apps, is estimated to be worth billions of dollars annually, and its growth shows little sign of slowing. Its **appeal** is easy to understand: it promises that personal transformation is available to anyone willing to follow the right method. Yet critics from psychology, sociology and economics have increasingly scrutinised not only whether these methods work but also what assumptions the industry quietly promotes.\n\nThe first criticism concerns evidence. Many best-selling programmes are built on anecdote, charismatic testimony and selectively cited studies, while claims that have been **proven** in carefully controlled research are often **exaggerated** once they reach the mass market. A modest laboratory effect becomes a life-changing secret; a correlation becomes a law. Some techniques do have **empirical** support, but readers rarely have the tools to **distinguish** them from those that merely sound scientific.\n\nA second criticism is economic. One journalist who investigated the industry observed that its most reliable customers are people who have already bought similar products, a pattern suggesting that the promised change is seldom **attained**. From a commercial perspective, this is hardly a weakness: a product that genuinely solved its customers' problems would gradually **exhaust** its own market, whereas one that offers inspiration without lasting results keeps them returning. Not every author is cynical, of course, but the **incentives** of the market reward novelty and emotional intensity rather than effectiveness.\n\nThe deepest objection, however, is sociological. Self-help typically frames problems as individual **deficiencies** to be corrected through better habits, mindset or discipline. Burnout becomes a failure of time management; financial insecurity becomes a lack of the right attitude. Critics argue that this framing obscures structural causes, such as **precarious** employment, **exorbitant** housing costs or unequal care responsibilities, and places the entire burden of change on the individual.\n\nDefenders reply that people cannot wait for society to reform before improving their own lives, and that many readers **derive** real comfort and practical ideas from the genre. Both positions have merit. Arguably, the most useful approach is a **critical** one: to treat self-help as a source of **hypotheses** rather than truths, to test advice against one's own experience, and to remain wary of any method that promises effortless transformation. Only when readers become sceptical consumers can the genre fulfil its better intentions.","gloss":[{"w":"appeal","he":"משיכה, קסם"},{"w":"proven","he":"מוכח"},{"w":"exaggerate","he":"להגזים"},{"w":"empirical","he":"אמפירי, מבוסס מחקר"},{"w":"distinguish","he":"להבחין"},{"w":"attain","he":"להשיג"},{"w":"exhaust","he":"למצות, לכלות"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"deficiency","he":"ליקוי, חוסר"},{"w":"precarious","he":"רעוע, לא יציב"},{"w":"exorbitant","he":"מופקע"},{"w":"derive","he":"להפיק"},{"w":"critical","he":"ביקורתי"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"}]},{"id":"h29","lvl":"h","topic":"business","title":"Start Small, Learn Fast","body":"For most of the twentieth century, launching a new business typically meant writing a detailed plan, raising money, building a finished product and only then discovering whether anyone wanted it. In 2011, the American entrepreneur Eric Ries popularised a different philosophy in his book The Lean Startup. Drawing on earlier work by the entrepreneur and academic Steve Blank, Ries argued that start-ups operate under conditions of extreme uncertainty, and that their **principal** **objective** should be not to execute a plan but to learn, as quickly and cheaply as possible, what customers actually value.\n\nThe central tool of this approach is the minimum viable product, or MVP: the simplest version of an offering that allows a founder to test a key **hypothesis** with real users. Rather than spending years on development, a team releases something deliberately incomplete, measures how people respond and then decides whether to **persevere** or change direction, a shift Ries called a 'pivot'. Before building its file-sharing software, Dropbox famously released a short video demonstrating how the product would work; the waiting list grew by tens of thousands almost overnight, providing **tangible** evidence of demand.\n\nThe appeal of the method is obvious, particularly for small entrepreneurs with **scarce** resources. A nail technician considering a new service, for instance, might offer it to a few regular clients at an introductory price before investing in expensive equipment or training. By exposing ideas to reality early, founders can **mitigate** the risk of devoting years to something nobody needs.\n\nYet the method has its critics. Some argue that the word 'minimum' is frequently stressed at the expense of 'viable', so that companies release products so crude that they **alienate** early adopters and damage their reputation before learning anything useful. In fields such as medicine, aviation or finance, where errors can be **lethal** or illegal, experimenting on customers is plainly unacceptable. Others note that the approach favours ideas whose value can be tested quickly, whereas some **profound** innovations required years of patient development before their worth became **apparent**.\n\nRies himself has emphasised that an MVP is a learning instrument rather than an excuse for poor quality. Interpreted in this spirit, the lean method is less a **rigid** formula than an attitude: a willingness to treat cherished assumptions as questions, to **acknowledge** evidence that contradicts them and to **revise** plans accordingly. Hardly any founder enjoys discovering that an idea is flawed, but discovering it after a week is far cheaper than discovering it after five years.","gloss":[{"w":"principal","he":"עיקרי, ראשי"},{"w":"objective","he":"מטרה"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"},{"w":"persevere","he":"להתמיד"},{"w":"tangible","he":"מוחשי"},{"w":"scarce","he":"מועט, נדיר"},{"w":"mitigate","he":"למתן, להפחית"},{"w":"alienate","he":"להרחיק, לנכר"},{"w":"lethal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"profound","he":"עמוק, מהותי"},{"w":"apparent","he":"ברור, גלוי"},{"w":"rigid","he":"נוקשה"},{"w":"acknowledge","he":"להכיר ב־, להודות ב־"},{"w":"revise","he":"לתקן, לעדכן"}]},{"id":"h30","lvl":"h","topic":"business","title":"Flexible for Whom? The Gig Economy","body":"When ride-hailing and delivery apps spread across the world in the 2010s, they were celebrated as a liberation from the rigid office. Anyone with a smartphone could earn money whenever they chose, without a boss or a fixed schedule. The so-called gig economy promised flexibility, entrepreneurial independence and a convenient supplement to ordinary **wages**. A decade later, however, a growing body of research suggests that for many participants the reality is considerably more **precarious**.\n\nThe core issue is classification. Most platforms treat their workers not as employees but as independent contractors, which means they are generally not entitled to a minimum wage, paid holidays, sick leave or pension contributions. Platforms argue that this status reflects the freedom workers genuinely enjoy; critics counter that the freedom is largely **superficial**, since prices, routes and performance standards are **determined** by the company. Workers can be **deprived** of their income almost instantly if their rating falls or an algorithm flags their account, often without a clear explanation or a meaningful **appeal**.\n\nThis form of algorithmic management is arguably the most novel feature of gig work. Rather than being **supervised** by a human manager, workers are **monitored** continuously by software that allocates tasks, sets prices and assesses performance. Because the rules are rarely made **explicit**, many drivers describe a constant sense of uncertainty, adjusting their behaviour to satisfy a system they cannot fully understand. The result is a peculiar combination of autonomy and control: no one tells them when to work, yet **incentives** and **penalties** shape almost every decision.\n\nCourts and legislators have responded inconsistently. In 2021, the United Kingdom's Supreme Court ruled that Uber drivers should be classified as workers with basic rights, whereas voters in California had approved a measure in 2020 that largely exempted app-based drivers from employee status. The European Union has since adopted rules intended to make it easier for platform workers to establish that they are, in **essence**, employees.\n\nNone of this means that gig work is uniformly harmful. Students, carers and people seeking occasional income may genuinely value the ability to log on and off at will, and for migrants excluded from other jobs it can provide a **vital** foothold. The difficulty arises when work designed as a supplement becomes a **primary** source of income, as it increasingly has for millions of people. In such cases, flexibility can quietly turn into insecurity, and the costs of illness, accidents and old age are shifted from companies onto the individuals least able to bear them.","gloss":[{"w":"wage","he":"שכר"},{"w":"precarious","he":"רעוע, לא יציב"},{"w":"superficial","he":"שטחי"},{"w":"determine","he":"לקבוע"},{"w":"deprive","he":"לשלול, למנוע מ־"},{"w":"appeal","he":"ערעור"},{"w":"supervise","he":"לפקח"},{"w":"monitor","he":"לנטר, לעקוב"},{"w":"explicit","he":"מפורש"},{"w":"incentive","he":"תמריץ"},{"w":"penalty","he":"עונש, קנס"},{"w":"in essence","he":"במהותו, בעיקרו"},{"w":"vital","he":"חיוני"},{"w":"primary","he":"עיקרי"}]},{"id":"h31","lvl":"h","topic":"business","title":"Why Everything Costs 9.99","body":"Walk through almost any supermarket and the same curious pattern **emerges**: prices end in 99, 95 or 9 far more often than chance would predict. Retailers call these 'charm prices', and their **prevalence** is no accident. They **exploit** a quirk in how people process numbers. Because we read from left to right, the first digit makes a disproportionate impression, so that a price of 4.99 is **perceived** as closer to four than to five, even though the actual difference is a single cent.\n\nThe effect is surprisingly robust. In one widely cited field experiment, a mail-order clothing company printed different versions of the same catalogue, and a dress priced at 39 dollars sold more copies than the identical dress priced at 34. Charm prices, it seems, do not merely make products appear cheaper; they also signal a **bargain**, since customers have learned to associate the ending with sales and discounts. Precisely for this reason, luxury brands **deliberately** avoid them, preferring round figures that convey quality and confidence rather than economy.\n\nA second technique, anchoring, relies on the tendency of the first number we **encounter** to shape **subsequent** judgements. In a classic experiment, psychologists asked participants to estimate a quantity after spinning a wheel that was rigged to stop at either a high or a low number; although the wheel was obviously **arbitrary**, those who saw the higher number gave markedly higher estimates. Retailers apply the same principle when they display an inflated 'original' price beside a discounted one, or when they place an extravagant item on a menu so that the second most expensive dish seems reasonable by comparison.\n\nAnchors can even be created by products nobody is expected to buy. A kitchenware retailer reportedly found that sales of its bread maker rose sharply after it introduced a larger, considerably more expensive model beside it; the new machine sold poorly, but it made the original appear to be a sensible purchase. Such 'decoy' options are now **ubiquitous** in subscription plans, where a deliberately unattractive middle tier steers customers towards the premium package.\n\nFor consumers, awareness of these tactics offers some protection, although research suggests that knowing about a bias does not fully neutralise it. For small businesses, the lesson is more ambivalent. A beauty salon may legitimately use pricing to communicate value, but tactics that **mislead** customers can **undermine** the trust on which repeat business depends. Arguably, the most **durable** pricing strategy is one in which the psychological framing **complements**, rather than disguises, genuine value.","gloss":[{"w":"emerge","he":"להופיע, לצוץ"},{"w":"prevalence","he":"שכיחות"},{"w":"exploit","he":"לנצל"},{"w":"perceive","he":"לתפוס, להבין כ־"},{"w":"bargain","he":"מציאה"},{"w":"deliberately","he":"במכוון"},{"w":"encounter","he":"להיתקל ב־"},{"w":"subsequent","he":"שלאחר מכן, עוקב"},{"w":"arbitrary","he":"שרירותי"},{"w":"ubiquitous","he":"נפוץ בכל מקום"},{"w":"mislead","he":"להטעות"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער, לחתור תחת"},{"w":"durable","he":"עמיד, מחזיק לאורך זמן"},{"w":"complement","he":"להשלים"}]},{"id":"h32","lvl":"h","topic":"business","title":"The Founders We Never Hear About","body":"During the Second World War, military analysts examining bombers that had returned from missions noticed that bullet holes were concentrated on the wings and body of the aircraft, and proposed **reinforcing** those areas. The statistician Abraham Wald reportedly pointed out the flaw in their reasoning: the planes they were studying were the ones that had survived. The undamaged areas were precisely where hits proved **fatal**, since aircraft struck there never returned to be counted. This error, now known as survivorship bias, **distorts** far more than military engineering; it shapes, perhaps above all, the way we tell stories about business.\n\nPopular accounts of entrepreneurship are dominated by a handful of **renowned** founders who dropped out of university, worked through the night and transformed entire industries. Their biographies are read as instruction manuals, as though imitating their habits would **yield** similar results. Yet for every celebrated success there are countless founders who followed the same path and failed, and whose stories are rarely published. Because the failures are invisible, the risks appear smaller and the **formula** more reliable than they actually are. One large study of American start-ups even found that the founders of the fastest-growing companies were, on average, in their mid-forties, a finding that sharply **contradicts** the image of the brilliant young dropout.\n\nSurvivorship bias also distorts how we understand the personal cost of building a company. The narrative of relentless effort glorifies sleep deprivation and total devotion as **prerequisites** for success. Surveys of entrepreneurs, however, suggest that many experience high levels of stress and anxiety, and burnout, a state of emotional **exhaustion**, cynicism and declining effectiveness, is widely reported among founders. Because those who collapse usually withdraw quietly, their experiences are **omitted** from the inspirational record, which then appears to prove that endless hustle works.\n\nThe pressures are **compounded** by the founder's peculiar position. Unlike employees, founders often feel **accountable** to investors, staff and family simultaneously, and may **conceal** their struggles for fear that admitting doubt will undermine confidence in the business. Their identity frequently becomes so entangled with the company that its difficulties feel like personal failures.\n\nRecognising survivorship bias does not mean discouraging ambition. It means interpreting success stories with appropriate scepticism, asking how many people attempted the same strategy and what became of them. It also means treating rest, boundaries and support as components of a **sustainable** business rather than signs of weakness. Arguably, the most useful lesson from the bombers is that the evidence we never see is often the most instructive.","gloss":[{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"fatal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"distort","he":"לעוות"},{"w":"renowned","he":"ידוע, מפורסם"},{"w":"yield","he":"להניב"},{"w":"formula","he":"נוסחה"},{"w":"contradict","he":"לסתור"},{"w":"prerequisite","he":"תנאי מוקדם"},{"w":"exhaustion","he":"תשישות"},{"w":"omit","he":"להשמיט"},{"w":"compound","he":"להחמיר, להגביר"},{"w":"accountable","he":"אחראי, חייב דין וחשבון"},{"w":"conceal","he":"להסתיר"},{"w":"sustainable","he":"בר־קיימא, שאפשר לשמר"}]},{"id":"h33","lvl":"h","topic":"mind","title":"When Famous Findings Fail to Return","body":"Science is supposed to correct itself: a finding is trusted not because a respected researcher announced it, but because others can **duplicate** it under similar conditions. In the early 2010s, psychologists began to test this principle systematically, and the results were unsettling. In 2015, a large collaboration of researchers attempted to repeat one hundred studies published in leading psychology journals and **obtained** statistically significant results in only around a third of them, although nearly all the originals had done so.\n\nSeveral celebrated effects proved especially **fragile**. The theory of 'ego depletion', which held that self-control draws on a limited resource that can be exhausted like a muscle, had been supported by hundreds of studies, yet a coordinated replication across many laboratories found an effect close to zero. Similarly, claims that adopting expansive 'power poses' for two minutes **alters** hormone levels did not survive repeated testing, and one of the original authors publicly stated that she no longer believed the effect was real. Such reversals were particularly painful because these findings had already been **embraced** by business schools, coaches and the media.\n\nThe causes are less a matter of **fraud** than of ordinary incentives. Journals have traditionally preferred novel, positive results, so that studies finding nothing were frequently left unpublished. Researchers, under pressure to publish, enjoyed considerable flexibility in how they collected and analysed data: which participants to exclude, which measures to report, when to stop testing. Each choice may appear innocent, but taken together they can make random noise look like a genuine discovery. Small samples **exacerbated** the problem, since dramatic results in small studies are often **accidental**.\n\nThe response has been, arguably, one of the most significant reforms in the history of the **discipline**. Many researchers now publicly register their predictions and analysis plans before collecting data, which makes it harder to adjust methods after seeing the results. Some journals agree to publish studies on the basis of their design rather than their outcome, removing the bias against null findings. Data are increasingly shared openly so that others can **scrutinize** them.\n\nIt would be a mistake to conclude that psychology is worthless. Many findings, from basic principles of memory to the effects of practice, have proven **robust**, and other fields, including medicine and economics, have faced similar problems. Nevertheless, the crisis offers a valuable lesson for ordinary readers: a single surprising study, however **prestigious** the journal or **eminent** the author, should be treated as a promising **hypothesis** rather than an established fact.","gloss":[{"w":"duplicate","he":"לשכפל, לחזור על"},{"w":"obtain","he":"להשיג, לקבל"},{"w":"fragile","he":"שביר"},{"w":"alter","he":"לשנות"},{"w":"embrace","he":"לאמץ"},{"w":"fraud","he":"הונאה, רמאות"},{"w":"exacerbate","he":"להחמיר"},{"w":"accidental","he":"מקרי"},{"w":"discipline","he":"תחום דעת"},{"w":"scrutinize","he":"לבחון בקפידה"},{"w":"robust","he":"יציב, חסין"},{"w":"prestigious","he":"יוקרתי"},{"w":"eminent","he":"דגול, נודע"},{"w":"hypothesis","he":"השערה"}]},{"id":"h34","lvl":"h","topic":"mind","title":"Rewiring the Brain: Promise and Overstatement","body":"For much of the twentieth century, neuroscientists believed that the adult brain was essentially fixed: its circuitry was established in childhood, after which it could only **deteriorate**. Over the past few decades, this view has been overturned. Research has shown that the brain remains capable of reorganising itself throughout life, strengthening connections that are used frequently and weakening those that are **neglected**. This capacity, known as neuroplasticity, has become one of the most celebrated ideas in popular science.\n\nSome of the evidence is genuinely striking. Brain-imaging studies of London taxi drivers, who must memorise thousands of streets, found that a region associated with spatial memory was larger in experienced drivers than in comparison groups, and that its size correlated with years spent on the job. Musicians show **distinct** patterns in areas controlling the fingers, and patients recovering from strokes can sometimes regain lost functions through intensive therapy that forces the brain to recruit undamaged regions. Such findings demonstrate that experience leaves a physical **imprint** on neural tissue.\n\nThe popular version of the story, however, has run far ahead of the science. Neuroplasticity is now invoked to sell everything from brain-training games to motivational courses promising that anyone can 'rewire' themselves in weeks. In 2014, dozens of scientists signed a public statement objecting that the claims of the brain-game industry were not supported by **adequate** evidence, and two years later one prominent company agreed to pay a settlement to American regulators, who had **accused** it of **deceptive** advertising. Training on a particular game reliably improves performance on that game, but the benefits rarely transfer to everyday memory or intelligence.\n\nPart of the problem is that plasticity is often presented as **inherently** positive, when it is in fact neutral. The same mechanisms that allow a pianist to refine her technique also **reinforce** **compulsive** habits, chronic pain and anxious patterns of thought. Moreover, change in the adult brain is typically slow, effortful and specific; it requires sustained, targeted practice rather than a few minutes of puzzles each morning. Even the question of whether adults generate significant numbers of new neurons remains contested among researchers.\n\nNone of this diminishes the importance of the discovery. Recognising that the brain can change has transformed rehabilitation and **undermined** fatalistic assumptions about ageing and learning. Yet the most accurate message is less glamorous than the marketing: the brain is shaped by what we repeatedly do, which means that meaningful change depends not on a clever product but on **consistent**, often tedious effort over months and years.","gloss":[{"w":"deteriorate","he":"להידרדר"},{"w":"neglect","he":"להזניח"},{"w":"distinct","he":"מובחן, ייחודי"},{"w":"imprint","he":"חותם, טביעה"},{"w":"adequate","he":"מספק, הולם"},{"w":"accuse","he":"להאשים"},{"w":"deceptive","he":"מטעה"},{"w":"inherently","he":"מטבעו, במהותו"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"compulsive","he":"כפייתי"},{"w":"undermine","he":"לערער"},{"w":"consistent","he":"עקבי"}]},{"id":"h35","lvl":"h","topic":"mind","title":"The Shortcuts That Lead Us Astray","body":"Humans like to think of themselves as rational decision-makers who weigh evidence carefully before acting. Since the 1970s, however, research pioneered by the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky has revealed that ordinary judgement relies heavily on mental shortcuts, or heuristics. These shortcuts **enable** us to make countless rapid decisions without exhausting ourselves, but they also produce systematic errors, known as cognitive biases, which are predictable enough to be studied and, increasingly, **exploited**.\n\nConsider the availability heuristic, the tendency to judge how likely an event is by how easily examples come to mind. Dramatic, heavily reported events such as plane crashes or shark attacks feel more common than they are, whereas quieter risks, like heart disease or car accidents, are underestimated. After the attacks of September 2001, many Americans avoided flying and drove instead; some researchers have estimated that the resulting increase in road traffic led to hundreds of additional deaths, a **grim** illustration of how a distorted **perception** of risk can be more **lethal** than the risk itself.\n\nOther biases shape our personal lives. Confirmation bias leads people to seek out information that supports what they already believe and to **disregard** what contradicts it, a pattern visible in political arguments, but also in how we judge friends and partners. The sunk-cost **fallacy** compels people to persist with failing projects, unsatisfying relationships or tedious courses simply because they have already invested time or money, even though past costs cannot be **retrieved**. And present bias leads many to favour small immediate rewards over larger future ones, which helps explain why **intentions** to save money or exercise regularly are so frequently abandoned.\n\nNot all psychologists regard these shortcuts as flaws. The German researcher Gerd Gigerenzer has argued that, in uncertain real-world environments, simple heuristics often perform as well as complex calculations, and sometimes better, precisely because they ignore irrelevant detail. On this view, biases are less a sign of irrationality than the **inevitable** cost of a mind **adapted** to make fast decisions with limited information.\n\nWhichever interpretation one prefers, awareness alone seems to offer limited protection, since people readily recognise biases in others while believing themselves **immune**. More effective strategies involve changing the environment rather than the mind: setting up automatic savings, consulting someone with no stake in the decision, or writing down in advance the **criteria** by which an option will be judged. Only by building such safeguards, it seems, can we **compensate** for the shortcuts our brains will continue to take.","gloss":[{"w":"enable","he":"לאפשר"},{"w":"exploit","he":"לנצל"},{"w":"grim","he":"קודר, עגום"},{"w":"perception","he":"תפיסה"},{"w":"lethal","he":"קטלני"},{"w":"disregard","he":"להתעלם מ־"},{"w":"fallacy","he":"כשל, טעות חשיבה"},{"w":"retrieve","he":"להחזיר, לשחזר"},{"w":"intention","he":"כוונה"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להתאים, להסתגל"},{"w":"immune","he":"חסין"},{"w":"criteria","he":"קריטריונים, אמות מידה"},{"w":"compensate","he":"לפצות, לאזן"}]},{"id":"h36","lvl":"h","topic":"mind","title":"Running in Place: The Hedonic Treadmill","body":"Why does the excitement of a new phone, a pay rise or even a long-awaited holiday fade so quickly? In 1971, the psychologists Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell offered an influential answer. Human beings, they proposed, are **equipped** with a remarkable capacity to **adapt**: after good or bad events, we tend to return to a relatively stable level of happiness. Like someone running on a treadmill, we keep pursuing improvements that leave us, emotionally, in roughly the same place.\n\nA frequently cited study published a few years later appeared to **reinforce** this idea. Researchers compared people who had won large lottery prizes with a control group and with people who had been paralysed in accidents. The lottery winners were not significantly happier than the controls and reported taking less pleasure in everyday activities, while the accident victims, though less happy, were not as **devastated** as one might **anticipate**. The study was small, but its message proved **enduring**: circumstances, it seemed, matter far less than we imagine.\n\nSubsequent research has **refined**, and partly challenged, this conclusion. Long-term studies following thousands of people over many years have found that adaptation is often incomplete. Unemployment, serious disability and the loss of a spouse can lower life satisfaction for years, sometimes permanently, whereas a good marriage may produce lasting gains for some individuals. In other words, people have something like a set point, but it is not immovable, and individuals **vary** considerably in how quickly and fully they recover.\n\nThe relationship between income and happiness illustrates the debate. An influential analysis suggested that, beyond a moderate income, more money **ceased** to improve day-to-day emotional well-being, although it continued to raise overall life evaluations. Later work using different methods found no such plateau for most people, and a joint study by researchers on both sides concluded that additional income does tend to bring more happiness, except among those who are deeply unhappy for other reasons. The disagreement remains a **subtle** one, but neither side supports the idea that **wealth** is irrelevant.\n\nIf adaptation is real but not absolute, what follows? Researchers suggest that experiences, relationships and activities that offer **variety** are less easily taken for granted than possessions, which quickly become part of the background. Deliberate practices of **appreciation** and savouring may slow adaptation by drawing attention back to what we already **possess**. The treadmill, it seems, cannot be switched off entirely, but we may have more control over its speed than early researchers supposed.","gloss":[{"w":"equip","he":"לצייד"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להסתגל"},{"w":"reinforce","he":"לחזק"},{"w":"devastate","he":"להרוס, להכות קשות"},{"w":"anticipate","he":"לצפות מראש"},{"w":"enduring","he":"מתמשך, עמיד"},{"w":"refine","he":"לחדד, לשכלל"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות, להיות שונה"},{"w":"cease","he":"לחדול, להפסיק"},{"w":"subtle","he":"עדין, דק"},{"w":"wealth","he":"עושר"},{"w":"variety","he":"מגוון"},{"w":"appreciation","he":"הערכה, הוקרה"},{"w":"possess","he":"להחזיק, להיות בעל"}]},{"id":"h37","lvl":"h","topic":"world","title":"When Helping Hurts: Orphanage Tourism","body":"Every year, large numbers of young travellers combine backpacking with short periods of volunteering abroad, teaching English, painting schools or caring for children in orphanages. The motivation is usually **sincere**: having witnessed **poverty** at close range, many feel an urgent need to contribute something. Yet over the past two decades, child-protection organisations have argued that one popular form of 'voluntourism', short visits to orphanages in countries such as Cambodia and Nepal, can cause serious harm to the very children it is intended to help.\n\nThe first problem is that many so-called orphans are not orphans at all. International agencies estimate that a large majority of children living in residential institutions worldwide have at least one living parent. In some regions, families are **persuaded** to give up their children by recruiters who promise education and a better future; the children are then housed in institutions that **derive** income from donations and paying volunteers. In Cambodia, the number of such institutions reportedly grew during a period when the number of orphans was actually falling, a pattern suggesting that supply was responding to tourist demand. Some governments now formally recognise 'orphanage trafficking' as a form of **exploitation**.\n\nThe second problem concerns children's emotional development. Psychologists stress that young children need stable, long-term relationships with caregivers in order to develop secure attachment. A steady stream of **affectionate** strangers, each of whom stays for a week before departing, may teach children to form shallow bonds quickly and to expect **abandonment**, a pattern that can have lasting consequences. Moreover, volunteers are rarely subjected to the background checks that would be **compulsory** for anyone working with children at home, leaving institutions **vulnerable** to abuse.\n\nCritics also point to a subtler imbalance. Voluntourism can turn local poverty into a backdrop for personal growth, encouraging visitors to see themselves as **benevolent** rescuers while **overlooking** the skills and agency of local communities. The photographs shared afterwards may do more for the volunteer's **image** than for the children in them.\n\nNone of this suggests that travellers should be **indifferent** to the inequalities they encounter. Rather, experts recommend redirecting goodwill: supporting organisations that help families stay together, choosing volunteer roles that match one's genuine qualifications, or simply spending money in locally owned businesses. Only when compassion is guided by evidence rather than emotion does it reliably benefit those it seeks to help. Good intentions, the critics argue, are a starting point, not a **guarantee**.","gloss":[{"w":"sincere","he":"כן, אמיתי"},{"w":"poverty","he":"עוני"},{"w":"persuade","he":"לשכנע"},{"w":"derive","he":"להפיק"},{"w":"exploitation","he":"ניצול"},{"w":"affectionate","he":"מלא חיבה"},{"w":"abandonment","he":"נטישה"},{"w":"compulsory","he":"חובה"},{"w":"vulnerable","he":"פגיע"},{"w":"benevolent","he":"טוב לב, מיטיב"},{"w":"overlook","he":"להתעלם מ־, לא לשים לב ל־"},{"w":"image","he":"תדמית"},{"w":"indifferent","he":"אדיש"},{"w":"guarantee","he":"ערובה"}]},{"id":"h38","lvl":"h","topic":"world","title":"Paying with Our Attention","body":"In 1971, the economist and psychologist Herbert Simon observed that 'a wealth of information creates a poverty of attention'. His insight has proven remarkably prophetic. In an environment where information is **abundant** and cheap, the **scarce** resource is the human capacity to notice and concentrate, and a vast industry has emerged to capture it. Because most social-media platforms, video services and news sites are free to use, their **revenue** depends largely on advertising, which in turn depends on how much time and attention users can be persuaded to give.\n\nThis business model creates powerful incentives to design products that are difficult to put down. Features such as infinite scrolling, autoplay and unpredictable notifications **exploit** well-documented features of human psychology. Rewards that arrive at irregular intervals are known from behavioural research to produce especially persistent behaviour, which is one reason checking a phone can become almost **compulsive**. The designer credited with inventing infinite scroll has publicly expressed regret about its effects, and several former technology employees have become prominent **critics** of the industry they helped build.\n\nThe consequences are debated. Some researchers argue that heavy use of social media has contributed to rising anxiety and depression among **adolescents**, particularly girls, and that constant interruptions **erode** the capacity for sustained concentration. Others counter that the evidence is largely correlational, that the effects measured in many studies are small, and that panics about new media, from novels to television, have a long history of exaggeration. The truth, arguably, lies between these positions: the effects appear to **vary** greatly depending on the person, the platform and how it is used.\n\nWhat is less disputed is that attention has become a **commodity**, bought and sold like any other. Critics argue that this arrangement places individuals at a profound disadvantage: a single user, equipped only with willpower, faces teams of engineers whose explicit **objective** is to maximise engagement. Framing excessive use as a personal failure of discipline therefore seems, to many observers, as simplistic as blaming individuals alone for rising obesity while ignoring the food industry.\n\nResponses range from personal strategies, such as disabling notifications or keeping phones out of bedrooms, to proposals for regulation that would **restrict** manipulative design, particularly for children. Some designers **advocate** an alternative ethic, in which technology is judged not by how much time it **consumes** but by whether people feel that time was well spent. Whether such ideals can **prevail** against a business model built on attention remains an open question.","gloss":[{"w":"abundant","he":"שופע, בשפע"},{"w":"scarce","he":"מועט, נדיר"},{"w":"revenue","he":"הכנסה"},{"w":"exploit","he":"לנצל"},{"w":"compulsive","he":"כפייתי"},{"w":"critic","he":"מבקר"},{"w":"adolescent","he":"מתבגר"},{"w":"erode","he":"לשחוק, לכרסם"},{"w":"vary","he":"להשתנות"},{"w":"commodity","he":"סחורה, מצרך"},{"w":"objective","he":"מטרה"},{"w":"restrict","he":"להגביל"},{"w":"advocate","he":"לצדד ב־, לקדם"},{"w":"consume","he":"לצרוך, לכלות"},{"w":"prevail","he":"לגבור, לנצח"}]},{"id":"h39","lvl":"h","topic":"world","title":"Silent Tongues: Why Languages Disappear","body":"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that around forty percent are endangered, many of them with only a few hundred speakers, most of whom are elderly. Meanwhile, more than half of humanity speaks one of a couple of dozen dominant languages. Some researchers warn that, unless current trends change, a large proportion of the world's linguistic **diversity** could vanish before the end of this century, a rate of loss that has prompted comparisons with the **extinction** of animal species.\n\nLanguages rarely die because their speakers are **conquered** overnight, although colonial policies that punished children for speaking their mother tongue at school certainly hastened the process in many regions. More often, death occurs through **gradual** 'language shift'. Parents who see that education, employment and social mobility depend on a dominant language, whether English, Spanish, Mandarin or a national standard, reasonably decide to raise their children in it. Each individual decision is rational, yet collectively they can break a chain of **transmission** that has lasted for millennia. Globalisation has **accelerated** this shift by expanding the reach of global media, migration to cities and integrated markets.\n\nWhat is lost when a language disappears? Linguists point out that languages embody distinct ways of classifying the world, from systems of kinship to detailed knowledge of local plants, animals and landscapes, much of which has never been written down. Oral traditions, songs and humour are lost along with the grammar. For **descendants** of a community, the loss can also be deeply personal, severing a link with their **ancestors** and weakening a sense of **identity**.\n\nNot everyone shares this alarm. Some economists argue that a world with fewer languages facilitates trade and communication, and that it is paternalistic to expect poor communities to **preserve** their languages for the benefit of outside scholars. **Proponents** of revitalisation respond that the choice is rarely free when one language carries all the economic rewards, and that bilingualism, rather than replacement, is entirely **feasible**.\n\nThere is reason for cautious optimism. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in prayer and scholarship, was revived as an everyday spoken language, a case arguably **unprecedented** in modern history. In New Zealand, Maori 'language nests' have immersed young children in the language since the 1980s, and similar programmes have helped Hawaiian and Welsh to regain ground. Such efforts demonstrate that decline is not **inevitable**, although they also show that revival requires sustained political will, funding and, above all, families who choose to speak the language at home.","gloss":[{"w":"diversity","he":"גיוון, מגוון"},{"w":"extinction","he":"הכחדה"},{"w":"conquer","he":"לכבוש"},{"w":"gradual","he":"הדרגתי"},{"w":"transmission","he":"העברה, מסירה"},{"w":"accelerate","he":"להאיץ"},{"w":"descendant","he":"צאצא"},{"w":"ancestor","he":"אב קדמון"},{"w":"identity","he":"זהות"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"},{"w":"proponent","he":"תומך, מצדד"},{"w":"feasible","he":"בר־ביצוע, אפשרי"},{"w":"unprecedented","he":"חסר תקדים"},{"w":"inevitable","he":"בלתי נמנע"}]},{"id":"h40","lvl":"h","topic":"world","title":"From Rishikesh to the Studio: Selling Wellness","body":"When the Beatles travelled to an ashram in Rishikesh in 1968, few Western observers could have predicted that yoga would one day support a global industry of studios, clothing lines, retreats and apps. Today, the broader wellness economy, encompassing fitness, nutrition, meditation, spa treatments and alternative medicine, is estimated to be worth trillions of dollars. For a practice that originated in ancient Indian traditions of renunciation and spiritual discipline, the transformation into a **lucrative** consumer **commodity** raises uncomfortable questions.\n\nHistorians have shown that the yoga practised in most Western studios is itself a relatively modern **hybrid**. Classical texts devote little attention to physical postures, emphasising instead meditation, ethical conduct and breath control. The flowing sequences now familiar from gyms developed largely in the twentieth century, when Indian teachers incorporated elements of gymnastics and physical culture, partly in **response** to colonial stereotypes of Indian bodies as weak. By the time yoga reached Western audiences on a large scale, it had already been **adapted** for modern, health-conscious practitioners.\n\nCommercialisation accelerated this process. In the West, yoga was increasingly marketed as exercise, stress relief and a lifestyle, often illustrated by slim, **affluent** women in expensive leggings. Critics argue that this **image** excludes people who do not fit it and strips the practice of its philosophical **essence**, reducing a path toward liberation from desire to yet another arena of consumption and self-improvement. There is an obvious irony in a tradition that warns against attachment to material possessions being used to sell them.\n\nSome Hindu organisations have campaigned to remind practitioners of yoga's roots, objecting that its religious **origins** are frequently **concealed** or ignored, while other commentators frame the issue as cultural appropriation. Yet the picture is not simply one of Western **exploitation**. India itself has actively promoted yoga abroad as a source of national prestige, and the United Nations declared an International Day of Yoga in 2014 at India's **initiative**. Many Indian teachers have built successful international careers, and countless practitioners report **genuine** benefits for physical and mental health.\n\nThe tension, therefore, is not between authentic and fake yoga but between different ideas of what yoga is for. Travellers returning from India often notice the gap between the quiet ashram and the branded studio at home. Arguably, the question worth asking is not whether a practice may **evolve**, since every living tradition does, but whether its commercial version still **preserves** something of the discipline, humility and curiosity that made it valuable in the first place.","gloss":[{"w":"lucrative","he":"רווחי"},{"w":"commodity","he":"סחורה, מצרך"},{"w":"hybrid","he":"הכלאה, שילוב"},{"w":"response","he":"תגובה"},{"w":"adapt","he":"להתאים"},{"w":"affluent","he":"אמיד, עשיר"},{"w":"image","he":"דימוי, תדמית"},{"w":"essence","he":"מהות"},{"w":"origin","he":"מקור"},{"w":"conceal","he":"להסתיר"},{"w":"exploitation","he":"ניצול"},{"w":"initiative","he":"יוזמה"},{"w":"genuine","he":"אמיתי"},{"w":"evolve","he":"להתפתח"},{"w":"preserve","he":"לשמר"}]}];
